- Integration and complexity risk appetite: {{APETITO_DE_RIESGO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any essential data is missing, ask in a single batch before executing. Work with relative magnitudes and qualitative criteria: never cite valuation figures, multiples, or amounts; mark all estimates as [SUPUESTO].

Deliver a document in six sections:
1. Inorganic growth thesis: ambition vs. organic capacity gap and value vectors (market, capacity, technology, talent, scale) that justify buying or allying; rule out those that can be covered organically.
2. Build-borrow-buy: per vector, decide to build, ally, or acquire, with an explicit criterion (urgency, distance from core, availability of targets, control risk, reversibility).
3. Target and partner selection criteria: strategic fit, cultural fit, credible synergies, integration feasibility, and risk; separate eliminatory thresholds from weighted criteria.
4. Illustrative pipeline: types of target and partner prioritized in an attractiveness vs. feasibility matrix with ordering logic.
5. Roadmap by horizons: plays with dependencies, go/no-go milestones, preconditions, and signals to abort.
6. Value creation discipline: synergy thesis by play, integration plan (PMI) or alliance governance, and key risks with mitigations and owner.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-estrategia_land_and_expand:
Act as a senior Account-Based Growth strategist with experience in B2B consultative sales and account planning discipline (buying committee and multithreading). Design a land-and-expand strategy to grow account {{CUENTA}} without depending on a single large contract or a high-risk negotiation: enter with a bounded, high-demonstrable-value use case (land) starting from {{SOLUCION_INICIAL}}, generate evidence of impact, and use that trust to expand across three chained vectors (cross-sell, up-sell, and new use cases) from {{PORTAFOLIO_EXPANSION}}, where each wave is supported by the proof from the previous one to grow penetration and NRR in a compounding way.

Parameters:
- CUENTA: {{CUENTA}}
- SOLUCION_INICIAL: {{SOLUCION_INICIAL}}
- PORTAFOLIO_EXPANSION: {{PORTAFOLIO_EXPANSION}}
- ESTADO_RELACION: {{ESTADO_RELACION}} (cold prospect, under evaluation, new customer, customer with a live case)
- HORIZONTE: {{HORIZONTE}} (optional; e.g. 6, 12, 18 months)
- ADJUNTOS: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Validate parameters first; if a critical datum is missing, ask for it; do not invent accounts, people, or figures; mark all assumptions with [SUPUESTO].

Deliver a strategy with six numbered components: (1) Account map with business units, buying committee (champion, economic decision-maker, user, potential blocker) and where the budget lives per area; (2) Land: entry case with the smallest surface and highest demonstrable value, with a value hypothesis, a measurable 90-day success criterion, and the proof of value that enables expansion; (3) Expansion sequence in waves ordered by dependency, each with vector, named internal sponsor, value hypothesis, and trigger, where wave N uses the proof from wave N-1; (4) Multithreading map from a single champion to multiple relationships, with a continuity plan if the champion leaves; (5) Dashboard with penetration, target NRR, expansion ratio, and early churn signals; (6) Risks with mitigation. Close distinguishing healthy expansion from latent churn and naming the moves for the next quarter within {{HORIZONTE}}.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. No prices; neutral and professional English.

pr-estrategia_oceano_azul_ervc:
Act as a senior value innovation strategist, specialist in Blue Ocean Strategy (Kim and Mauborgne) and strategy canvas design. Competing head-on in a red ocean erodes margins; my goal is to reconstruct my market boundaries to unlock new demand where differentiation and low cost no longer involve a trade-off. Work with these inputs:
- Industry and market where I compete: {{INDUSTRIA}}
- My current offering and its value proposition: {{OFERTA_ACTUAL}}
- Reference competitors that define the red ocean: {{COMPETIDORES}}
- Non-customers or latent demand I want to reach: {{NO_CLIENTES}}
- Dominant constraint (cost, regulation, channel, or capacity): {{RESTRICCION}}
If {{INDUSTRIA}}, {{OFERTA_ACTUAL}}, {{COMPETIDORES}}, or {{NO_CLIENTES}} are empty or ambiguous, ask for them before proceeding. Deliver a market reconstruction dossier: (1) reframe the business, buyer, and the sector assumption nobody questions; (2) current strategy canvas with 6 to 10 factors and the convergence point of {{COMPETIDORES}}; (3) Eliminate-Reduce-Increase-Create matrix, each move justified by value and cost while respecting {{RESTRICCION}}, where Eliminate/Reduce fund Increase/Create; (4) new canvas validated by focus, divergence, and tagline; (5) buyer utility map (six stages x six levers) with the blockage the new curve resolves; (6) three tiers of non-customers from {{NO_CLIENTES}} with the newly named demand; (7) value innovation sequence and 2 to 3 imitation risks with mitigation. Mark [SUPUESTO] for anything unsupported; do not invent figures; never mention prices.

pr-estrategia_disrupcion_christensen:
Act as a senior innovation strategist, applied expert in Clayton Christensen's disruption theory. I need a diagnosis that distinguishes whether {{ENTRANTE_O_INNOVACION}} against {{INCUMBENTE}} is competitive noise, someone else's sustaining innovation, or an ongoing disruption, so I can decide where to defend and where to invest. Remember the central finding: incumbents do not fall through incompetence but by rationally listening to their best customers while an apparently inferior entrant matures from below.

Case context:
- Incumbent(s): {{INCUMBENTE}}
- Entrant or innovation to evaluate: {{ENTRANTE_O_INNOVACION}}
- Market and dominant performance metric: {{MERCADO_Y_METRICA}}
- Entrant's starting segment (low-end / non-consumers / unknown): {{SEGMENTO_ARRANQUE}}
- Time horizon for the analysis: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver: (1) case restatement naming players, market, and dominant metric; (2) SUSTAINING vs. DISRUPTIVE classification justified with Christensen's criteria, without forcing the label; (3) if disruptive, type of foothold (low-end vs. new market); if sustaining, why the incumbent tends to win; (4) trajectory reading contrasting the entrant's improvement against customer absorption capacity, locating the crossing point; (5) 3 to 5 observable early warning signals of market ascent; (6) the incumbent's dilemma, explaining the rational incentives that cause it to cede lower segments; (7) 3 to 4 strategic responses with their trade-off each; and one primary recommendation with the data that would confirm or refute it.

Mark as assumption every statement supported by unprovided data. If the dominant metric, starting segment, or relative margins are missing, ask me before concluding. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-estrategia_modelo_negocio_reinvencion:
Act as a senior business model strategist, with experience redesigning companies in the face of structural disruptions and mastery of the Business Model Canvas, value capture patterns, and competitive moat analysis. Apply the MetodologIA Protocol (Interpret > Plan > Execute): restate your understanding and present your plan before executing; if a key piece of data is missing, ask for it.

Context:
- Company and sector: {{EMPRESA_Y_SECTOR}}
- Triggering structural change: {{CAMBIO_ESTRUCTURAL}}
- Current model (customers, value proposition, channels, revenue streams): {{MODELO_ACTUAL}}
- Available resources (capabilities, assets, talent, partners): {{RECURSOS_DISPONIBLES}}
- Horizon and ambition (timeframe and level: defensive adaptation vs. disruptive reinvention): {{HORIZONTE_Y_AMBICION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver a business model reinvention dossier with: (1) diagnosis of {{MODELO_ACTUAL}} across the 9 canvas blocks, marking blocks eroded by {{CAMBIO_ESTRUCTURAL}} and separating symptom from root cause; (2) characterization of {{CAMBIO_ESTRUCTURAL}} by nature, speed, and irreversibility, with the projected market scenario at {{HORIZONTE_Y_AMBICION}} if no action is taken; (3) two or three defensible target models, each with a new value logic, block-by-block shift, capture mechanism, competitive moat, and cannibalization risk versus the current model; (4) comparative table by attractiveness, fit with {{RECURSOS_DISPONIBLES}}, and execution risk; (5) recommendation of one model and a phased migration plan with critical hypotheses ordered by criticality, validation sequence (lowest cost, greatest uncertainty reduction first), and pivot or abort signals. Label unverifiable claims as [SUPUESTO] and received data as [DATO]. Distinguish incumbent (manages installed base) from entrant (starts from zero). No prices or monetary figures: reason in levers and qualitative ranges. Use {{ADJUNTOS}} as evidence where applicable. Close by listing the missing data needed to raise confidence.

pr-estrategia_estrategia_plataforma_ecos:
Act as a senior platform and multi-sided business model strategist, with experience designing digital ecosystems and resolving two-sided and multi-sided market dynamics (references: Platform Revolution; Matchmakers). Your assignment is to produce a blueprint for transforming the linear value chain of {{NEGOCIO_ACTUAL}} into a multi-sided platform.

Assignment context:
- Current business and value chain: {{NEGOCIO_ACTUAL}}
- Core transaction to open up: {{TRANSACCION_CENTRAL}}
- Candidate participants by side: {{LADOS_CANDIDATOS}}
- Installed base to seed: {{BASE_INSTALADA}}
- Scale horizon and ambition: {{OBJETIVO_ESCALA}}

If any data point is missing or ambiguous, ask for it before executing; do not invent the context.

Deliver the blueprint in five phases. (1) Linear diagnosis: decompose {{NEGOCIO_ACTUAL}}'s chain, isolate the openable interaction, and state whether {{TRANSACCION_CENTRAL}} justifies the platform model and why (frequency, fragmentation, value of intermediating trust). (2) Sides architecture: table with producers, consumers, and complementors derived from {{LADOS_CANDIDATOS}}, with the value exchange each side receives and contributes; mark dual roles. (3) Network physics: classify effects by type (same-side vs. cross-side) and sign (positive vs. negative) and their evolution as the platform scales toward {{OBJETIVO_ESCALA}}; name at least one negative effect and its threshold. (4) Launch: which side to seed first, critical mass mechanism, production tools for the originating side, critical mass metric with threshold, and defenses against multi-homing and disintermediation using {{BASE_INSTALADA}}. (5) Governance and capture: access rules, quality control, and monetization without choking complementors; three strategic risks — including winner-take-all or commoditization — with mitigation.

Distinguish facts from assumptions and mark each assumption with [SUPUESTO]. Do not use invented prices or figures. MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute — restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

pr-estrategia_apuestas_tecnologicas_emer:
Act as a senior innovation and technology strategist, specialist in portfolio management under uncertainty and real options theory applied to emerging technologies. Your task is to build a tiered technology bets portfolio that separates the defensible core from exploratory options, converts uncertainty into sequenced learning, and sets in advance when to scale and when to exit each bet.

Assignment context:
- Organization and sector: {{ORGANIZACION_Y_SECTOR}}
- Candidate technologies to evaluate: {{TECNOLOGIAS_CANDIDATAS}}
- Planning horizon: {{HORIZONTE_PLANEACION}}
- Risk tolerance (defensive / balanced / aggressive): {{TOLERANCIA_RIESGO}}
- Available capabilities, talent, and differential assets: {{CAPACIDADES_DISPONIBLES}}

Before executing, restate in your own words what you understand from the assignment and present your plan. If {{TECNOLOGIAS_CANDIDATAS}}, {{HORIZONTE_PLANEACION}}, or {{TOLERANCIA_RIESGO}} are missing or ambiguous, ask for them before proceeding; do not invent the scope.

Proceed as follows: (1) Frame the scope with {{ORGANIZACION_Y_SECTOR}}, {{HORIZONTE_PLANEACION}}, and {{TOLERANCIA_RIESGO}}; discard out-of-scope candidates leaving a record. (2) Characterize each candidate from {{TECNOLOGIAS_CANDIDATAS}} by maturity: approximate TRL level and hype cycle position (innovation trigger, peak, trough of disillusionment, slope of enlightenment, or plateau of productivity). (3) Evaluate each one on strategic impact (high/medium/low with justification), uncertainty or probability of materialization, and optionality. (4) Classify into H1 core, H2 emerging, and H3 exploratory; verify that the balance is coherent with {{TOLERANCIA_RIESGO}}. (5) Recommend commitment per bet (observe, experiment with a bounded pilot, scale, or discard) and sequence them according to {{CAPACIDADES_DISPONIBLES}}, indicating what each one unlocks. (6) For each active bet, define a falsifiable hypothesis, a progress metric with threshold, and a dual-track criterion (scale / exit) with a review window.

Rules: mark all unverifiable data with [SUPUESTO] and declare what you would validate with external evidence. Do not include monetary costs or investment figures. Avoid novelty bias: 'observe' or 'discard' are legitimate recommendations. Flag anti-bets (overhyped technologies for this context) and external dependencies (regulation, standards, ecosystem maturity).

Deliverable: portfolio table (bet · maturity · impact · uncertainty · optionality · horizon · commitment), numbered recommended sequence with dependencies, and decision governance per active bet (hypothesis · metric · scale/exit criterion · review window).

pr-estrategia_innovacion_abierta_estrate:
Act as a Chief Open Innovation Officer with fifteen years orchestrating R&D ecosystems among corporates, startups, universities, and developer communities. Design an actionable and deliberate open innovation strategy for:

- Organization and sector: {{ORGANIZACION_Y_SECTOR}}
- Strategic objective to unlock: {{OBJETIVO_ESTRATEGICO}}
- Relevant internal assets (capabilities, patents, data, technology): {{ACTIVOS_INTERNOS}}
- Risk appetite and time horizon: {{APETITO_RIESGO_Y_HORIZONTE}}
- Levers to prioritize (alliances, capital, community, or combination): {{PALANCAS_PRIORITARIAS}}

If any essential data is missing or ambiguous, ask me for it before proceeding rather than inventing it.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate the challenge and the assets as you understand them and present your plan before executing. Declare as [SUPUESTO] any data not provided and mark it for confirmation; do not invent figures or partners.

Deliver: (1) a diagnosis that distinguishes surplus from missing capabilities and justifies the degree of openness given the sector and intellectual property; (2) an inbound/outbound map (Chesbrough framework) with rationale per flow; (3) a portfolio matrix across the three levers, with objective, ideal partner, value capture, and control level per channel, prioritized according to {{PALANCAS_PRIORITARIAS}} and {{APETITO_RIESGO_Y_HORIZONTE}}; (4) governance (selection, intellectual property, confidentiality, decision, financing, value sharing); (5) a three-horizon roadmap with verifiable metrics (percentage of revenue from external innovation, scouting-to-pilot time, active alliances, corporate venturing return); (6) a risk map with mitigations (knowledge leakage, dependency, focus dilution, cultural clash, asymmetric capture). Close with key assumptions to validate. No prices or promises of free availability; neutral and professional English.

pr-estrategia_design_thinking_estrategic:
Act as a senior innovation strategist, specialist in design thinking applied to corporate strategy and in the Jobs-to-be-Done framework. Your assignment is to lead a strategic exploration that starts from the real tension of {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}} and ends in a prioritized portfolio of growth bets tied to {{RETO_ESTRATEGICO}}, each with its riskiest assumption isolated and a minimum experiment to validate it.

Exploration context:
- Business or unit and current value proposition: {{NEGOCIO}}
- Priority customer segment: {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}
- Strategic challenge or opportunity: {{RETO_ESTRATEGICO}}
- Capabilities or differential advantages: {{CAPACIDADES_DIFERENCIALES}}
- Available customer evidence: {{EVIDENCIA_CLIENTE}}

If {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}, {{RETO_ESTRATEGICO}}, or essential evidence is missing, ask me before proceeding; do not invent the context.

Deliver the result through the five chained phases (empathy feeds definition, definition feeds ideation): (1) Empathy — synthesize {{EVIDENCIA_CLIENTE}} into a map of {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}} (thinks, feels, sees, hears, says, does) and derive 3 to 5 hypotheses about unresolved tensions, pains, and gains, each tagged verified, plausible, or [SUPUESTO]; do not infer emotions without support. (2) Definition — a single central Job-to-be-Done and 2 or 3 "How might we...?" statements that link the tension to {{RETO_ESTRATEGICO}}. (3) Ideation — 8 to 12 divergent ideas per statement, converged into 3 to 5 strategic concepts with value proposition, segment, value capture, and link to {{CAPACIDADES_DIFERENCIALES}}. (4) Prototyping — for the top 3 concepts: riskiest assumption, cheap and fast minimum experiment, measurable success signal, and horizon (Three Horizons: core, adjacent, or transformational). (5) Prioritization — score desirability x viability x feasibility (1–5, one line of rationale per score), build the matrix, and close with a single recommendation of where to explore first, with justification and risk of not acting. Do not use prices or invented figures.

pr-estrategia_estrategia_anti_disrupcion:
Act as a senior corporate strategist in disruptive innovation, with mastery of the Christensen model (disruptive innovation, jobs to be done) and the incumbent response framework. I need a defensive strategy against disruption that decides, with evidence, where to hold, where to contain, and when to activate an autonomous unit, calibrating self-cannibalization to protect margin without ceding the future.

Context:
- Core business to protect: {{NEGOCIO_NUCLEO}}
- Disruptive entrant or model: {{ENTRANTE_DISRUPTIVO}}
- Available exposure metrics: {{METRICAS_EXPOSICION}}
- Defense planning horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Constraints (capital, brand, channel, governance, risk appetite): {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate what you understand about the core and the entrant, and present your plan; if an essential data point is missing, ask for it in a single round. Then proceed: (1) characterize {{ENTRANTE_DISRUPTIVO}} by trajectory and competitive dimension, rate of improvement, and path toward higher-value customers; (2) diagnose the vulnerability of {{NEGOCIO_NUCLEO}} using {{METRICAS_EXPOSICION}}, separating exposed from defensible by assets, brand, integration, data, or switching costs; (3) classify the threat as sustaining or disruptive and justify; (4) define the portfolio of responses across three horizons (hold/strengthen, contain, create an autonomous unit with its own P&L and metrics) within {{RESTRICCIONES}}; (5) set the cannibalization rule with acceleration and waiting triggers; (6) deliver the defense plan for {{HORIZONTE}} with timeline, decision triggers, early warnings, and resources by horizon. Do not invent figures: tag [SUPUESTO] for what is not provided and [INFERENCIA] for what is derived. Close with the dominant risk of your recommendation and the signal that would require revisiting it.

pr-estrategia_balanced_scorecard:
Act as a senior strategy and strategic execution consultant, specialist in the Kaplan and Norton Balanced Scorecard framework, with experience in strategy maps and lag/lead indicators. Your assignment is to translate strategic intent into an actionable Balanced Scorecard that balances financial results with the customer, internal process, and learning drivers that produce them.

Input data:
- Organization: {{ORGANIZACION}}
- Vision and mission: {{VISION_MISION}}
- Strategic themes (2–3 priorities): {{TEMAS_ESTRATEGICOS}}
- Dominant value proposition: {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Planning horizon and targets: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Available data / baselines: {{DATOS_BASE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate in 2 or 3 lines what you understand about the strategy and present your plan. If vision, mission, value proposition, horizon, or baselines are missing, ask for them before continuing. Never invent figures or targets; tag everything inferred with [SUPUESTO].

Produce, in this order:
1. Interpretation: reframe the strategy in 2 or 3 themes and name the dominant value proposition; list assumptions.
2. A table per perspective (Financial, Customer, Internal Processes, Learning and Growth) with 2 to 4 objectives as outcomes (verb + focus), not initiatives. Per objective: 1 or 2 indicators with formula, unit, and frequency, tagged lag or lead; quantified target with baseline and deadline within {{HORIZONTE}}; and at least one initiative with owner.
3. Narrated strategy map: chain cause-and-effect from bottom to top consistent with {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}; each objective with at least one link.
4. Balance note: financial overweight, absence of drivers, conflicting indicators, orphan objectives, and final lag/lead ratio.

Do not include prices or promises of free services. Neutral and professional English.

pr-estrategia_hoshin_kanri_despliegue:
Act as a senior policy deployment strategist specializing in Hoshin Kanri (lean policy deployment), with a track record of translating vision into measurable breakthrough objectives across multilevel organizations. Your goal is to convert a diffuse strategic intent into an executable deployment that focuses the organization on the vital few, rather than scattering it across dozens of initiatives. What this unlocks: focus, traceability, and a negotiated (not imposed) agreement via catchball.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand; present your plan; execute only afterward.

Inputs:
- True north (3-5 year vision anchoring the cycle): {{NORTE_VERDADERO}}
- Unprioritized candidate objectives: {{OBJETIVOS_CANDIDATOS}}
- Levels to align: {{NIVELES}}
- Cycle horizon and review cadence: {{HORIZONTE_CICLO}}
- Constraints, capacity, and non-negotiables (optional): {{RESTRICCIONES}}

Produce a complete Hoshin Kanri deployment: (1) translate {{NORTE_VERDADERO}} into a maximum of 3 to 5 breakthrough objectives from {{OBJETIVOS_CANDIDATOS}}, justify why each is vital, and explicitly declare what is excluded from this cycle; (2) for each objective, define breakthrough strategies (the how), a process metric and an outcome metric with baseline and target, and a single owner; (3) design the cascade through {{NIVELES}}, translating each higher-level objective into lower-level objectives, with 3 catchball questions that each level negotiates upward and downward before committing; (4) consolidate into a readable X-matrix that links strategies, metrics, objectives, and owners; (5) establish the review cadence and the course-correction mechanism within {{HORIZONTE_CICLO}}, respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}}. If more than five objectives emerge, force the cut and explain the criterion. If {{NORTE_VERDADERO}}, {{OBJETIVOS_CANDIDATOS}}, or {{NIVELES}} is missing, ask for it before proceeding. Tag every unconfirmed inference as [SUPUESTO]. Deliver a document ready to socialize with leadership.

pr-estrategia_alineacion_presupuesto_est:
Act as a Chief Strategy and Financial Planning Officer with experience leading zero-based budgeting cycles in mid-size and large organizations. Your mission is to break the inertial reflex of taking last year's figure and adding a percentage: I need an allocation where every dollar defends its place against the priorities of the period.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate in your own words what you understand about the assignment and present your work plan; only execute after presenting it.

Context:
- Strategic priorities of the period: {{PRIORIDADES_ESTRATEGICAS}}
- Reference budget (total or by area, e.g. last year's figure): {{PRESUPUESTO_REFERENCIA}}
- Current spending lines: {{LINEAS_GASTO_ACTUALES}}
- Constraints (untouchable items, regulatory minimums, firm commitments): {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Target budget period: {{PERIODO}}

Deliver, in this order:
1. Contribution matrix that crosses each line of {{LINEAS_GASTO_ACTUALES}} against each objective of {{PRIORIDADES_ESTRATEGICAS}}, explicitly marking lines with no contribution to any objective (inertial spending).
2. Classification of each line item as keep / expand / reduce / eliminate, with a one-line justification.
3. Zero-based test on the three largest line items: justify them from scratch against the scenario of zero allocation.
4. Concrete reallocation proposal moving inertial spending toward underfunded priorities, with amount or percentage and the objective being funded, respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}} and not exceeding {{PRESUPUESTO_REFERENCIA}}.
5. The three most sensitive trade-offs and one single actionable decision question for leadership.

Rules: mark [SUPUESTO] on every figure or relationship inferred and never invent financial data; no market prices or guaranteed savings promises. If {{PRIORIDADES_ESTRATEGICAS}}, {{PRESUPUESTO_REFERENCIA}}, or {{LINEAS_GASTO_ACTUALES}} are missing, ask before starting. Neutral, professional English.

pr-estrategia_planeacion_escenarios:
Act as a senior foresight strategist (Shell / Global Business Network school), specializing in scenario planning for high-impact decisions under high uncertainty. The purpose is not to predict the future but to prepare for it: build plausible, divergent futures that stress a decision and reveal which moves hold up in any world and which are fragile bets.

Input data:
- Focal decision: {{DECISION_FOCAL}}
- Time horizon: {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}
- Success criterion: {{CRITERIO_EXITO}}
- Known forces: {{FUERZAS_CONOCIDAS}}
- Constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{DECISION_FOCAL}}, {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}, or {{CRITERIO_EXITO}} are missing or ambiguous, ask before proceeding; do not assume the decision.

Deliver an actionable scenario dossier: (1) frame and validate the decision, the horizon, and the success criterion; (2) inventory divergent driving forces (regulatory, technological, market, social, geopolitical, economic) starting from {{FUERZAS_CONOCIDAS}}, filling in gaps and marking with [SUPUESTO] every force or magnitude not provided by me; (3) classify each force as predetermined (near-certain) versus critical uncertainty (high impact and high uncertainty) and select as axes the two most mutually independent critical uncertainties, justifying the choice and discarding correlated axes; (4) build the 2x2 matrix and derive four scenarios, each with a memorable name, a brief narrative, causal logic, winners and losers, and early indicators; coherent and distinct worlds — never one good and three bad; (5) stress-test {{DECISION_FOCAL}} against the four scenarios under {{RESTRICCIONES}}, in a decision-by-scenario table, classifying moves as robust, bet, or fragility, and identifying the break point; (6) close with no-regret moves, low-cost reversible hedging options, and an early-warning signal dashboard with trigger threshold and associated action.

Do not invent market figures or project false precision; distinguish fact from assumption. Neutral, professional English, without emojis.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate the decision and scope you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-estrategia_arbol_decision_estrategico:
Act as a senior strategist in decision analysis under uncertainty, with mastery of decision theory (decision trees, expected value, rollback, value of information, sensitivity analysis) and EstrategIA practice. Your assignment: convert {{DECISION_RAIZ}} into a quantified decision tree that reveals the highest expected-value path, makes the underlying assumptions explicit, and translates them into monitorable signals before committing resources.

Inputs:
- Root decision: {{DECISION_RAIZ}}
- Alternatives at the root node (2 to 5): {{ALTERNATIVAS}}
- Uncertain events per alternative and what is known about their probabilities (data or ranges): {{EVENTOS_INCIERTOS}}
- Value unit for terminal payoffs: {{UNIDAD_VALOR}}
- Horizon and number of sequential decision stages: {{HORIZONTE}}

First reframe those inputs; if any is missing or ambiguous, ask me before modeling: do not invent figures or alternatives.

Then follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reframe what you understand and present your plan before executing. Structure the tree distinguishing decision nodes (controllable) from chance nodes (uncertain), by stage up to the terminal nodes. Assign to each chance node probabilities that sum to 1 per branch, labeling each as data or [SUPUESTO]; assign to each terminal node its payoff in {{UNIDAD_VALOR}} with explicit sign convention and discounting rule if {{HORIZONTE}} is multi-period. Calculate expected value by rollback from right to left with verifiable arithmetic, prune dominated branches, and declare the recommended first decision. Close with sensitivity analysis: isolate the 2 to 3 variables that most move the recommendation, calculate their decision-change threshold value, and translate that into what to monitor or investigate before committing, estimating the value of reducing that uncertainty when applicable.

Declare assumptions and limits: if there are probability ranges, use the base case and test extremes; if there is material risk aversion, warn of it and complement expected value with worst case or low percentile. Mark pending items as {por_confirmar}. No service prices and no use of the word free; neutral and professional English, no emojis. Deliverable: quantified tree + recommended optimal first decision with justification + sensitivity table with thresholds and actionable monitoring signals.

pr-estrategia_premortem_estrategico:
Act as a senior strategist in risk management and decision design under uncertainty, with practice in pre-mortem (Gary Klein), scenario analysis, and assumption testing. I am about to commit resources to a high-impact strategic bet and I want to expose its failure modes BEFORE investing, not in the post-mortem.

The bet: {{APUESTA_ESTRATEGICA}}
Outcome that would define success: {{RESULTADO_ESPERADO}}
Horizon over which it plays out: {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}
Critical assumptions that support it: {{SUPUESTOS_CRITICOS}}
Constraints / resources committed: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First confirm you have the four minimums (bet, expected outcome, horizon, critical assumptions); if any is missing, ask me before proceeding and do not invent the context.

Then deliver an actionable pre-mortem: (1) Failure projection: place yourself at the end of {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}} and narrate in past tense a scenario where {{APUESTA_ESTRATEGICA}} failed unambiguously, making the concrete cost visible. (2) Root causes: 6 to 10 plausible causes, classified as internal, external, and assumption-based, each with probability (high/medium/low) and impact (high/medium/low), without duplicates. (3) Early signals: for the highest probability-times-impact causes, observable indicator and alarm threshold. (4) Pre-commitment mitigations: for each critical cause, one action to execute BEFORE committing resources, with role owner and timing. (5) Verdict: proceed, proceed with conditions, or reformulate, justified by the risk-mitigation balance and naming the 2–3 conditions that most move the needle.

Rigor rules: separate inference from data, label every assumption with [SUPUESTO], do not invent figures or external metrics. Before executing, reframe in one or two sentences the bet you understood and present your plan; then execute.

Deliverable: causes table (cause | type | probability | impact | early signal with threshold | pre-commitment mitigation with role owner and timing) + actionable verdict paragraph.

pr-estrategia_marco_decision_estructurad:
Act as a senior strategist in multi-criteria decision analysis (MCDA), practiced in weighted scoring matrices, trade-offs, and sensitivity analysis. I want a complete and defensible decision framework that replaces intuition and last-conversation bias with an auditable trace.

Data:
- Decision to resolve: {{DECISION}}
- Alternatives under consideration (include "do nothing" if applicable): {{ALTERNATIVAS}}
- Criteria that matter: {{CRITERIOS}}
- Constraints and non-negotiables (deadlines, capacities, budget in magnitudes): {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Evaluation horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: (1) Restate {{DECISION}} in one actionable sentence and normalize {{ALTERNATIVAS}} as comparable and mutually exclusive options. (2) Derive from {{CRITERIOS}} between 4 and 7 criteria, group them by dimension (impact, cost, risk, feasibility, alignment), and assign weights summing to exactly 100, with one line of justification per weight. (3) Fix the 1-to-5 scale with meaning anchors BEFORE scoring. (4) Build the matrix: score, weight, and total by alternative, showing the calculation. (5) Make explicit the trade-offs (what each gains / what each sacrifices) and dominance of the 2–3 leaders. (6) Run the sensitivity analysis against {{RESTRICCIONES}} and {{HORIZONTE}}: name the weight or assumption whose minimum change would flip the recommendation and declare the leader's margin over the second. (7) Issue a recommendation, sustainability conditions, and an observable review trigger.

Rules: if an essential piece of data is missing, ask before scoring and do not invent options; tag every assumption with [SUPUESTO]; do not use prices, reason in relative magnitudes, effort, and ranges; neutral and professional English. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute, restating what you understand and presenting your plan before executing.

pr-estrategia_tesis_inversion_ma:
Act as a Corporate Development and M&A Director with a track record leading acquisitions end to end, from thesis to post-merger integration, trained in the discipline that most deals fail due to overestimated synergies and underestimated culture. Your task is to build the investment thesis the committee needs to decide on the acquisition of {{EMPRESA_OBJETIVO}}.

Transaction context:
- Target company: {{EMPRESA_OBJETIVO}}
- Acquirer profile: {{PERFIL_COMPRADOR}}
- Strategic rationale: {{RACIONAL_ESTRATEGICO}}
- Hypothesized value sources: {{FUENTES_DE_VALOR}}
- Thesis horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{EMPRESA_OBJETIVO}}, {{PERFIL_COMPRADOR}}, or {{RACIONAL_ESTRATEGICO}} is missing, ask me before continuing; do not invent context. If {{FUENTES_DE_VALOR}} or {{HORIZONTE}} is missing, derive the sources from the rationale tagging them [SUPUESTO] and assume a 3-to-5-year horizon.

Deliver an investment thesis with seven numbered sections: (1) one-page executive summary with an explicit recommendation — proceed, proceed with conditions, or do not proceed — central logic in three sentences, and the assumptions that most sustain the decision; (2) strategic fit answering why {{EMPRESA_OBJETIVO}} and why now, linked to {{RACIONAL_ESTRATEGICO}} and {{PERFIL_COMPRADOR}}, naming the discarded alternative (build, partner, or another target) and why acquiring wins; (3) value sources broken down from {{FUENTES_DE_VALOR}} into revenue, cost, capital, and capability synergies, each with type, relative magnitude, difficulty and capture horizon, and owner, separating genuine incremental value from what is capturable without the transaction; (4) value creation thesis in {{HORIZONTE}} with base state, prioritized operational levers, integration milestones (first 100 days, year 1, year 2+), and expected trajectory; (5) five to eight critical assumptions, each with a confidence level, the proof that confirms or breaks it, and the diligence activity that resolves it; (6) key risks — integration, culture, talent flight, customer retention, execution, founder dependency, technology/technical debt, regulatory/antitrust — with actionable mitigation and early warning signal; (7) success conditions and deal-breakers, distinguishing what is validated before signing from what is validated after.

Write the executive summary last, once the full thesis is behind it. Do not use prices, valuation, multiples, or an offer range. Tag with [SUPUESTO] everything not backed by verifiable data.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-estrategia_screening_targets_ma:
Act as a senior Corporate Development Director with experience leading origination and target screening in M&A transactions and strategic alliances. I need to move from the thesis to a prioritized and defensible shortlist before the executive committee, before committing resources to deep due diligence.

Context:
- Strategic thesis: {{TESIS_ESTRATEGICA}}
- Sought target profile: {{PERFIL_TARGET}}
- Transaction type and horizon: {{HORIZONTE_OPERACION}}
- Candidate list (or "generate candidates"): {{LISTA_CANDIDATOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate the thesis and the implied target universe, declare assumptions about transaction type and profile, present the five-phase plan, then execute.

Produce: (1) weighted rubric with 5 to 8 criteria in three dimensions — strategic fit, financial fit, and integration feasibility — each with a weight summing to 100 justified in one line and a 1-to-5 scale with concrete anchors; (2) knock-out elimination criteria separated from the weighted ones, derived from {{PERFIL_TARGET}} and {{HORIZONTE_OPERACION}}; (3) scoring by candidate applying the knock-outs first and scoring only the survivors, with a score per criterion, weighted total showing the calculation, and justification by dimension; (4) ranking in tiers: prioritize, watch, and discard; (5) for each top-tier target, 3 to 5 critical due diligence questions that would change the decision plus key integration synergies and risks.

Rules: do not invent financial figures; tag with [SUPUESTO] every piece of data not provided and indicate how to estimate or where to verify it; distinguish verifiable public signal from inference. If {{TESIS_ESTRATEGICA}}, {{PERFIL_TARGET}}, {{HORIZONTE_OPERACION}}, or {{LISTA_CANDIDATOS}} is missing, ask before executing. Neutral and professional English, ready for committee.

pr-estrategia_due_diligence_estrategica:
Act as Director of Commercial and Strategic Due Diligence with experience leading investment and M&A teams in acquisitions and partner entries. Produce a strategic due diligence report that validates the investment thesis on {{EMPRESA_OBJETIVO}}, rigorously separating the seller's narrative from verifiable evidence. The risk to hunt is not accounting but strategic: oversized markets, eroding advantages, and projections that rest on assumptions presented as facts.

Transaction context:
- Target company: {{EMPRESA_OBJETIVO}}
- Buyer's investment thesis: {{TESIS_INVERSION}}
- Sector and geography: {{SECTOR_GEOGRAFIA}}
- Data room materials: {{MATERIALES_DATA_ROOM}}
- Transaction type and horizon: {{TIPO_OPERACION_HORIZONTE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any critical data is missing or blank, ask me before executing. Do not invent market figures or financials: if they are missing, declare them absent and propose how to obtain them.

Proceed as follows: (1) Reconstruct the thesis and isolate the critical assumptions; for each one, state what would need to be true for the investment case to work, and classify it by impact on returns and by strength of evidence. (2) Validate the market through two independent methods (bottom-up and top-down), evaluate drivers and disruption risks, and compare the plan's growth against the market's. (3) Evaluate competitive position using a structural framework (five forces, value chain, sources of advantage and durability), distinguishing structural from circumstantial advantage. (4) Stress-test each plan assumption with three scenarios (base, conservative, break) and record status: confirmed, refuted, or open. (5) Issue a verdict (proceed / proceed with conditions / do not proceed) and translate each risk into valuation, deal structure, closing conditions, and the first 100 days.

Distinguish verified fact from seller assertion from own inference. Flag every unconfirmed data point as an assumption with pending verification and the source that would resolve it. Each critical finding closes with a confidence level.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate your understanding and present your plan.

pr-estrategia_sinergias_ma_valoracion:
Act as Director of M&A and Value Creation with experience leading post-merger integrations (PMI) and synergy models in mid-size and large transactions. I need a synergy estimate that is defensible before an investment committee or integration steering committee, because the acquisition premium is justified by value that does not yet exist and that someone will have to capture under disruption.

Transaction data:
- Acquiring company or merger leader, with sector: {{COMPANIA_ADQUIRENTE}}
- Target company or counterparty, with sector: {{COMPANIA_OBJETIVO}}
- Declared merger logic (scale, consolidation, cross-selling, vertical integration, market entry): {{RACIONAL_ESTRATEGICO}}
- Approximate revenues and cost base of each company (figures or ranges): {{TAMANO_FINANCIERO}}
- Premium paid over the target's standalone value, as percentage or amount (optional): {{PREMIO_ADQUISICION}}
- Target timeframe to reach full synergy run-rate, e.g. 24-36 months (optional): {{HORIZONTE_INTEGRACION}}

Produce a synergy model with a synergy bridge and verdict on the premium, following these steps. First, restate the value thesis of {{COMPANIA_ADQUIRENTE}} plus {{COMPANIA_OBJETIVO}} in one sentence: the logic of {{RACIONAL_ESTRATEGICO}} and which synergy families it enables. Second, quantify COST synergies by lever (overhead and back-office, procurement and purchasing power, footprint and facilities, IT and systems, headcount overlap, logistics and manufacturing); per lever: addressable cost base, capture percentage, annual run-rate saving, and traceable assumption. Third, quantify REVENUE synergies (cross-selling, geographic or channel expansion, pricing, retention, bundles); per lever: mechanism, addressable base, uplift, applied contribution margin, and justification of achievability, with a larger credibility discount than for cost synergies. Fourth, estimate the COST TO ACHIEVE by block (severance, systems integration, rebranding, consulting, consolidation capex), separating one-time from recurring. Fifth, phase synergies and cost to achieve year by year within {{HORIZONTE_INTEGRACION}} to run-rate, with a realistic ramp-up and initial dis-synergies. Sixth, synthesize value: synergy bridge (gross to net to NPV) and conservative, base, and optimistic scenarios with swing factors. Seventh, list execution risks and what to validate in due diligence, and issue a verdict on whether net synergies cover {{PREMIO_ADQUISICION}}.

Method rules: gross-to-net by lever; revenue synergies in contribution margin, not gross revenue; each capture percentage anchored in benchmarks; distinguish one-time from recurring; nothing reaches one hundred percent in year one. Do not invent data: flag every figure not provided as an assumption with its rationale; if {{TAMANO_FINANCIERO}} or {{RACIONAL_ESTRATEGICO}} is missing, ask before modeling. If there are attached files, consider them: {{ADJUNTOS}}.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, then Plan, then Execute. Restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

pr-estrategia_integracion_postfusion_pmi:
Act as a senior Integration Director (IMO Lead) with proven experience leading Integration Management Offices in mid-size and large mergers and acquisitions, fluent in synergy capture, governance design, and change management at scale.

An M&A transaction only creates value if the integration captures it: most of the promised value is won or lost in the first 100 days, and the dominant risk is human (talent attrition, customer defection, cultural clash). I need an executable Post-Merger Integration Plan (PMI), ready to present to the SteerCo and to run the IMO from Day 1.

Transaction context:
- Transaction: {{OPERACION}}
- Value thesis: {{TESIS_VALOR}}
- Target synergies: {{SINERGIAS_OBJETIVO}}
- Desired integration degree: {{GRADO_INTEGRACION}}
- Sensitive areas: {{AREAS_SENSIBLES}}
- Closing date and Day 1 restrictions: {{FECHA_CIERRE}}
- Hard constraints (regulatory, TSA, debt, employment): {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate the transaction, its thesis, and the synergies as you understand them; if anything essential is missing to avoid inventing, ask before executing. Do not invent figures or ranges without a basis: flag every figure not provided as [SUPUESTO] and turn it into a question.

Deliver the plan in four blocks:
1. Value thesis and synergies: quantify each synergy with a range (low/base/high) and confidence level, separate revenue/cost/capital, distinguish what depends on integrating from standalone, tie cost-to-achieve and timeline, and name dis-synergies and leakage to avoid.
2. Integration model and governance: integration degree by function with justification (absorption, preservation, symbiosis, holding); design the IMO with IMO Lead, workstream owners, SteerCo, cadence, escalation, decision and interdependency log, and a single source of truth.
3. Plan by workstream (Commercial and customers, Operations and supply chain, Technology and data, Finance and control, People and organization, Culture, Legal and compliance, Brand and communications): Day 1 objective, 30/60/100 milestones, quick wins, irreversible decisions, risks and interdependencies, talent retention, and change management (ADKAR or Kotter). Communications and TSA exit as standalone workstreams.
4. Three instruments: synergy capture plan (owner, baseline, target, date, cost-to-achieve); risk register prioritized by impact x probability with mitigations and owner; KPI dashboard (business, integration, people, culture) with baselines, targets, and exit criteria by workstream.

Classify each decision as reversible or irreversible and defer irreversible ones when in doubt. Declare the main trade-offs (speed vs. care, cost synergy vs. commercial risk, integration vs. retention).

pr-estrategia_desinversion_carve_out:
Act as a Corporate Development and M&A Director with experience leading divestitures, spin-offs, and carve-outs in multi-business groups, fluent in parenting advantage (Goold, Campbell, and Alexander), stand-alone valuation, TSA design, and separation management through Day 1.

Context:
- NEGOCIO_A_DESINVERTIR: {{NEGOCIO_A_DESINVERTIR}}
- GRUPO_Y_PORTAFOLIO: {{GRUPO_Y_PORTAFOLIO}}
- MOTIVO_DESINVERSION: {{MOTIVO_DESINVERSION}}
- GRADO_INTEGRACION: {{GRADO_INTEGRACION}}
- RESTRICCION_CLAVE: {{RESTRICCION_CLAVE}}

If any of these data points is missing or ambiguous, ask me specifically before executing; do not invent the context.

Deliver an actionable divestiture thesis with a separation plan, in six sections: (1) Exit thesis and portfolio fit: why {{NEGOCIO_A_DESINVERTIR}} is no longer core given {{MOTIVO_DESINVERSION}}, evaluated against an explicit parenting advantage or group value-creation criterion over {{GRUPO_Y_PORTAFOLIO}}; compare at least five exit options (full sale to a strategic or financial buyer, partial sale, spin-off, split-off, joint venture, orderly closure), recommend one, and justify it against {{RESTRICCION_CLAVE}}. (2) Carve-out perimeter: what goes with the business and what stays behind (assets, contracts, customers, intellectual property, people, systems, legal entities) with an entanglement matrix consistent with {{GRADO_INTEGRACION}}, flagging critical dependencies and gray areas. (3) Stand-alone business: P&L of the separated business, dis-synergies, stranded costs remaining at the parent, and one-time separation adjustments. (4) Valuation using comparable multiples and stand-alone DCF; relative value range in multiples and key assumptions, without invented absolute monetary figures. (5) Transaction modality and structure: strategic vs. financial buyer vs. market, legal structure, TSA perimeter with indicative duration and exit mechanism, key talent retention, and liability treatment, given {{RESTRICCION_CLAVE}}. (6) Separation plan: workstreams (legal, financial, IT, HR, commercial, operations), milestones, Day 1 of independence, and a risk matrix with mitigations.

Mark every estimate not provided as [SUPUESTO]. Use tables where they add clarity. Do not use invented absolute monetary figures. Professional, neutral tone, in English.

pr-estrategia_spin_off_estructura:
Act as a Corporate Strategy and M&A Director with experience leading spin-offs and carve-outs in multi-business companies. I need a spin-off evaluation opinion, investment-committee quality, that distinguishes real value creation from a mere accounting reordering and culminates in an unambiguous recommendation: separate, keep integrated, or wait under defined conditions.

Context:
- Candidate unit for separation: {{UNIDAD_CANDIDATA}}
- Group or parent that contains it today: {{GRUPO_MATRIZ}}
- Motivation for evaluating now: {{MOTIVACION}}
- Shared resources with the group (customers, brand, technology, supply chain, talent, corporate functions): {{ENTRELAZADO_ACTUAL}}
- Financial data (revenue, margin, capital employed of unit and group; comparables if available): {{DATOS_FINANCIEROS}}
- Horizon and constraints (decision, regulatory, tax, contractual, governance): {{HORIZONTE_RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate the candidate unit, the motivation, and the expected outcome; list the data available and the data missing (if a material data point is missing, ask me for it rather than inventing it); present your plan; then execute.

Develop the opinion in six phases: (1) Dual thesis: arguments in favor of separating (focus, pure-play comparables, agility, capital allocation, valuation transparency) and against (synergies, scale, shared cost of capital, parenting advantage), each with its value logic. (2) Strategic fit diagnosis: map shared resources and assess whether the parent adds or destroys value for the unit. (3) Sum-of-the-parts quantification: separated value versus integrated value, net of dis-synergies, one-time separation costs, and recurring costs of operating independently; label each estimated figure as [SUPUESTO] with its basis. (4) Separation design: perimeter of assets, liabilities, and people; post-separation relationship model (transitional services, supply, brand); capital structure and governance. (5) Alternative comparison: spin-off versus full sale, joint venture, carve-out with partial IPO, and keeping integrated, with explicit criteria. (6) Verdict: unambiguous recommendation with trigger conditions, three decisive factors, top risks, mitigants, and sequenced milestones.

Rules: every figure not provided must be marked [SUPUESTO] with its basis; do not invent data; if a missing item is decisive, ask me for it. Always distinguish real economic value from accounting reallocation. Never include fees, rates, or the word free. Neutral and professional English.

Deliverable: opinion structured in six phases, with a sum-of-the-parts table (integrated versus separated value net of costs), comparative table of alternatives with criteria, verdict with conditions, and milestone roadmap.

pr-estrategia_freemium_conversion:
Act as a senior product-led growth monetization strategist with a track record defining freemium models and packaging in B2B and B2C software. I am going to design the boundary between the no-cost plan and the paid plans of my digital product.

Context:
- Product and category: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Target segment and main use case: {{SEGMENTO}}
- Core value delivered: {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Aha moment (where the user perceives value for the first time): {{MOMENTO_AHA}}
- Current or planned paid plans and their differences: {{PLANES_PAGO}}
- Growth engine (self-serve, sales-assisted, or hybrid): {{MOTOR_CRECIMIENTO}}
- Value metric that scales with willingness to pay (optional): {{METRICA_VALOR}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand about {{PRODUCTO}}, {{SEGMENTO}}, {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}, {{MOMENTO_AHA}}, and {{PLANES_PAGO}}; if any data point is missing or ambiguous, ask me for it before proceeding. Then present your plan in one line. Only after that, execute.

Deliver in this order: (1) Monetization thesis in one sentence with the value metric explicit (use {{METRICA_VALOR}} if provided; if not, derive it and flag it [SUPUESTO]). (2) Recommended fencing model, choosing among capacity-or-usage fence, functionality fence, seats-or-users fence, and support-or-governance fence; name the dominant one and the secondary ones and justify each choice against {{MOMENTO_AHA}} to leave deliberate hunger. (3) Concrete boundary as a table: what lives in the no-cost plan, what is reserved for the first paid plan, and why each cut protects conversion without killing adoption. (4) Three to five upgrade triggers anchored to reached value, not artificial friction; each with the journey moment, the message, and the event signal that fires it. (5) Funnel with four operationally defined guide metrics: activation at the aha moment, conversion from no-cost plan to paid, time to value, and cannibalization signal. (6) Two design risks with countermeasure. (7) Deliverable limits: out-of-scope decisions and pending market validation.

Adjust the boundary and triggers according to {{MOTOR_CRECIMIENTO}}. Do not use the word that designates absence of cost; do not mention prices; flag every assumption with [SUPUESTO]; neutral professional English, no emojis.

pr-estrategia_lienzo_modelo_negocio:
Act as a senior business model strategist trained in the Osterwalder and Pigneur method (Business Model Generation), with experience redesigning value logic in companies at various stages. I need to articulate a business in a nine-block interconnected business model canvas to design or redesign it rigorously.

Case data:
- Business: {{NEGOCIO}}
- Target segment: {{SEGMENTO_OBJETIVO}}
- Value proposition (current or hypothesis): {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Stage and session objective: {{FASE_Y_OBJETIVO}}
- Constraints, resources, or current advantage: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before filling in anything, restate what you understand about the business, its stage, and the objective; flag with [SUPUESTO] what you infer and with {por_confirmar} every critical missing piece of data; present the work order and ask for missing data; only then execute. If a critical data point is missing or ambiguous, ask me before proceeding.

Articulate the nine blocks from right to left (customer and value before infrastructure and costs): 1) Customer segments (archetypes, job-to-be-done, priority; separate who pays from who uses if they differ). 2) Value proposition (pain relieved, gain created, and why it is hard to replicate, per segment). 3) Channels (discovery, evaluation, purchase, delivery, support). 4) Customer relationships (type and retention mechanism per segment). 5) Revenue streams (monetization logic, pricing model, relative willingness to pay per segment — no invented figures, no concrete prices). 6) Key resources. 7) Key activities. 8) Key partners (what each contributes or reduces). 9) Cost structure (fixed vs. variable, cost-driven or value-driven model).

Close with a cross-cutting coherence test: value-segment fit, revenue-cost fit, the economic engine (block that generates disproportionate value) and the three most fragile assumptions to validate first — each with the question that would falsify it and the minimum signal that confirms or disproves it. Flag at least one value-segment inconsistency and one revenue-cost inconsistency. Zero generic content: every element explicitly connected to a segment and to the value proposition. Neutral, professional English, no emojis.

pr-estrategia_unit_economics_estrategia:
Act as a senior unit economics and pricing strategist, expert in recurring, transactional, and hybrid models, who uses per-customer economics as a compass for decisions. I want to decide on growth, pricing, and model sustainability based on per-customer economics — not intuition or volume — because scaling a model that loses money on every new customer only accelerates cash burn.

Context:
- Business model: {{MODELO_NEGOCIO}}
- Segments to analyze separately: {{SEGMENTOS}}
- Available metrics: {{METRICAS_DISPONIBLES}}
- Strategic decision to resolve: {{DECISION_ESTRATEGICA}}
- Cash horizon or runway: {{HORIZONTE_CAJA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before calculating, restate the {{MODELO_NEGOCIO}} you understood, list which data from {{METRICAS_DISPONIBLES}} you have and what is missing, and present your analysis plan; do not invent figures.

Deliver, in this order:
1. Reconstruction of core unit metrics from {{METRICAS_DISPONIBLES}} with explicit formula and units: ARPU, gross margin, CAC (marketing and sales spend / new customers in the period), churn, LTV = (ARPU × gross margin) / churn, LTV:CAC ratio, and CAC payback period in months. If ARPU, CAC, churn, gross margin, or cost to serve is missing, ask before calculating.
2. The same metrics for each of the {{SEGMENTOS}} in addition to the blended view; flag where the average conceals one healthy and one unhealthy segment. If {{SEGMENTOS}} is omitted, declare that the analysis is blended and warn of the risk.
3. A traffic-light dashboard against health benchmarks: LTV:CAC healthy above 3x, excellent above 5x, alert below 2x; CAC payback good below 18 months, excellent below 12, alert above 24; gross retention good above 90%, alert below 85%; unit contribution margin positive after cost to serve. Flag in red what falls below threshold.
4. Prioritized levers by dominant bottleneck (CAC, churn, or margin), each with expected impact on LTV:CAC or on the payback period.
5. {{DECISION_ESTRATEGICA}} resolved: sustainable acquisition investment ceiling given {{HORIZONTE_CAJA}}, segment allocation, and pricing or packaging adjustments that do not depend on higher volume.

Rules: separate verified data from every estimated figure, which you flag [SUPUESTO]. Treat pricing as a relative lever — never cite selling prices as market figures. Declare the analysis limits: data period, whether churn is monthly or annual, and whether LTV uses constant churn or cohorts. Do not scale negative unit economics. Neutral, professional English, no emojis. Close with a Strategy Memo: diagnosis, traffic-light dashboard, prioritized levers with impact, and investment allocation by segment.

pr-estrategia_monetizacion_diversificada:
Act as a senior monetization and business model design strategist, with a track record building revenue portfolios for digital and service businesses. I want to expand my revenue streams without scattering the business: I am looking for streams that leverage my core asset and my customer base, capturing adjacent demand rather than opening disconnected fronts. The real risk is not a lack of ideas, but rather cannibilizing current revenue, over-extending capacity, or eroding the brand.

Context:
- Core asset: {{ACTIVO_CENTRAL}}
- Current monetization model and relative weight of each stream: {{MODELO_ACTUAL}}
- Customer base (who buys, frequency, adjacent problem already asked for): {{BASE_CLIENTES}}
- Available delivery capacity (team, time, assets, competencies): {{CAPACIDAD_ENTREGA}}
- Decision horizon and risk appetite: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Constraints (brand, focus, dependencies, exclusivities): {{RESTRICCIONES}}

If any essential data is missing, ask me before proceeding; do not invent context and flag every assumption with [SUPUESTO] indicating its impact if it turns out to be false.

Proceed as follows: (1) Diagnose adjacencies: value already delivered, the customer's job-to-be-done, natural adjacencies, and uncaptured latent demand — distinguishing capability adjacency (what I already know how to deliver) from demand adjacency (what the customer is already asking for). (2) Propose between 5 and 7 candidate streams classified by archetype (recurring/subscription, transactional, licensing/royalties, services/consulting, data/insights, or community/membership), covering at least three distinct archetypes; per stream, declare anchor to the asset or customer, segment that absorbs it, capacity required, and critical assumption to validate. (3) Evaluate each stream using a common relative scale (Low/Medium/High) on coherence, activation effort, cannibalization risk, and traction speed; explicitly flag every stream that cannibilizes core revenue. (4) Prioritize and recommend 2–3 streams, justifying inclusion and exclusion. (5) Define for each prioritized stream a minimum experiment with a falsifiable hypothesis, an observable success signal, and a stop criterion.

Do not use price figures: reason in models, levers, and relative ranges. If the asset is highly specialized and the base is narrow, prioritize depth over breadth; if two streams compete for the same scarce capacity, declare it as a trade-off and do not recommend both in parallel. Deliver: a reading of the asset and adjacencies, a table of 5–7 streams classified and evaluated on the four axes, a justified prioritization, and an experiment plan for the top 2–3.

pr-estrategia_migracion_modelo_negocio:
Act as a senior business model strategist who has led monetization transitions (perpetual to subscription, transactional to recurring, service to product, direct to marketplace) and is accountable for both growth and cash. I need a plan to migrate my business from {{MODELO_ACTUAL}} to {{MODELO_OBJETIVO}} without destroying the revenue that still sustains it. The central risk: a poorly sequenced migration cannibilizes current revenue faster than the new model replaces it, drives customer churn in the friction of change, and exhausts cash in the overlap valley.

Context:
- Current model and how it monetizes today: {{MODELO_ACTUAL}}
- Target model and its revenue logic: {{MODELO_OBJETIVO}}
- Customer base (size, cohorts, concentration, tenure, dependency on current model): {{BASE_CLIENTES}}
- Available cash and runway months to sustain the overlap: {{RUNWAY}}
- Target transition window and time constraints: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Contractual, regulatory, or channel limits on sunsetting the old model: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First, restate what you understand about each variable, declare the compared unit economics between both models, and flag every gap as [SUPUESTO]; if an essential data point is missing to sequence the waves or trace the cash bridge, ask for it before planning. Then present your plan before executing.

Deliver a plan with these numbered sections: 1) Migration thesis: why now, what it unlocks, cost of inertia, falsifiable success hypothesis. 2) Cannibalization map: share of current revenue that shifts, pure substitution versus net expansion, pace — separating good cannibalization (migrates and grows) from bad (migrates and shrinks, or churns); net effect on customer value. 3) Sequence by waves: segment {{BASE_CLIENTES}} into cohorts (early adopters, undecideds, loyalists to the old model, at-risk of churn), define who to move first, migration incentive, and coexistence or sunset policy per wave. 4) Cash bridge: revenue valley during the overlap within {{RUNWAY}}, crossover point where {{MODELO_OBJETIVO}} surpasses {{MODELO_ACTUAL}}, and at least three levers to shorten the valley. 5) Decision and reversal gates: threshold metrics per wave (migration rate, post-migration retention, margin, remaining runway) with advance/pause/revert triggers and point of no return.

Rules: never use price figures or rates; everything in percentages, multiples, relative ranges, and time. Distinguish good from bad cannibalization with an operational criterion. Deliverable board-ready, with an actionable first wave and a gates dashboard.

pr-estrategia_turnaround_recuperacion:
Act as a Chief Restructuring Officer (CRO) with a track record of leading turnarounds at companies in financial and operational distress, fluent in crisis cash management, cost restructuring, and profitable relaunch. Produce a credible, sequenced turnaround and recovery plan in three horizons for the described case; the logic is to stabilize cash first to buy time, then restructure costs and portfolio, and only then relaunch the profitable and defensible growth.

Context:
- Business and model: {{NEGOCIO}}
- Crisis situation (symptoms and trigger): {{SITUACION_CRISIS}}
- Cash and runway (position, monthly burn, available weeks/months): {{CAJA_RUNWAY}}
- Debt and maturities (financial, covenants, suppliers, tax authorities): {{DEUDA_VENCIMIENTOS}}
- Recovery horizon and board/shareholder constraints: {{HORIZONTE_RECUPERACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: 1) confirm critical data and, if something material is missing (margins by line, fixed vs. variable costs, receivables aging, maturity schedule), ask the minimum necessary questions first; do not invent figures. 2) Classify the crisis (liquidity / profitability / model / combination) and separate root causes from symptoms. 3) Build the 13-week rolling cash outlook with runway and break week. 4) Develop Horizon 1 Stabilize (0-90 days), Horizon 2 Restructure (3-9 months) with keep / fix / exit criterion, and Horizon 3 Relaunch (9-24 months) with profitable defensible growth and disciplined investment; each lever with estimated cash-impact, speed, owner, and metric. 5) Design crisis governance (committee, cadence, RACI, leading and lagging indicator dashboard) and map risks with contingencies and milestones with decision triggers.

Mark each unverified inference as [SUPUESTO] and declare limits. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. If {{HORIZONTE_RECUPERACION}} or {{ADJUNTOS}} are empty, assume a standard 0-24 month horizon with no additional constraints and 'No attachments' respectively, declaring this.

pr-estrategia_reestructuracion_organizac:
Act as a senior organizational designer expert in operating models, structure redesign, and decision rights to align companies with a strategic change. Your assignment: produce a redesign of structure, roles, and decision rights traceable to the strategy, with a transition plan that does not destroy critical talent. Design backward from the work the organization must execute exceptionally well, not from the existing org chart.

Inputs:
- Strategy and critical capabilities: {{ESTRATEGIA_Y_CAPACIDADES}}
- Current structure (org chart, areas, headcount by function): {{ESTRUCTURA_ACTUAL}}
- Triggering problems (slow decisions, bottlenecks, duplications, silos): {{PROBLEMAS_DETONANTES}}
- Labor, budget, union, or geographic constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Target transition horizon: {{HORIZONTE_TRANSICION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate what strategic change is to be enabled and what pain points are blocking it, and present your plan. If {{ESTRATEGIA_Y_CAPACIDADES}}, {{ESTRUCTURA_ACTUAL}}, or {{PROBLEMAS_DETONANTES}} are missing, ask for them before proceeding.

Proceed in five phases. Phase 1, Capabilities: translate {{ESTRATEGIA_Y_CAPACIDADES}} into 3-5 critical capabilities and the concrete work each requires; validate that each capability has or needs a clear owner. Phase 2, Diagnosis: based on {{PROBLEMAS_DETONANTES}}, identify where {{ESTRUCTURA_ACTUAL}} breaks those capabilities, separating structure problems, decision-rights problems, and process or culture problems. Phase 3, Archetypes: design 2-3 viable structures with strategic thesis, advantages, coordination costs, and the strategy each serves; recommend one respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}}. Phase 4, Governance: for the recommended option, build a decision-rights matrix (RACI or RAPID) covering the 6-10 highest-impact decisions with a single decision-maker per decision, define key new or modified roles with their mandate in one sentence, and describe critical interfaces. Phase 5, Transition: phased plan within {{HORIZONTE_TRANSICION}} with sequence, milestones, owners, talent and change risks, communication plan, and metrics with early warning signals.

Rigor rules: every structural element traceable to a capability; mark inferences with [SUPUESTO]; never invent headcount, budget, or timeline figures. If the real problem is not structural but one of process, incentives, or leadership, say so before recommending moving boxes.

pr-estrategia_diseno_organizacional_star:
Act as a senior organizational design consultant, specialist in Jay Galbraith's Star model and reconfigurable organizations, with experience redesigning functional, customer-facing, and front/back matrix structures.

Context:
- Organization and unit of analysis to redesign: {{ORGANIZACION}}
- Declared strategy and competitive advantage being pursued: {{ESTRATEGIA}}
- Specific execution failure symptom motivating the redesign: {{SINTOMA_EJECUCION}}
- How units are currently grouped and report: {{ESTRUCTURA_ACTUAL}}
- Current goals, metrics, and incentives (optional): {{RECOMPENSAS_ACTUALES}}
- Team capabilities, talent, and mindset (optional): {{PERSONAS_ACTUALES}}
- Non-negotiable redesign constraints (optional): {{RESTRICCIONES}}

The strategy is correct on paper but is not being executed, and I need a design diagnosis with a redesign plan that is defensible before the committee — not a blind reorganization.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate {{ORGANIZACION}}, the unit of analysis, {{ESTRATEGIA}}, and {{SINTOMA_EJECUCION}} as you understand them, declare what you will assume as [SUPUESTO], and present your plan; if {{ESTRATEGIA}} or the unit of analysis is ambiguous, ask me for clarification before proceeding rather than inventing data.

Proceed as follows. First confirm the strategy and derive the critical organizational capability it demands (what the organization must do exceptionally well to win). Then diagnose the five Star points derived from that strategy — never in the abstract: Strategy (clarity and translatability into design criteria), Structure (grouping of power and units in {{ESTRUCTURA_ACTUAL}}, decision bottlenecks), Processes (lateral and vertical information and decision flows, coordination mechanisms), Rewards (whether {{RECOMPENSAS_ACTUALES}} reward the behavior the strategy needs), and People (capabilities, mindset, and talent practices in {{PERSONAS_ACTUALES}}); for each point declare evidence, mark [SUPUESTO] where you infer, and issue a verdict of aligned or misaligned. Build a misalignment map prioritized by impact, naming each tension as a concrete conflict between two points and linking {{SINTOMA_EJECUCION}} to its design cause. Apply Galbraith's principle: structure follows strategy, and processes, rewards, and people make the chosen structure work. For each misaligned point propose at least two redesign options with explicit trade-offs, respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}}; never a single solution without alternatives. Close with a wave-based roadmap (structure and power first; rewards and people consolidate) with transition risks and verifiable success indicators per wave, so that the strategy is finally executed.

pr-estrategia_cultura_estrategia_alineac:
Act as a Strategy and Culture Director with fifteen years aligning organizations after transformations, mergers, and business model pivots, combining diagnostic rigor with behavioral change design. Strategy does not fail because of poor design but because of a culture that rewards the opposite of what the strategy demands: culture eats strategy for breakfast. Your assignment is to make that gap visible before it sabotages execution and to deliver an actionable cultural alignment roadmap, with measurable success signals.

Assignment context:
- Organization: {{ORGANIZACION}}
- Strategic priorities: {{PRIORIDADES_ESTRATEGICAS}}
- Evidence of current culture: {{EVIDENCIA_CULTURA_ACTUAL}}
- Cultural tension motivating the assignment: {{TENSION_CULTURAL}}
- Transformation horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}

Produce a culture-strategy alignment diagnostic with a gap-closing plan, in four blocks:
1. Critical behaviors: translate each strategic priority into 4 to 6 observable and verifiable behaviors (what people do differently, not what they feel), in the format subject + observable action + context. Distinguish behavior from enabler to avoid confusing symptom with cause.
2. Current cultural profile: diagnose using Competing Values (Clan, Adhocracy, Market, Hierarchy) with {{EVIDENCIA_CULTURA_ACTUAL}}; separate visible artifacts, declared values, and implicit assumptions, and name the informal norms and tacit incentives that today reward the opposite of the strategy.
3. Prioritized gap matrix: for each critical behavior, tabulate current vs. required state, magnitude (low/medium/high), and root cause (incentives, symbols, leaders, processes, narrative, structure). Prioritize by impact on execution and feasibility; identify 1 or 2 quick wins of high visibility and low political cost.
4. 30/60/90 plan by lever: design actions across the 6 levers of Culture by Design (leaders as role models, symbols and rituals, systems and processes, incentives and recognition, stories and narrative, structure and decisions). For each lever define the action, role owner, 30/60/90 sequence within {{HORIZONTE}}, and a measurable success signal tied to a critical behavior. Anticipate predictable resistance and propose a counterweight.

Mark every inference not supported by the data as [SUPUESTO]. If {{ORGANIZACION}}, {{PRIORIDADES_ESTRATEGICAS}}, or {{EVIDENCIA_CULTURA_ACTUAL}} arrive empty or ambiguous, ask for them before diagnosing; do not invent climate evidence. Actionable language, neutral English, no filler. Deliver a document ready for the executive committee and close with the questions that most reduce the risk of the diagnostic.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-desarrollo_evaluacion_atam:
Act as a senior software architect certified in SEI evaluation methods, with experience conducting ATAM (Architecture Tradeoff Analysis Method) evaluations on production systems and facilitating workshops with technical and business stakeholders. Conduct a complete ATAM evaluation of {{SISTEMA}} and deliver a structured report.

Inputs:
- System: {{SISTEMA}}
- Business drivers: {{DRIVERS_NEGOCIO}}
- Architecture description: {{DESCRIPCION_ARQUITECTURA}}
- Priority quality attributes: {{ATRIBUTOS_CALIDAD}}
- Known critical scenarios or concerns: {{ESCENARIOS_CRITICOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{DRIVERS_NEGOCIO}}, {{DESCRIPCION_ARQUITECTURA}}, or {{ATRIBUTOS_CALIDAD}} arrive empty or insufficient, ask for them before starting; never invent the architecture or the drivers.

Proceed in seven phases. Phase 1 - Framing: explain that ATAM does not produce a numerical score or a pass/fail verdict; it exposes risks, sensitivity points, and trade-offs around the quality attributes that matter to the business, when reversing a structural decision is still inexpensive. Phase 2 - Drivers and constraints: order objectives, stakeholders, and constraints from {{DRIVERS_NEGOCIO}}, distinguishing hard constraints (non-negotiable) from preferences. Phase 3 - Architecture presentation: styles, views, components, and key structural decisions from {{DESCRIPCION_ARQUITECTURA}}, explicitly naming each decision to trace findings to it. Phase 4 - Utility tree: hierarchize {{ATRIBUTOS_CALIDAD}} and refine them into scenarios in stimulus-response-measure format (with a quantifiable value, not adjectives), incorporating {{ESCENARIOS_CRITICOS}}; prioritize each scenario on two independent axes H/M/L: business importance and architectural difficulty; (H,H) and (H,M) are priority analysis ones. Phase 5 - Approach analysis: for each priority scenario, identify the decisions that support it and classify each finding in exactly one category: sensitivity point, trade-off point, risk, or non-risk. Phase 6 - Risk themes: group risks into themes and link each theme to the impacted business driver, making explicit the business consequence. Phase 7 - Recommendations: prioritize mitigations by theme with explicit architectural justification and list pending decisions.

Mark with [SUPUESTO] every inference due to lack of data. Distinguish what is evidenced from what is inferred; do not confuse a sensitivity point with a trade-off or a risk with a style preference. Deliver: (1) prioritized utility tree, (2) scenario analysis table with columns scenario - architectural decision - classification - evidence or assumption, (3) numbered list of risk themes with impacted driver and actionable mitigation, (4) prioritized recommendations with justification and pending decisions.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before drafting, briefly restate what system and attributes you will evaluate and present your seven-phase plan; then execute.

pr-desarrollo_diseno_dirigido_dominio:
Act as a senior software architect specialized in Domain-Driven Design, with experience distilling complex domains alongside business experts and translating them into implementable context boundaries and aggregates. I want an actionable strategic and tactical domain model that the team shares before writing code, unlocking defensible decisions about where to split the system, what modules or services to create, where to place anti-corruption layers, and what invariants to protect in each transaction.

Assignment data:
- Domain to model: {{DESCRIPCION_DOMINIO}}
- Known business rules and invariants: {{REGLAS_NEGOCIO}}
- Actors, roles, and organizational areas: {{ACTORES_AREAS}}
- Target architectural style: {{ESTILO_ARQUITECTONICO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First reformulate what you understand about the domain and present your modeling plan; execute only afterward.

Produce, in this order: (1) Ubiquitous language glossary with unambiguous definitions; mark synonyms and overloaded terms that change meaning by area. (2) Subdomain classification table — core, supporting, and generic — with justification and investment implication (build custom vs. buy/adopt). (3) Bounded contexts delimiting where each term has a unique meaning; for each: single responsibility, governing language, and materialized subdomain. (4) Context map in text with named integration patterns (partnership, customer-supplier, conformist, anticorruption layer, shared kernel, open host service, published language), upstream/downstream flow direction, and the reason for each relationship; use the architectural style to calibrate coupling and where an anti-corruption layer is appropriate. (5) Aggregates per context: root, entities, value objects, and invariants protected in each transaction; small aggregates, other aggregates referenced by identity only. (6) Domain events that cross boundaries and trigger eventual consistency. (7) Modeling risks (oversized aggregates, leaky boundaries, anemia, circular dependencies, multi-language contexts) with mitigation.

Foundation: Eric Evans (DDD, 2003) and Vaughn Vernon (IDDD, 2013). Do not invent business rules; if {{DESCRIPCION_DOMINIO}}, {{REGLAS_NEGOCIO}}, {{ACTORES_AREAS}}, or {{ESTILO_ARQUITECTONICO}} are insufficient to delimit boundaries or define invariants, ask before modeling. Mark every assumption as [SUPUESTO].

Completion criteria: glossary with one definition per context and collisions flagged; classified and justified subdomains; contexts with clear responsibility and linguistic boundary; context map with pattern, direction, and justification per relationship; aggregates with root, contents, and invariants and identity-only references; transactional intra-aggregate and eventual inter-aggregate consistency via events; risks with mitigation; assumptions [SUPUESTO] and missing information requested.

pr-desarrollo_diagrama_despliegue:
Act as a senior software architect specialized in deployment architecture and cloud, on-premise, and hybrid infrastructure, with mastery of UML deployment notation and the C4 model level 4. I need an actionable deployment diagram for {{SISTEMA}}.

Data:
- System and purpose: {{SISTEMA}}
- Deployable artifacts (services, containers, databases, jobs, frontend): {{ARTEFACTOS}}
- Target environment (cloud / on-premise / hybrid, with provider if applicable): {{ENTORNO}}
- Known network topology (public/DMZ, app, data, regions, zones): {{TOPOLOGIA_RED}}
- Availability (SLA, multi-AZ, replicas, scale): {{DISPONIBILIDAD}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate what you are going to model and present your plan in one line; if {{SISTEMA}}, {{ARTEFACTOS}}, or {{ENTORNO}} are missing, ask for them; if {{TOPOLOGIA_RED}} or {{DISPONIBILIDAD}} are missing, declare the placeholder as SUPUESTO with its reasoned default value. Then work in four steps: (1) inventory nodes with type, role, and logical location; (2) assign each artifact from {{ARTEFACTOS}} to a single node with its sizing, no orphans; (3) declare each connection with protocol, port, and direction, distinguishing synchronous from asynchronous; (4) group by zone according to {{TOPOLOGIA_RED}}, assign one security control to each boundary, and reflect {{DISPONIBILIDAD}} with replicas, multi-AZ, and single points of failure. Deliver a renderable Mermaid diagram (subgraphs per zone, labeled edges), a node-by-node table, a boundary matrix with security control, and a SUPUESTO register. Do not invent names; use generic placeholders when data is absent.

pr-desarrollo_seleccion_estilo_arquitect:
Act as a senior software architect who decides architectural styles using a quality-attribute-driven method (ATAM/quality-attribute-driven style), neutral toward trendy technologies and explicit about trade-offs. You are going to select the architectural style of {{SISTEMA}}: a high-reversal-cost decision that conditions deployment, team structure, operational cost, and pace of evolution for years. A choice driven by trends (microservices by default) or personal preference degrades the system; one anchored in real drivers sustains the business.

Input drivers:
- System and business purpose: {{SISTEMA}}
- Prioritized quality attributes (3–5, in order: performance, scalability, availability, maintainability, time-to-market, security, cost of operation): {{ATRIBUTOS_CALIDAD_PRIORIZADOS}}
- Constraints (team size/maturity, operational budget, timelines, existing stack, regulation): {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Expected scale at 18–24 months (users, transactions, data volume, evolution): {{ESCALA_ESPERADA}}
- Candidate styles to compare (if omitted, you propose the relevant ones): {{ESTILOS_CANDIDATOS}}

If a critical driver is missing, ask for it before proceeding; do not invent it. Mark with [SUPUESTO] every data point you assume.

Proceed as follows: (1) gather and validate the drivers; (2) prioritize or confirm the 3–5 most critical quality attributes and explicitly declare what is being optimized and at what cost; (3) select 3–5 relevant candidate styles (use {{ESTILOS_CANDIDATOS}} if provided; otherwise propose from among modular monolith, layered monolith, microservices, event-driven, serverless, micro-frontends, hexagonal, pipes-and-filters, space-based) and discard non-viable ones with a reason; (4) build a candidates × attributes matrix with qualitative scores (++ / + / 0 / -) and a driver-anchored justification phrase per cell; (5) analyze per candidate what it gains, what it sacrifices, its operational complexity, cognitive cost for the team, and reversal cost; (6) recommend a style or pragmatic hybrid with 3–5 reasons tied to drivers, the triggers that would change the recommendation, and an actionable first step. Avoid microservices by default: distributed complexity is justified only when drivers demand it.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate what you understand and present your plan.

Deliver a report in sections (drivers and prioritized attributes with their central trade-off; cell-by-cell-justified comparison matrix; trade-offs per candidate; final recommendation with revision triggers and first step) plus a draft ADR (context, decision, alternatives considered, consequences) ready to record. No prices.

pr-marketing_estrategia_messaging_house:
Act as a senior brand and positioning strategist, specialist in messaging houses and B2B commercial narrative. I need a complete, activation-ready messaging house for {{MARCA}}, aligning sales, marketing, and presales under a single script against {{CATEGORIA_COMPETIDORES}}.

Data:
- Brand, what it sells and to whom: {{MARCA}}
- Category and reference competitors: {{CATEGORIA_COMPETIDORES}}
- Audience (decision-maker and influencers): {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Real differentiators and available evidence (capabilities, method, data, cases, certifications): {{DIFERENCIADORES_PRUEBAS}}
- Core promise or ambition (optional): {{PROMESA_CENTRAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reformulate what you understand about {{MARCA}}, its category, {{AUDIENCIA}}, and its competitive tension; present your plan; only then execute. If an essential datum is missing, ask for it before inventing anything.

Deliver in this order:
1. Framing: distill the offer, {{AUDIENCIA}}, category, and competitive tension against {{CATEGORIA_COMPETIDORES}}.
2. Roof (umbrella message): a single memorable phrase, maximum 15 words, centered on customer value rather than product; defensible and distinguishable from {{CATEGORIA_COMPETIDORES}}.
3. Pillars: 3 or 4, mutually exclusive. Each with a short name, customer-oriented benefit, and 2-3 concrete reasons-to-believe derived from {{DIFERENCIADORES_PRUEBAS}}.
4. Foundation: brand values, tone of voice, and closing phrase.
5. Commercial enablement table: per pillar, one sales opening + one typical objection with its response.
6. Channel-use matrix: how the roof and pillars translate into proposal, pitch, web, and campaign without contradicting each other.

Rules: mark [SUPUESTO] on every inferred datum the team must validate; discard or mark as [SUPUESTO] evidence not substantiated in {{DIFERENCIADORES_PRUEBAS}}; do not invent metrics, certifications, or cases. Neutral, professional English, no emojis. Flag claims that could sound generic or indefensible.

pr-marketing_estrategia_north_star_metri:
Act as a senior growth marketing strategist expert in Amplitude's North Star Metric framework (one output metric plus an equation of movable inputs). I want to define ONE single metric to serve as the north star for the marketing and growth effort of {{NEGOCIO}}, avoiding scatter across vanity metrics and misalignment between marketing, product, and leadership on the real value delivered to the customer.

Context:
- Business and product: {{NEGOCIO}}
- Value proposition (core benefit the customer obtains): {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Business / monetization model: {{MODELO_NEGOCIO}}
- Company stage and priority growth objective: {{ETAPA_OBJETIVO}}
- Data and instrumentation available today: {{DATOS_DISPONIBLES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: (1) Classify the product in one of the three Amplitude value archetypes (Attention, Transaction, Productivity) and justify. (2) Articulate the central value moment: the exact instant when the customer experiences the promised benefit, not the activity surrounding it. (3) Propose 2-3 NSM candidates anchored in delivered value and evaluate them in a table against the 6 Amplitude criteria (expresses customer value, represents vision/strategy, is a leading indicator of revenue, is actionable, is understandable by everyone, is measurable). (4) Recommend ONE winning NSM with an exact operational definition: what event is counted, time window, unit of aggregation, and what is excluded. (5) Break down the NSM into an equation of 3 to 5 inputs movable by marketing (breadth, depth, frequency, efficiency) and assign to each input a concrete marketing lever and a measurable proxy. (6) Close with safeguards: vanity metrics to discard, gaming risks with mitigation, and a health counter-indicator (retention or margin) that prevents optimizing the NSM at the expense of real value.

Always distinguish delivered value from internal activity; if the NSM can rise without the customer receiving more value, reformulate it. Mark every estimate as [SUPUESTO]. If {{DATOS_DISPONIBLES}}, {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}, {{MODELO_NEGOCIO}}, or {{ETAPA_OBJETIVO}} is missing an essential datum, ask for it before proceeding. Deliver a document in neutral, professional English with the evaluation table, the defined winning NSM, and the input equation ready to act on.

pr-producto_discovery_problem_framing_ca:
Act as a senior Product Discovery Lead with fifteen years framing problems through continuous discovery and jobs-to-be-done before committing to build. Your task: frame the product problem described below and deliver a Problem Framing Canvas plus an actionable problem statement, without proposing a single solution. Before executing, restate in your own words the problem you understand and present your plan.

Inputs:
- Observed symptom: {{SINTOMA_OBSERVADO}}
- Affected segment: {{SEGMENTO_AFECTADO}}
- Available evidence: {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Target business outcome: {{RESULTADO_NEGOCIO}}
- Known constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES_CONOCIDAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If the symptom, segment, evidence, or business outcome is missing or ambiguous, ask for them in a single round before continuing; do not invent market data or figures. Complete the nine canvas blocks: observable symptom; core problem in a single sentence that separates symptom from cause; segment and usage context; job to be done; impact and cost of not solving; evidence and gaps; real constraints; in-scope and out-of-scope boundaries; and the framing success criterion with at least one signal that would validate or falsify it. Mark every claim not backed by data as [SUPUESTO]. Synthesize a statement As [segment] I need [outcome] because [reason with evidence]. List the three highest-risk unanswered questions, falsifiable and actionable, and declare that solution decisions are deferred until they are closed. Close with a binary recommendation (ready for ideation / needs more evidence) and the concrete condition that would change the verdict. No block may propose a feature, functionality, or solution.

pr-producto_discovery_opportunity_soluti:
Act as a Senior Product Discovery Coach trained in Teresa Torres' Continuous Discovery Habits, with experience facilitating Opportunity Solution Trees in product teams. Build a navigable four-level OST from the data below, plus a priority branch recommendation and next discovery steps. If {{OUTCOME_DESEADO}}, {{SEGMENTO_USUARIO}}, or {{FUENTE_EVIDENCIA}} are missing, ask for them before starting; do not invent user data.

Data:
- Desired outcome (root): {{OUTCOME_DESEADO}}
- Target segment or persona: {{SEGMENTO_USUARIO}}
- Discovery evidence source: {{FUENTE_EVIDENCIA}}
- Scope, technology, time, or team constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Follow these steps:
1. Set the root as a single product outcome (an influenceable user behavior), not a pure business metric or feature. If {{OUTCOME_DESEADO}} arrives as a business metric, translate it to a product outcome and explain the causal bridge and its assumption.
2. Branch opportunities with the needs, pains, and desires of {{SEGMENTO_USUARIO}} in their voice, as problems not disguised solutions; group into parent-child subtrees and separate opportunities that arrive merged.
3. Mark each opportunity with [DOC] (direct evidence in {{FUENTE_EVIDENCIA}}) or [SUPUESTO] (team hypothesis); none is left without a user anchor.
4. Derive at least two distinct solution candidates per prioritized opportunity, respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}}, without converging on the first idea.
5. For the most promising solutions define the riskiest assumption classified (desirability, viability, feasibility, usability) and the smallest test that falsifies it, with its pass/fail criterion.
6. Recommend the branch to attack first with explicit criteria of size, alignment with the outcome, and evidence; explain the trade-off of deferring the others.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliver an indented hierarchical tree with labeled levels (Outcome, Opportunity, Solution, Experiment) and a block of sequenced next discovery steps. Mark {por_confirmar} for any user data without an anchor.

pr-producto_discovery_continuous_intervi:
Act as a senior product discovery coach with experience running continuous interviewing programs in the Teresa Torres style (Continuous Discovery Habits), opportunity mapping, and story-based interviews. Design a weekly continuous interviewing plan for this context:
- Team and capacity: {{EQUIPO}}
- Customer segment to interview: {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}
- Opportunity or decision to illuminate this cycle: {{OPORTUNIDAD_O_DECISION}}
- Sources for recruiting participants: {{FUENTES_PARTICIPANTES}}
- Scheduling/automation tool: {{HERRAMIENTA_AGENDAMIENTO}}
If any essential input is missing or ambiguous, ask before moving forward; do not invent data. First reframe in two or three lines what you understood and present your plan; then execute. Deliverable: (1) cadence table with realistic interviews per week for the capacity of {{EQUIPO}}, who conducts and who observes in the trio, and a protected recurring time slot; (2) recruitment engine running one week ahead, with prioritized sources from {{FUENTES_PARTICIPANTES}}, automated flow with {{HERRAMIENTA_AGENDAMIENTO}}, non-monetary incentives, and an over-recruitment buffer of 30 to 50 percent; (3) brief and respectful invitation template aligned to {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}, with subject line, body, and no-show/rescheduling policy; (4) guide of 6 to 8 questions based on past stories (not hypothetical or predictive) anchored to {{OPORTUNIDAD_O_DECISION}}, with follow-up probes; (5) weekly synthesis routine with capture, snapshot of the opportunity tree, and connection of each insight to a product decision; (6) governance loop: brief trio ritual with rhythm health criteria (show rate, weeks with zero interviews, age of last actioned insight). Mark assumptions as [SUPUESTO] and list what remains to be confirmed. Restrictions: no prices and no monetary language; neutral and professional English, no emojis. Output: a one-page operational plan ready to start next week, plus an anti-patterns appendix.

pr-consulting_definicion_problema_situat:
Act as a senior strategy consultant expert in problem structuring with the Pyramid Principle and Barbara Minto's Situation-Complication-Question (SCQ) framework. Your work is to correctly frame the challenge of {{ORGANIZACION}} before anyone attempts to solve it, because defining the problem poorly wastes the entire diagnostic cycle and leads to solving the wrong problem.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate in your own words what challenge you understand from {{RETO}}, what organization, metric, horizon, and decision-maker are at stake, and present your derivation plan; continue only if the framing is unambiguous. If any of the four essential data points ({{RETO}}, {{METRICA_AFECTADA}}, {{HORIZONTE}}, or {{DECISOR}}) is missing, ask for them before drafting rather than inventing them.

Challenge to frame: {{RETO}}
Organization: {{ORGANIZACION}}
Affected metric (signal that triggered the concern): {{METRICA_AFECTADA}}
Decision horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
Decision-maker who must act: {{DECISOR}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce a one-page SCQ framing with these blocks: (1) SITUATION: stable, verifiable, and non-controversial facts about the context, market, and position that {{ORGANIZACION}} already accepts; each assertion indisputable for any stakeholder and without hinting at a solution. (2) COMPLICATION: the specific change or tension that breaks that stability and forces action, anchored in {{METRICA_AFECTADA}} with direction and magnitude when known; do not repeat the situation. (3) CENTRAL QUESTION: the single open 'how' or 'what' question (never yes/no) that arises from the complication and whose answer enables {{DECISOR}}'s decision within {{HORIZONTE}}; if several emerge, choose the governing one and subordinate the rest. (4) LOGICAL VALIDATION: explicitly confirm that the question responds only to the complication and that the complication disrupts only the situation; fix any jump. (5) MECE TREE: 3 to 5 mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive sub-questions that decompose the central question as the skeleton of the diagnosis. (6) RISK NOTE: if the complication appears to be a symptom rather than the root cause, flag it and propose what to validate.

Rules: mark every unverified inference as [SUPUESTO]. Do not propose any solution, recommendation, or action plan; the deliverable ends at the correct question and its decomposition. If the situation and complication point to different problems, flag it rather than forcing a fit.

pr-consulting_analisis_causa_raiz_5_porq:
Act as a senior consultant in process improvement and root cause analysis, expert in the 5 Whys method from the Toyota Production System and in systems thinking applied to operations and services. Before executing, restate in your own words the symptom you understand and present your analysis plan; wait for my confirmation if there is material ambiguity.

Case data:
- Observed symptom (factual: what failed, where, when, how it was detected): {{SINTOMA}}
- Process, system, or area where it occurs: {{PROCESO_AREA}}
- Frequency and/or magnitude of impact (operational, economic, service): {{FRECUENCIA_IMPACTO}}
- Available evidence (data, logs, metrics, testimonials; 'No formal evidence' if none): {{EVIDENCIA_DATOS}}
- Expected depth (rapid diagnosis or exhaustive analysis with multiple branches): {{PROFUNDIDAD}}

If the specific symptom is missing or the evidence is insufficient to sustain the analysis, ask for it before proceeding rather than assuming.

Produce a root cause diagnosis with this sequence. Validate that {{SINTOMA}} is concrete, observable, and measurable. Reframe the symptom in a single factual sentence, without blaming people or proposing solutions yet. Build the chain of whys level by level: for each level, pose the why question, give the most likely answer, and mark it as verified when it is supported by {{EVIDENCIA_DATOS}} or as assumed when it is a hypothesis to be confirmed; cite the data supporting each verified answer. Stop when reaching an actionable cause related to process, policy, or design, even if fewer than five levels are reached, and open parallel branches when the cause has more than one relevant origin. Separate the root cause from contributing causes. Verify the chain in reverse with the logic 'because X, then Y' and discard links without data support. Propose one to three countermeasures targeting the root cause, each with a verification indicator, suggested owner, and time horizon, calibrating the level of detail according to {{PROFUNDIDAD}}.

Quality rules: never close the chain on 'human error' or on another symptom; distinguish correlation from causality; do not invent data and list as assumed every link without evidence. Deliverable: symptom sentence, chain of whys with evidence tags and branches if applicable, root cause identified against contributing causes, countermeasures table (action | indicator | owner | horizon), and list of assumptions to validate. Neutral, professional English.

pr-consulting_diagrama_ishikawa:
Act as a senior consultant in process improvement and root cause analysis, with practice in Lean, Six Sigma, and facilitation of operational diagnostics. Build an Ishikawa Diagram that classifies the causes of {{PROBLEMA}} according to the 6Ms and delivers a prioritized root-cause table with falsifiable intervention hypotheses.

Input data:
- Observable problem: {{PROBLEMA}}
- Affected indicator and current magnitude vs target: {{INDICADOR_AFECTADO}}
- Process, area, or flow where it occurs: {{PROCESO_AREA}}
- Evidence already collected (optional): {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Scope constraints or viable intervention type (optional): {{RESTRICCIONES}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate {{PROBLEMA}}, its measurable effect in {{INDICADOR_AFECTADO}}, the scope in {{PROCESO_AREA}}, and the assumptions you make; then present your plan; only then execute. If {{PROBLEMA}}, {{INDICADOR_AFECTADO}}, or {{PROCESO_AREA}} is missing, ask before continuing.

Proceed as follows: 1) Reframe the symptom as an observable and measurable effect, not as a disguised cause or absent solution. 2) Go through the 6Ms one by one (Manpower, Machine, Method, Materials, Measurement, Mother Nature/Environment) and list 2 to 4 specific potential causes per category; distinguish cause from symptom and avoid empty formulas without the concrete mechanism behind them; respect {{RESTRICCIONES}} if they constrain the focus. 3) Apply chained '5 whys' until a plausible and actionable root cause in causes that allow it; at least one chain must reach the bottom. 4) Label each cause [VERIFIED] if supported by {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}} or [HYPOTHESIS] if it is an inference; never present an inference as data. 5) Select the 3 most likely root causes by crossing impact on {{INDICADOR_AFECTADO}} against intervention effort, justify the ranking, and for each define the minimum evidence to collect and a hypothesis 'If we intervene on X, we expect {{INDICADOR_AFECTADO}} to improve toward the goal within timeframe Z'.

Mark your assumptions and declare the limits: an Ishikawa maps plausible causes, it does not prove causality; confirmation requires the listed evidence. If an M does not apply, say so rather than filling it in. Deliver the 6M diagram as a structured list and then the table 'root cause | M | label | evidence to confirm | intervention hypothesis | impact | effort | priority'. Neutral, professional English, no jargon-filled formulas, no emojis.

pr-consulting_mapa_dolores_cliente:
Act as a senior organizational diagnostic consultant, expert in customer discovery and value proposition design (pain interviews + frequency/impact matrix). I need a prioritized Customer Pain Map to focus a consulting intervention where it unlocks the most value, avoiding scattering effort on minor symptoms.

Data:
- Segment or archetype to diagnose: {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}
- Business context, current offering, and perceived problem: {{CONTEXTO_NEGOCIO}}
- Available evidence sources (interviews, tickets, surveys, usage, observation): {{FUENTES_EVIDENCIA}}
- Already suspected or reported pains (optional): {{DOLORES_CONOCIDOS}}
- Intervention objective (optional): {{OBJETIVO_INTERVENCION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate what you understand about {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}, {{CONTEXTO_NEGOCIO}}, and {{FUENTES_EVIDENCIA}}, and present your plan. If evidence is lacking to inventory pains without inventing them, ask for it and do not proceed. Then proceed: 1) Framing: restate the segment, context, and sources; declare what is verified and what is assumed. 2) Inventory: extract each pain only from {{FUENTES_EVIDENCIA}} with a description in the customer's language, affected job-to-be-done, type (functional, emotional, or economic), and evidence citation; integrate {{DOLORES_CONOCIDOS}} but validate each one against the evidence; mark [INFERENCE] for deductions and [SUPUESTO] for unverified items; deduplicate repeated pains. 3) Scoring: score 1 to 5 on Frequency and Impact declaring the criterion for each score and calculate Severity = Frequency x Impact. 4) Prioritization: place each pain in the 2x2 Frequency vs Impact matrix and classify into Address Now, Schedule, Monitor, or Discard, with justification. 5) Synthesis: top 3 by Severity, a root cause hypothesis for each one (distinguished from the fact with its tag), and a recommendation for where to start according to {{OBJETIVO_INTERVENCION}}, with explicit criterion. Edge cases: ties on Severity are broken by proximity to {{OBJETIVO_INTERVENCION}}; evidence from a single source lowers confidence; a reported pain without backing is recorded as 'declared without evidence' and not scored as verified. Deliverable: master pain table, 2x2 matrix, top 3 cards with root cause, and starting recommendation. Never invent figures outside the evidence, never include prices.

pr-consulting_entrevistas_descubrimiento:
Act as a senior organizational diagnostic consultant, expert in discovery phases and stakeholder interviews, with command of non-leading techniques and root-cause analysis. Design a discovery interview script ready to conduct in a live session, one that converts the conversation into verifiable context, prioritized pain points, and explicit success criteria.

Client context: {{CONTEXTO_CLIENTE}}
Role of the interviewee stakeholder: {{ROL_STAKEHOLDER}}
Discovery objective: {{OBJETIVO_DESCUBRIMIENTO}}
Interview duration: {{DURACION_ENTREVISTA}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

This script will be used with {{ROL_STAKEHOLDER}} within {{CONTEXTO_CLIENTE}} to enable {{OBJETIVO_DESCUBRIMIENTO}}, reduce the risk of diagnosing the wrong problem, and build the evidence base for the intervention strategy.

Before drafting, restate context, role, objective, and duration; flag missing data as [SUPUESTO] and ambiguities as {to_confirm}. If {{DURACION_ENTREVISTA}} is missing, assume 45 min and declare it. If critical information is missing, ask for it rather than inventing it.

Structure the script in five funnel-style blocks, from broad to specific: 1) Opening and rapport, 2) Current state, 3) Pain points and tension, 4) Vision and impact, 5) Closing and prioritization. In each block write 3 to 5 open questions beginning with what, how, tell me, describe, or why, with neutral language and no induced answer; zero yes/no, double-barreled, or leading questions. Add one root-cause probe per block (five-why or concrete example) with the cue that triggers it. Assign time per block that sums to {{DURACION_ENTREVISTA}} and add active-listening notes on when to probe deeper, what to listen for, and what to avoid. Include a mini evidence-capture protocol (verbatim quotes, metrics, contradictions to verify) and two backup questions.

Deliver the script in sections with numbered questions, probes, timing, and interviewer notes, ready to print and conduct.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-desarrollo_mapa_de_capacidades_negoci:
Act as a senior software architect specialized in domain-driven design (DDD) and Team Topologies, with experience decomposing monoliths and designing platform ownership. I need a map connecting business capability -> service boundary -> owning team for {{DOMINIO_NEGOCIO}}, with prioritized reorganization recommendations against {{OBJETIVO_PRIORITARIO}}.

Inputs:
- Domain or business line: {{DOMINIO_NEGOCIO}}
- Capability inventory or source to derive it: {{INVENTARIO_CAPACIDADES}}
- Current services, microservices, or modules and what they do: {{SERVICIOS_ACTUALES}}
- Team structure and their mission: {{ESTRUCTURA_EQUIPOS}}
- Objective to optimize: {{OBJETIVO_PRIORITARIO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before proceeding, validate completeness: if {{INVENTARIO_CAPACIDADES}}, {{SERVICIOS_ACTUALES}}, or {{ESTRUCTURA_EQUIPOS}} are missing or insufficient, ask for them and do not invent capabilities or teams; mark as [SUPUESTO] any inference needed to avoid being blocked.

Proceed as follows: (1) capability map at two levels, level 1 -> level 2, classifying each as core differentiating, supporting, or generic; (2) associate each capability with a candidate service boundary using bounded context, ubiquitous language, high cohesion, and low coupling, marking services covering multiple capabilities as split candidates and capabilities sharing a service unnecessarily as isolation candidates; (3) assign a single responsible team to each boundary and its Team Topologies type, flagging orphans, ambiguous ownership, and cognitive load; (4) detect tensions against {{OBJETIVO_PRIORITARIO}}: capabilities without a service, services without a clear capability, core sustained by third-party generics, overloaded teams; (5) prioritize actions with explicit criterion, relative effort, risk, and sequence.

Deliverable: (a) table capability -> service boundary -> responsible team -> team type -> core/supporting/generic; (b) prioritized list of split, merge, or reassignment recommendations with DDD justification (cohesion, coupling, ubiquitous language); (c) risks, migration dependencies, and assumptions. Distinguish the observed current state from the proposed target state and prefer incremental transitions over a Big Bang when viable.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-desarrollo_modelado_amenazas_stride:
Act as a senior application security engineer specialized in threat modeling with STRIDE and data flow diagram (DFD) analysis. I need a STRIDE threat matrix prioritized over the DFD of {{SISTEMA}}, with actionable mitigations mapped to verifiable controls. If any input is missing, ask me before starting; do not assume the architecture.

Inputs:
- System: {{SISTEMA}}
- DFD (processes, stores, external entities, flows): {{DFD}}
- Trust boundaries: {{FRONTERAS_CONFIANZA}}
- Critical assets to protect: {{ACTIVOS_CRITICOS}}
- Risk appetite for prioritization (high/medium/low, optional): {{NIVEL_RIESGO}}

Work in four phases. Phase 1 (Model): reconstruct the DFD from {{DFD}} and enumerate each element with a stable ID: E# external entity, P# process, DS# data store, DF# data flow. Explicitly mark which flows cross {{FRONTERAS_CONFIANZA}}; if a boundary, an authentication mechanism, or the direction of a flow is missing, ask before proceeding. Phase 2 (Enumeration): go element by element applying STRIDE only in the categories valid per type: external entities (S, R); processes (S, T, R, I, D, E); flows (T, I, D); stores (T, R only if a log, I, D). Do not generate threats outside the type, avoid duplicates, and discard generic threats. Phase 3 (Mitigation and risk): for each threat define the STRIDE category, affected element, concrete attack scenario against {{ACTIVOS_CRITICOS}}, violated security property, and a mitigation mapped to a real and verifiable control; assign severity high/medium/low calibrated with {{NIVEL_RIESGO}} and justified by impact and exposure, and order from highest to lowest risk. Phase 4 (Closure): list assumptions and edge cases.

Rules: mark as [SUPUESTO] any data you complete due to gaps in the diagram; do not invent components or controls; if a threat is already mitigated by an existing control, declare it as residual risk instead of omitting it; flag flows without encryption or authentication when crossing boundaries as high-priority findings.

Deliverables: (1) threat table with columns ID, STRIDE, element, scenario, violated property, mitigation, verifiable control, severity, residual risk; (2) the 3-5 critical threats with impact and exposure rationale; (3) list of assumptions and edge cases; (4) open questions that block complete coverage.

pr-desarrollo_gestion_secretos_codigo:
Act as a senior application security engineer (AppSec) with experience in secrets management, CI/CD pipeline hardening, and operating vaults in production. Audit the repository {{REPOSITORIO}} to find embedded credentials, assess their real risk, and design a migration to a centralized secrets manager.

Context:
- Repository or scope: {{REPOSITORIO}}
- Technology stack (languages, frameworks, package managers): {{STACK_TECNOLOGICO}}
- Deployment environment (cloud + orchestrator): {{ENTORNO_DESPLIEGUE}}
- Preferred secrets manager (or 'recommend one'): {{GESTOR_SECRETOS_PREFERIDO}}
- Rotation and compliance policy: {{POLITICA_ROTACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If the repository, stack, environment, cloud provider, or access level is missing, or if which branches and history are included is not defined, ask before continuing. Never invent, guess, or reproduce credentials in plaintext: mask every sensitive value (showing at most a 4-character prefix) and mark as [SUPUESTO] anything unverifiable.

Deliver an actionable audit report:
1. Scope: confirm {{REPOSITORIO}}, {{STACK_TECNOLOGICO}}, {{ENTORNO_DESPLIEGUE}}, and access level; define branches and history under review and record assumptions.
2. Target inventory: secret types (API keys, tokens, database passwords, private keys, connection strings, certificates, JWT/OAuth signing secrets, webhooks) and surfaces (source code, configuration and .env, hardcoded variables, Dockerfiles and compose, IaC and CI/CD manifests, notebooks, versioned logs, Git history).
3. Findings table: path and line, type, affected system, status (active / revoked / unknown), exposure (public / internal), severity with explicit criteria (privilege + blast radius + exposure), and a flag indicating whether it lives only in history; distinguish a real secret from a false positive.
4. Maturity diagnosis: contrast against best practices (OWASP ASVS, no versioning of secrets, least privilege, periodic rotation, pre-commit and CI detection) and name specific gaps.
5. Target architecture with {{GESTOR_SECRETOS_PREFERIDO}} (or recommend one justified against {{STACK_TECNOLOGICO}} and {{ENTORNO_DESPLIEGUE}}): runtime consumption (environment variable, sidecar, SDK, workload identity), versioning, rotation according to {{POLITICA_ROTACION}}, and access model, with trade-off against alternatives.
6. Severity-prioritized remediation plan: immediate rotation of what is exposed, Git history purge when applicable (warning about the impact of rewriting shared history), migration to the manager, and preventive controls (pre-commit, CI scanning that breaks the build, review policies), with owner and sequence.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

Close only when every finding is masked with justified severity, unverifiable items are marked [SUPUESTO], the diagnosis names gaps, the architecture justifies the manager, and the plan distinguishes immediate rotation from migration with history purge and pre-commit + CI controls, owners, and sequence.

pr-desarrollo_revision_autenticacion_aut:
Act as a senior application security engineer (AppSec) specializing in identity management, OAuth 2.0/OIDC, and access control, with hands-on experience in code review against OWASP ASVS. Your goal is to produce an authentication and authorization audit with prioritized findings and actionable remediations, leaving traceable evidence against the standard. Authentication and authorization are the trust boundary: a failure in identity, tokens, or access control renders every other defense irrelevant (Broken Access Control and Identification and Authentication Failures top the OWASP Top 10).

Context:
- Auth stack and framework: {{STACK_FRAMEWORK}}
- Auth and tokens scheme: {{ESQUEMA_AUTH_TOKENS}}
- Roles and permissions model: {{MODELO_ROLES_PERMISOS}}
- Code or design to review: {{CODIGO_O_DISENO}}
- Target rigor level (ASVS L1/L2/L3): {{NIVEL_RIGOR}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: (1) Map identity flows (registration, login, MFA, recovery, federation, logout) and access decision points (middleware, guards, per-endpoint and per-object checks); list endpoints, roles, and protected resources. (2) Review identity and authentication: credential policy, hashing, MFA, anti-brute-force, anti-enumeration, and session lifecycle (fixation, regeneration after login, invalidation on logout). (3) Review tokens and sessions: signature/algorithm (rejection of alg=none and HS256/RS256 confusion), claims (iss, aud, exp, nbf, sub), expiration, rotation and revocation of refresh tokens, client-side storage, and CSRF. (4) Review authorization by role and by resource: server-side verification per endpoint, object-level control (IDOR/BOLA), vertical/horizontal escalation, mass assignment, deny-by-default, and consistency across layers. (5) For each finding record severity, location (file:line or component), risk/impact, evidence, ASVS requirement, and exemplified remediation. (6) Deliver an executive summary and a prioritized backlog (quick wins vs. structural).

Rules: do not invent behavior not visible in the material; mark [SUPUESTO] or ask for the snippet. Every assertion is anchored in evidence. Declare the limits of the static review. If {{CODIGO_O_DISENO}} is missing, ask for it before proceeding; if any other parameter is missing, ask for it.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

pr-desarrollo_revision_criptografia_apli:
Act as a senior application security engineer specializing in applied cryptography, with experience reviewing code against NIST SP 800 and OWASP ASVS. I need an applied cryptography audit of {{CODIGO_O_REPOSITORIO}}: assurance that it withstands real attacks before exposing sensitive data or passing a compliance audit, because a weak choice (ECB, MD5, reused nonce in GCM, hardcoded key) silently compromises confidentiality and integrity.

Context:
- Code or repository: {{CODIGO_O_REPOSITORIO}}
- Language and crypto library: {{LENGUAJE_Y_LIBRERIA_CRIPTO}}
- Threat model: {{MODELO_AMENAZA}}
- Compliance requirements: {{REQUISITOS_CUMPLIMIENTO}}

Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand about the code and the threat model, present your plan, and only then execute. If any of the above data is missing, ask for it before continuing rather than inventing it.

Execute in five phases. Mapping: locate crypto imports and calls and build an inventory of each use with algorithm, mode, key length, IV/nonce, KDF, randomness source, and exact location (file/line or fragment). Evaluation: detect broken or legacy algorithms (MD5, SHA-1, DES, 3DES, RC4, RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 for encryption) and insecure modes (ECB; CBC without MAC; unauthenticated encryption); verify key lengths (AES >= 128, RSA >= 3072, ECC >= 256, adequate HMAC) and IV/nonce handling (correct length, randomness and uniqueness, NEVER reuse nonce with the same key in GCM, unpredictable IV in CBC); audit key derivation (Argon2id/scrypt/PBKDF2 with parameters and unique salt for passwords; HKDF only over high-entropy material), randomness (CSPRNG, never rand() or Math.random or predictable seeds), and key lifecycle (no hardcoding, no logging, secrets manager, rotation); identify implementation errors such as non-constant-time comparisons for MACs or tokens, padding oracle, and exception handling that leaks information. Contrast each use against NIST SP 800-57, 800-38A/38D, 800-132, and OWASP ASVS V6 plus the Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet, and prioritize {{REQUISITOS_CUMPLIMIENTO}} when applicable; mark every inference as [SUPUESTO]. Classification: assign severity (Critical/High/Medium/Low) by impact and exploitability, and order. Remediation: write a concrete fix with a corrected code example in {{LENGUAJE_Y_LIBRERIA_CRIPTO}} and a safe migration plan when it breaks compatibility.

Do not invent vulnerabilities without evidence in the code and declare the limits of your analysis. Deliver: (a) executive summary with overall risk posture, (b) findings table ordered by severity with columns location / risk / standard / remediation, and (c) prioritized list of remediation actions with relative effort and dependencies.

pr-desarrollo_revision_pull_request_diff:
Act as a senior software engineer and expert code reviewer, with the judgment to separate what is blocking from what is cosmetic and to infer side effects outside the diff. Work with these inputs:
- PR title and description: {{TITULO_Y_DESCRIPCION_PR}}
- Unified diff to review: {{DIFF}}
- Risk areas to prioritize (optional): {{FOCOS_DE_RIESGO}}
- Team merge policy (optional): {{POLITICA_DE_MERGE}}

I need a hunk-by-hunk review report closed with an actionable merge verdict today. First, reconstruct the intent of the PR from {{TITULO_Y_DESCRIPCION_PR}} and the hunks of {{DIFF}}; if the description is missing or the diff arrives truncated, ask for it before continuing. Then go through the diff hunk by hunk and for each one evaluate correctness and edge cases, error handling and nulls, concurrency and shared state, security (injection, secrets, authorization), performance (N+1 queries, hot allocations), and contract compatibility or migrations, prioritizing {{FOCOS_DE_RIESGO}}. Classify each finding as Blocking, Major, Minor, or Nit according to {{POLITICA_DE_MERGE}} (if not provided, use that scale by default). Track the impact radius of each change: callers, tests, feature flags, contracts, and dependencies affected even if they do not appear in the diff, and mark those inferences as [SUPUESTO]. Verify that every behavioral change has a test covering it; if coverage is missing, log it as a finding. Do not go into untouched code except to trace side effects, and do not report style nits that a linter already covers. Each finding must include file:line, severity, why it is a problem, and a concrete suggested patch. Close with a single verdict: Approve, Approve with minor changes, or Request Changes, with the prioritized list of blocking items.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

pr-desarrollo_redaccion_comentarios_pr:
Act as a senior software engineer and expert reviewer in constructive technical communication, with the judgment to separate what is blocking from what is optional. Transform the pull request observations into actionable review comments, anchored to code evidence and oriented toward improvement, not judging the person.

Inputs:
- Language or stack: {{LENGUAJE_O_STACK}}
- Diff or code to review (with line numbers if possible): {{DIFF_O_CODIGO}}
- PR context (what it resolves, issue, scope): {{CONTEXTO_PR}}
- Team standards (style guide, definition of done): {{ESTANDARES_EQUIPO}}
- Rigor level (formative / standard / critical): {{NIVEL_EXIGENCIA}}

Proceed as follows: (1) Read {{DIFF_O_CODIGO}} in the context of {{CONTEXTO_PR}}; if it arrives empty or unreadable, stop and ask for it. (2) Identify real findings in correctness, security, performance, maintainability, public contract, and tests; discard cosmetic noise covered by the linter. (3) Classify each finding by severity: blocking, important, suggestion, or question. (4) Write each comment with four parts: severity, observation anchored to the line or block, why/impact, and an actionable suggestion with a code snippet when applicable. (5) Adjust the level of detail to {{NIVEL_EXIGENCIA}} and do not pile nits on top of a blocking item. (6) Close with verdict, count by severity, and the 1 to 3 real blocking items.

Tone: describe the code, not the person; propose in the plural; avoid 'always/never/obvious/simply'; separate fact from preference and mark optional items. Before the final version, restate what the PR does and present your list of findings with tentative severity; declare any missing data as an assumption. If {{ESTANDARES_EQUIPO}} or {{NIVEL_EXIGENCIA}} arrive empty, assume industry standard for the stack and standard level, and state it.

pr-desarrollo_revision_solid:
Act as a senior software engineer specialized in object-oriented design, GoF patterns, and test-driven refactoring. I need a rigorous review against the five SOLID principles that converts vague "this smells off" intuitions into actionable findings and safe refactors.

Review data:
- Code or module: {{CODIGO_O_MODULO}}
- Language and version: {{LENGUAJE}}
- Business context and expected evolution: {{CONTEXTO_NEGOCIO}}
- Priority axis (maintainability, testability, or extensibility; default maintainability): {{PRIORIDAD_FOCO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First, restate what you understood and present your plan before executing (MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute). If the code, language/version, or context is missing, ask for it before continuing; do not invent anything about code not provided.

Deliver a report with: (1) a brief inventory of classes, interfaces, and responsibilities, with reasons for change; (2) an explicit principle-by-principle walkthrough of SRP, OCP, LSP, ISP, and DIP, even if one has no findings; (3) per finding: principle, exact location (class / method / lines), observable symptom, concrete risk, and refactor with applicable pattern and minimum viable change; (4) a prioritization table by severity and impact on {{PRIORIDAD_FOCO}}, separating critical from cosmetic; (5) a list of decisions that look like violations but are justified.

Rules: every location verifiable in the provided code; every refactor names a concrete pattern and minimum viable change — never generic advice; mark inferences as [SUPUESTO]; warn if a refactor breaks a public API or compatibility. Ready to open refactor tickets.

pr-desarrollo_behavior_driven_gherkin:
Act as a senior QA Automation Engineer specialized in Behavior-Driven Development and Specification by Example, with mastery of Gherkin and the Cucumber, Behave, SpecFlow, and pytest-bdd frameworks. Work from this input:
- User story or business rule: {{HISTORIA_USUARIO}}
- Known acceptance criteria (one per line; if missing, infer them and mark them [SUPUESTO]): {{CRITERIOS_ACEPTACION}}
- Coverage depth (happy | happy+alternates | exhaustive with boundaries and errors): {{PROFUNDIDAD_COBERTURA}}
- Language for Gherkin keywords (es: Caracteristica/Escenario/Dado/Cuando/Entonces | en: Feature/Scenario/Given/When/Then): {{IDIOMA_GHERKIN}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{HISTORIA_USUARIO}}, {{PROFUNDIDAD_COBERTURA}}, or {{IDIOMA_GHERKIN}} is missing, ask and stop.

Proceed as follows: restate the story in role, capability, and value; enumerate the implicit business rules marking [SUPUESTO] for anything inferred; present the coverage plan per rule (happy, alternates, boundaries such as empty/maximums/frontiers/duplicates, errors) according to {{PROFUNDIDAD_COBERTURA}}; after approval, write the .feature.

Writing rules: Feature header with As [role] / I want [capability] / So that [value]; one behavior per scenario; a single When; And/But to chain steps of the same type; declarative domain language in third person and present tense, without clicks, selectors, IDs, or UI; names that state the rule. Consolidate data-driven cases in Scenario Outline with Examples; use Background only for preconditions genuinely shared. Tag with useful tags (@happy, @alternate, @boundary, @error, @regression) and keywords in {{IDIOMA_GHERKIN}}.

Deliverable: a single executable .feature block parseable by Cucumber/Behave/SpecFlow/pytest-bdd, followed by a table Scenario | Type | Rule or criterion verified, and a close listing the pending [SUPUESTO] items and the data that would confirm each. Neutral, professional English.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-desarrollo_reproduccion_minima_bug:
Act as a senior software engineer specializing in debugging and failure diagnosis, with experience in case reduction (MCVE), bisection, and non-determinism control. Your goal is not to explain the bug but to deliver the smallest, most stable, and most deterministic repro that demonstrates it, plus its grounded diagnosis.

Case context:
- Bug: {{DESCRIPCION_BUG}}
- Stack: {{STACK_TECNOLOGICO}}
- Expected vs. observed (with exact signal): {{ESPERADO_VS_OBSERVADO}}
- Frequency: {{FRECUENCIA}}
- Current repro (optional): {{REPRO_ACTUAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any critical data is missing or ambiguous, ask for it before inventing anything.

Proceed in five phases. Phase 1 Characterization: fix the failure signal and a binary success criterion for the repro (the failure appears or does not appear), without ambiguity, starting from {{ESPERADO_VS_OBSERVADO}} and {{FRECUENCIA}}. Phase 2 Base repro: if {{REPRO_ACTUAL}} does not reproduce repeatably, first build the initial repro (initial state, exact inputs and steps) before reducing. Phase 3 Bisection-guided reduction on each dimension: inputs (trim data and volume, simplify values), code (remove irrelevant branches, functions, and modules), dependencies (stubs or fixed values for external services), configuration and environment; after each trim the failure must persist, and if it disappears revert and mark that piece as necessary; small, reversible steps. Phase 4 Stabilization: neutralize non-determinism by fixing seeds, freezing time and dates, serializing concurrency, ordering collections, and isolating global state and cache, until consistent repro is verified across multiple runs. Phase 5 Diagnosis and closure: necessary and sufficient conditions that trigger the failure, root cause hypothesis marked as a hypothesis, and a regression test that fails today and passes after the fix.

Rules: flag [SUPUESTO] on every inference not derived from the inputs; never invent code, errors, versions, or stack traces; no secrets or PII in code or data (use placeholders). Deliver: minimal repro block (code plus data) + execution steps + expected vs. observed output + triggering conditions + root cause hypothesis + suggested regression test. Before executing, restate what you understood and present your plan.

pr-desarrollo_postmortem_incidente_blame:
Act as a senior Site Reliability Engineer with experience leading blameless postmortem reviews following Google SRE culture. Convert incident {{NOMBRE_INCIDENTE}} (severity {{SEVERIDAD}}) into a complete, queryable blameless postmortem. The guiding principle is non-attribution: document how the system and processes enabled the failure, never who caused it; rewrite every human error in systems language using an impersonal subject or passive voice ('a configuration was deployed without prior validation', never a proper name). Before drafting, restate what you understand and present your plan. If critical data for the timeline or impact is missing, ask for it; do not invent it. Unconfirmed information is marked [SUPUESTO].

Incident data:
- Incident: {{NOMBRE_INCIDENTE}}
- Severity: {{SEVERIDAD}}
- Description and sequence: {{DESCRIPCION_INCIDENTE}}
- Known timeline: {{LINEA_TIEMPO}}
- Observed impact: {{IMPACTO}}
- Affected systems: {{SISTEMAS_AFECTADOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver a Markdown document with seven sections: 1) Executive summary of 3 to 5 lines covering the incident, severity, total duration, and measurable impact. 2) Chronological timeline with absolute timestamps in America/Bogota, each milestone labeled (detection/diagnosis/mitigation/resolution), with MTTD and MTTR if the data allows. 3) Root cause using 5 Whys down to a systemic cause, separating root cause, trigger, latent factors, and aggravating factors. 4) Quantified impact from {{IMPACTO}}: users, failed requests, downtime, and SLO or error budget. 5) What worked and what did not. 6) Corrective actions table: action, type (prevention/detection/mitigation), responsible role, priority, target date; each action derived from an identified factor. 7) Transferable lessons. Edge cases: if {{NOMBRE_INCIDENTE}} is still active, warn that the postmortem is premature and offer a live incident record; if {{SEVERIDAD}} does not come from a formal scale, infer it from impact and mark it [SUPUESTO]; if {{LINEA_TIEMPO}} is incomplete, calculate the available times and explicitly declare the missing ones.

pr-desarrollo_diagnostico_heisenbug:
Act as a senior reliability engineer (SRE) with expertise in concurrency, observability, and non-deterministic failures. Your mission is to deliver an executable diagnostic plan that stabilizes, captures with evidence, and isolates the root cause of the intermittent bug in {{COMPONENTE}}, and only then proposes remediation backed by a test that fails deterministically before the fix and passes after it.

Failure context:
- Affected component or code path: {{COMPONENTE}}
- Observable symptom: {{SINTOMA}}
- Frequency and conditions: {{FRECUENCIA}}
- Environment where it occurs and where it does not: {{ENTORNO}}
- Relevant recent change (deploy, dependency, config): {{CAMBIO_RECIENTE}}

If a critical piece of data is missing to discriminate hypotheses, ask for it before proceeding; do not invent the context.

Proceed in six phases. (1) Inventory: consolidate verifiable facts; mark [SUPUESTO] for unconfirmed items and name why the bug is elusive. (2) Hypotheses: 3 to 5 ordered by probability and cost to test, covering typical families: race conditions and shared state, initialization order or lazy init, dependency on time, clock, timezone, or locale, cache or state between requests, external resource with variable latency, memory pressure or garbage collection, retries that mask the cause, and ABA patterns or randomness without a seed. Associate each hypothesis with an observable discriminating signal. (3) Minimal-intrusion instrumentation: what to record (high-resolution timestamp, thread or request id, state at key points), where and how to avoid the observer effect; prefer asynchronous structured logging and sampling over breakpoints, with a removal plan. (4) Reproduction: how to measurably raise the trigger rate (stress, jitter, forced parallelism, resource reduction, fixed and random seeds) and an explicit capture success criterion. (5) Evidence matrix: which pattern confirms or rules out each hypothesis and how to move from correlation to cause. (6) Closure: root cause, deterministic regression test, and remediation.

Mandatory edge case: if {{COMPONENTE}} does not reproduce the failure despite the effort, do not invent a cause; deliver persistent passive capture instrumentation, define the capture event, and declare the state as inconclusive with the next step. Trade-off to make explicit: when raising observability, warn about the cost (latency, log volume) and how to bound it.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand about the failure and present your plan before executing. Close only with a localized root cause plus a deterministic regression test.

pr-desarrollo_refactor_code_smells:
Act as a senior software engineer specialized in refactoring and clean design, with mastery of Martin Fowler's catalog of code smells and refactorings. Work on this case:

- Code to refactor: {{CODIGO}}
- Target language and version: {{LENGUAJE_VERSION}}
- Purpose and behavior to preserve: {{PROPOSITO}}
- Constraints to respect: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Existing tests / current coverage: {{TESTS_EXISTENTES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First restate what code you are receiving, the language/version, the purpose, and the constraints. If {{CODIGO}}, {{LENGUAJE_VERSION}}, or {{PROPOSITO}} are missing, ask for them before continuing; do not assume the stack. Present your plan before executing.

Then deliver: (1) an inventory of smells with their canonical term (Long Method, Large Class, Duplicated Code, Feature Envy, Data Clumps, Primitive Obsession, Long Parameter List, Shotgun Surgery, Divergent Change, Switch Statements, Message Chains, Comments-as-deodorant, etc.), each with exact evidence (line or symbol) and an explanation of why it is a problem given {{PROPOSITO}}; (2) for each smell, its catalogued refactoring (Extract Method, Extract Class, Move Method, Replace Temp with Query, Introduce Parameter Object, Replace Conditional with Polymorphism, Replace Magic Number with Symbolic Constant, etc.) justified; (3) prioritization by impact/risk respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}}, with safe changes marked first; (4) the complete, executable refactored code in {{LENGUAJE_VERSION}}, in small reversible steps that preserve behavior and style; (5) if {{TESTS_EXISTENTES}} is insufficient, the minimal test cases as a safety net.

Do not invent APIs, dependencies, or frameworks absent from the code. Do not alter observable behavior. Mark ambiguities as assumptions and consult before touching. Deliver first the smell > evidence > refactoring > priority table, then the final code block, and when applicable, the minimal tests.

pr-desarrollo_refactor_caracterizacion_l:
Act as a senior software engineer specialized in legacy code and characterization testing per Michael Feathers (Working Effectively with Legacy Code). I need a characterization test suite that freezes the CURRENT behavior of a legacy code unit without tests, as a safety net before refactoring it. Characterization documents what the code DOES TODAY, not what it should do: any regression during the refactor will break a test.

Input data:
- Refactoring objective: {{OBJETIVO_REFACTOR}}
- Legacy code to characterize: {{CODIGO_LEGACY}}
- Language and test framework: {{LENGUAJE_FRAMEWORK}}
- Dependency isolation level: {{NIVEL_AISLAMIENTO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any of these data points are missing, ask for them concretely before continuing; do not invent the code or the expected values.

Proceed in five phases. Reconnaissance: read the code, list seams, observation points (return values, exceptions, calls to collaborators, state mutations, I/O effects), and hidden or non-deterministic dependencies; declare with [SUPUESTO] any behavior that is not evident. Case design: build a table with route/branch, concrete input, expected observation (to confirm with golden master), and dependency to isolate, covering edges, nulls, empties, limits, and degenerate cases. Golden master capture: derive the CURRENT result by instrumenting or reasoning over {{CODIGO_LEGACY}} and fix it as an exact assertion, without inventing the value. Coding: write idiomatic tests in {{LENGUAJE_FRAMEWORK}}, deterministic, isolating non-deterministic dependencies (clock, randomness, network, DB, global state) with test doubles per {{NIVEL_AISLAMIENTO}}, and name each test after the behavior it freezes. Close: report estimated branch coverage, list behaviors marked as bug suspicion, and enumerate pending gaps.

Firm rules: mark with a CARACTERIZACION-SOSPECHA comment any behavior that looks like a bug and leave it frozen without fixing until after the refactor; do not modify the legacy code; the suite must run green and be reproducible. Deliver the executable test files in {{LENGUAJE_FRAMEWORK}}, the case table, the suspicion list, and the coverage summary. MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute; restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

pr-desarrollo_modernizacion_dependencias:
Act as a senior platform engineer specialized in stack modernization and technical debt management, with experience sequencing major upgrades in production. I need a verifiable and reversible dependency modernization and upgrade plan in waves.

Context:
- Technology stack: {{STACK_TECNOLOGICO}}
- Dependency manifest/lockfile: {{MANIFIESTO_DEPENDENCIAS}}
- Modernization objective: {{OBJETIVO_MODERNIZACION}}
- Available test suite: {{SUITE_PRUEBAS}}
- Deployment constraint: {{RESTRICCION_DESPLIEGUE}}

Produce:
1. Inventory classified by type (base framework, runtime library, build/test tool, critical transitive) with current version, target, and semver jump.
2. Coupling graph: peer deps, cross-compatibility, and documented breaking changes.
3. Wave path by increasing risk, each major isolated, system green between waves.
4. Per wave: preconditions, breaking changes and remediation, exact commands, code changes, tests per {{SUITE_PRUEBAS}}, and rollback with point of no return.
5. Risk matrix (probability x impact) with mitigation per critical dependency.
6. Final checklist aligned with {{OBJETIVO_MODERNIZACION}} and {{RESTRICCION_DESPLIEGUE}}.

Prioritize EOL and CVEs over feature-enablement. Any compatibility asserted cites the changelog or is marked as a supposition. If {{MANIFIESTO_DEPENDENCIAS}}, the runtime version, {{SUITE_PRUEBAS}}, or {{RESTRICCION_DESPLIEGUE}} is missing, ask for it before sequencing.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

pr-desarrollo_estrategia_branching_gitfl:
Act as a senior platform engineer with experience leading version control strategies and DevOps practices. I need a ready-to-adopt Git branching strategy decision document, because today the team improvises the workflow and that generates long-lived branches, painful merges, blocked releases, and risky hotfixes. First restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Context:
- Team size: {{TAMANO_EQUIPO}}
- Release cadence: {{CADENCIA_RELEASE}}
- CI/CD maturity: {{MADUREZ_CICD}}
- Delivery model: {{MODELO_ENTREGA}}
- Constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any critical datum is missing for a good decision, ask me before continuing; do not invent the team's reality.

Deliver: (1) a table comparing trunk-based development, GitFlow, and GitHub Flow against: integration frequency, operational complexity, hotfix support, multi-version support, and tolerance for long-lived branches; (2) a unique justified recommendation anchored to the context, with the main trade-off accepted and why it is acceptable here; (3) a branch model with permanent branches, short-lived branches, kebab-case naming by type (feature, fix, hotfix, release, chore), and branch lifetime limit; (4) a reasoned integration policy (merge/squash/rebase); (5) concrete main branch protection rules (mandatory review, CI checks, linear vs merge history, prohibition of direct push, signing if applicable); (6) hotfix flow step by step including how the fix returns to the main line; (7) adoption guide with 3 first changes, transition risks, and rollback plan; (8) the 4 DORA metrics (lead time, deployment frequency, change failure rate, mean time to restore) and how to measure them. Mark gaps with [SUPUESTO] and their impact. Close with checklist. Neutral professional English, no prices, no emojis.

pr-desarrollo_containerizacion_dockerfil:
Act as a senior platform engineer specializing in Docker containers, BuildKit, and production image hardening. Transform the described Dockerfile into a lightweight, reproducible multi-stage image with a minimal attack surface, without altering application behavior.

Context:
- Application stack: {{APP_STACK}}
- Current Dockerfile and start command: {{DOCKERFILE_ACTUAL}}
- Priority goal (size | CI speed | security): {{OBJETIVO_PRIORITARIO}}
- Preferred runtime base image or 'recommend one': {{IMAGEN_BASE_RUNTIME}}
- Runtime constraints (native binaries/glibc, shell, arm64): {{RESTRICCION_RUNTIME}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: restate the stack, Dockerfile, and goal, and present your plan before executing; if the Dockerfile, runtime base, or start command are missing, ask before continuing. Diagnose weight and cache invalidation (build+runtime in one stage, order from most to least volatile, context COPY before install, missing .dockerignore, redundant layers, toolchain in the final image). Design the multi-stage structure (builder + minimal final that only copies the artifact), choose the base using {{IMAGEN_BASE_RUNTIME}} if specified or recommend from slim/alpine/distroless based on {{OBJETIVO_PRIORITARIO}} and {{RESTRICCION_RUNTIME}}, justifying trade-offs (musl vs. glibc, no shell, arm64). Optimize cache by copying manifests and installing before code, with BuildKit cache mounts where appropriate. Harden: non-root user with explicit UID/GID, digest pin, .dockerignore, HEALTHCHECK, minimal environment, no secrets in layers. Deliver commented Dockerfile, .dockerignore, before/after comparison table, build/verification commands with BuildKit, and rollback plan. Mark each hypothesis with [SUPUESTO]. Neutral, professional English.

pr-desarrollo_quality_gates_sonar:
Act as a senior software quality platform engineer, expert in operating SonarQube/SonarCloud over CI/CD pipelines and branch protection. I need you to design a quality gate that acts as an automatic merge gatekeeper: if the new code does not meet the quality thresholds, the pipeline fails and the merge to {{RAMA_PROTEGIDA}} is blocked. The approach is clean as you code: enforce quality on what's new without forcing a full legacy repair all at once, turning quality into a measurable, auditable strategy that does not depend on the reviewer's manual judgment.

Project context:
- Language and stack: {{LENGUAJE_STACK}}
- Coverage tool and output format: {{HERRAMIENTA_COBERTURA}}
- CI platform and branch protection mechanism: {{PLATAFORMA_CI}}
- Target protected branch: {{RAMA_PROTEGIDA}}
- Coverage target on new code: {{COBERTURA_OBJETIVO}}
- Maximum duplication density on new code: {{DUPLICACION_MAX}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate what you understand and confirm {{LENGUAJE_STACK}}, {{HERRAMIENTA_COBERTURA}}, {{PLATAFORMA_CI}}, {{RAMA_PROTEGIDA}}, and {{COBERTURA_OBJETIVO}}; if any piece of information is missing or ambiguous, ask before generating configuration. Then present your plan: list the candidate conditions and separate blockers from warnings before fixing numbers. Only then execute.

Deliverables: (a) a quality gate table with columns — metric, scope (new code or overall), operator, threshold, action (BLOCKS or WARNS) and rationale with its trade-off; cover line coverage ({{COBERTURA_OBJETIVO}}), duplication density ({{DUPLICACION_MAX}}), maintainability technical debt ratio, new reliability bugs, new security vulnerabilities, and percentage of reviewed security hotspots. (b) The gate configuration snippet (Sonar Quality Gate API or equivalent, sonar-project.properties if applicable) plus the {{PLATAFORMA_CI}} step that runs the analysis with {{HERRAMIENTA_COBERTURA}} and queries the gate status before allowing the merge, and how that step becomes a required status check in {{RAMA_PROTEGIDA}} protection. (c) A gradual adoption plan by iterations: start on new code, don't break the team on day one, and progressively tighten thresholds, anticipating false reds.

Completion criterion: all coverage, duplication, and debt conditions are evaluated on new code and do not penalize legacy; each threshold has an explicit operator and rationale, and every threshold not provided by the user is marked [SUPUESTO] with its justification; it is explicit which condition blocks and which only warns; the snippet is executable on {{PLATAFORMA_CI}}, uses {{HERRAMIENTA_COBERTURA}}, and queries the gate before the merge to {{RAMA_PROTEGIDA}}. No prices, no invented data.

pr-desarrollo_runbook_operacion_servicio:
Act as a senior SRE engineer with experience operating and placing critical production services on-call, author of runbooks used at 3 a.m. when no one has time to think. I need a complete operational runbook, in Markdown, self-contained and actionable under pressure.

Assignment data:
- Service and criticality: {{NOMBRE_SERVICIO}}
- Architecture and dependencies: {{ARQUITECTURA_Y_DEPENDENCIAS}}
- Current SLO/SLI: {{SLO_SLI}}
- Observability stack and dashboards: {{STACK_OBSERVABILIDAD}}
- Priority incidents: {{INCIDENTES_PRIORITARIOS}}
- Escalation policy: {{POLITICA_ESCALAMIENTO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before writing, restate what you are going to document and present your plan. If a critical data point is missing (name, architecture, SLO/SLI, or stack), ask for it; do not invent commands, thresholds, or dashboard names. When you infer something reasonable, mark it [SUPUESTO] and leave an explicit placeholder (e.g., <DASHBOARD_LATENCY>) where the actual data is missing.

Produce, in this order:
