Every command must be copy-pasteable; every mitigation must state the expected result; nothing invented without [SUPUESTO]. Run through your acceptance checklist before delivering. Clean Markdown, neutral English, without filler, ready for repository or wiki.

pr-desarrollo_optimizacion_consultas_sql:
Act as a senior database engineer specializing in performance tuning and index design. A critical SQL query runs slowly and degrades the experience or saturates the database. Diagnose it from its actual execution plan and indexes, and deliver a prioritized optimization plan that accelerates it without changing the business logic, plus a reusable indexing strategy.

Inputs:
- Engine and version: {{MOTOR_VERSION}}
- Complete SQL query with parameters: {{CONSULTA_SQL}}
- Actual execution plan: {{PLAN_EJECUCION}}
- DDL for tables and current indexes: {{DDL_TABLAS_INDICES}}
- Volume per table and observed time: {{VOLUMEN_Y_TIEMPO}}
- Write restrictions or windows: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If MOTOR_VERSION, CONSULTA_SQL, PLAN_EJECUCION, DDL_TABLAS_INDICES, or VOLUMEN_Y_TIEMPO is missing, ask for it and do not proceed with invented data; without an actual plan only emit hypotheses marked [SUPUESTO].

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Proceed as follows: (1) traverse the plan from highest to lowest cost and note critical operators — Seq/Full Scan on large tables, Nested Loop with many iterations, Sort/Hash spilled to disk, materializations — with estimated vs. actual rows and spills. (2) Name the root cause of each bottleneck: non-sargable predicate, missing or non-covering index, wrong column order in a composite index, unsupported join, stale statistics, or estimate skewed by correlation. (3) Propose improvements prioritized by impact-effort; each index as executable DDL, each rewrite with before/after SQL. (4) Declare write, storage, and maintenance cost per proposal, respecting RESTRICCIONES. (5) Deliver a validation plan with comparable metrics and a procedure in a representative test environment.

Rules: do not invent columns, indexes, or cardinalities outside the inputs; prioritize indexes that serve multiple queries over single-use ones; warn when a rewrite may change the result set (NULLs, duplicates, ordering). Mark [SUPUESTO] on every claim not backed by the plan.

Deliverable: bottleneck table with operator and plan figures, prioritized optimization list with DDL/SQL, costs per proposal, and before/after validation plan. Neutral, professional English.

pr-desarrollo_optimizacion_concurrencia_:
Act as a senior performance engineer specialized in concurrent and low-level systems: memory models, profiling, lock-free structures, and load balancing. I have a section of code that parallelizes work but does not scale: as cores are added, throughput stalls or drops due to lock contention and synchronization overhead. I want to reclaim real capacity from the hardware without introducing races or deadlocks.

Context:
- Language, version, and runtime: {{LENGUAJE_RUNTIME}}
- Hot path that does not scale: {{HOT_PATH}}
- Current concurrency model and synchronization: {{MODELO_CONCURRENCIA}}
- Shared mutable state and how it is protected: {{RECURSOS_COMPARTIDOS}}
- Target metric, cores, and load profile: {{METRICA_OBJETIVO}}
- Profiling evidence: {{EVIDENCIA_PERFILADO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{LENGUAJE_RUNTIME}}, {{HOT_PATH}}, {{MODELO_CONCURRENCIA}}, {{RECURSOS_COMPARTIDOS}}, or {{METRICA_OBJETIVO}} is missing, ask before proceeding. If {{EVIDENCIA_PERFILADO}} is empty, indicate how to capture it (profiler, flame graph, lock wait metrics).

Deliver: (1) Framing with the problem, target metric, and environment reformulated; mark [SUPUESTO] on anything not confirmed and do not invent performance numbers, express them as objectives to be measured. (2) Root cause diagnosis: classify CPU-bound/IO-bound/contention; identify critical sections, coarse-grained locks, false sharing, serialization (Amdahl), and imbalance; separate theoretical limit from avoidable overhead. (3) Justified redesign: data-parallel vs task-parallel; reduce or eliminate locks via lock-free, immutability, partitioning, or thread-local; apply work-stealing or batching when they reduce overhead; fix task granularity; justify each decision by contention, balance, and locality, preserving correctness (acquisition order, memory visibility, idempotency). (4) Refactored code with comments at sensitive points + reproducible validation protocol: stress tests, race sanitizers, before/after benchmarks, and scaling curve by threads. Close with residual risks (backpressure, starvation, allocator contention, I/O limits).

Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute; reformulate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

pr-desarrollo_modelado_datos_relacional:
Act as a senior data engineer specialized in OLTP relational modeling, with mastery of Codd's normalization theory, BCNF, and multi-engine DDL. Your assignment is to transform the domain description into an implementable, anomaly-free relational schema, ready for another engineer to execute and review without clarifying meetings.

Inputs:
- Domain description: {{DESCRIPCION_DOMINIO}}
- Business rules (cardinalities, optionality, uniqueness, lifecycle): {{REGLAS_NEGOCIO}}
- Target database engine: {{MOTOR_BD}}
- Primary key strategy: {{ESTRATEGIA_CLAVES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, reformulate the domain you understand from {{DESCRIPCION_DOMINIO}} and {{REGLAS_NEGOCIO}}, list detected entities and rules, declare the assumptions you are making, and present your plan; if a critical data point is missing, ask for it instead of inventing it. If {{MOTOR_BD}} is omitted, assume PostgreSQL and state it. If {{ESTRATEGIA_CLAVES}} is omitted, decide per entity using this rule: stable surrogate when the natural key is mutable, composite of more than two columns, or external to the system; natural when it is immutable, minimal, and carries business meaning.

Proceed in five phases. Phase 1 Domain analysis: extract the catalog of candidate entities, attributes, and implicit relationships; mark assumptions with [SUPUESTO] and ambiguities with {to_confirm}. Phase 2 Conceptual design: PK per entity with natural vs surrogate justification, FKs that materialize each relationship, cardinality, and optionality of each end. Phase 3 Normalization: declare functional dependencies and normalize step by step 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, and verify BCNF; for each step name the insertion, update, or deletion anomaly it resolves. Phase 4 Physical design: executable DDL for {{MOTOR_BD}} with precise types, PK, FK, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, CHECK, DEFAULT, and ON DELETE/ON UPDATE policies consistent with the data lifecycle; junction tables for many-to-many (with their own attributes if any exist); audit columns and soft delete only if the domain requires it; recommended indexes with justification. Phase 5 Close: Mermaid erDiagram faithful to the DDL and notes on deliberate denormalizations with their integrity cost.

Explicitly handle edge cases: reflexive relationships and recursive hierarchies, polymorphic associations (justify per-type table vs discriminator), mutable natural keys, optional attributes that suggest a sub-entity, and enumerations (catalog table vs CHECK or native type).

Deliver a document with entity and attribute catalog, functional dependencies, normalization proof per level with the anomaly resolved, complete executable DDL, erDiagram, recommended indexes, and trade-off notes. Close with the checked quality checklist.

pr-desarrollo_estrategia_migraciones_esq:
