- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any of the five critical data points is missing (audience, product or topic, objective, baseline rate, list size), ask for it before drafting; do not invent values because the experimental design depends on them. If the minimum detectable effect is not specified, propose a reasonable one in percentage points of open rate and declare it as an assumption.

Generate eight subject lines covering six psychological angles: curiosity through information gap, genuine urgency or scarcity, concrete benefit, social proof, segment personalization, and before/after contrast; without duplicating angles unless justified. For each subject line: text of no more than fifty characters, a complementary preheader that does not repeat keywords from the subject line, the angle and cognitive trigger in one line. Avoid clickbait the body does not deliver.

Define the experiment: falsifiable hypothesis, primary open-rate metric (unique), spam report and unsubscribe guardrails with an abort threshold, audience split, minimum sample size per arm according to {{TASA_APERTURA_BASE}} and the minimum detectable effect, measurement window, and decision rule with numerical threshold. If {{TAMANO_LISTA}} is not sufficient to detect the desired effect, say so and propose reducing variants or widening the minimum detectable effect. Recommend two-arm champion-vs-challenger or multivariate based on what the sample size supports. Flag validity risks: short window biased by time zone, novelty effect, contamination between arms.

