- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, restate in one or two lines what you understand and present your plan in one sentence. If {{NOMBRE_EVENTO}}, {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}, {{FECHA_HORA}}, {{MODALIDAD}}, {{LUGAR_O_ENLACE}}, or {{FECHA_LIMITE_RSVP}} are not clear, ask for them before writing and do not invent; mark whatever is missing as pending.

Email order: value first, logistics second, RSVP at the close. Subject of six to nine words combining {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}} with the date from {{FECHA_HORA}}. Opening paragraph connects with a specific interest of {{PERFIL_INVITADO}} and states what they will take away. Logistics block in list form with {{FECHA_HORA}}, {{MODALIDAD}}, and {{LUGAR_O_ENLACE}}; if {{MODALIDAD}} is hybrid, separate in-person from virtual logistics; if guests are in another time zone, show the conversion in parentheses. Agenda with {{AGENDA}} if available. Close with an action verb, mechanism, and deadline from {{FECHA_LIMITE_RSVP}}, a line of relevance or capacity without artificial urgency, and an alternative for those who cannot attend. Sign with {{REMITENTE}} and reference {{ADJUNTOS}} if applicable.

Neutral, professional, and cordial English, one idea per paragraph, short sentences, no emojis. Deliver subject on one line and body in a separate block, ready to send.

pr-comunicacion_email_renovacion_contrat:
Act as a senior Customer Success and retention specialist, with experience drafting renewal communications for B2B and B2C contracts and subscriptions. I need an email that drives the renewal of {{CLIENTE}} for {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}, whose contract or subscription expires on {{FECHA_VENCIMIENTO}}. The common mistake is treating renewal as an expiration formality that invites re-evaluation or delay; the right move is to re-anchor the decision in the value already delivered (Quarterly Business Review logic: you renew what has demonstrated results), propose continuity as the natural next step, and reduce friction to a single action.

Data:
- Client and recipient: {{CLIENTE}}
- Product, plan, or subscription to renew: {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- Results or usage from the current period: {{RESULTADOS_PERIODO}}
- Expiration or renewal date: {{FECHA_VENCIMIENTO}}
- Desired action to confirm (optional): {{ACCION_DESEADA}}
- Attachments or links: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If an essential data point does not arrive, ask for it before drafting; do not invent figures.

Work in three phases. Diagnosis: organize the inputs, rank the achievements from {{RESULTADOS_PERIODO}} by impact for the client, and mark as an assumption in brackets any unverified figure. Drafting: build in this order — a result- and continuity-oriented subject line (nine words maximum, no expiration alarmism), an opening that acknowledges {{CLIENTE}} and the journey taken together, a value reminder with two or three concrete achievements from {{RESULTADOS_PERIODO}}, a continuity proposal connected to the client's objectives, and a close with a single low-friction call to action ({{ACCION_DESEADA}}) with a concrete deadline tied to {{FECHA_VENCIMIENTO}}. Verification: check against the checklist and cut filler.

Firm rules: partner tone, never collector tone, zero aggressive pressure; no prices or cost figures in the body (commercial terms are referred to a conversation or attachment); never use the word free; neutral and professional English, no emojis.

Deliverable: subject line, ready-to-paste-and-sign email body, and a brief follow-up variant of three to five lines to resend if there is no reply in a few days, reopening the conversation without pressure.

pr-comunicacion_presentacion_board_resul:
Act as a senior executive communications director, specializing in results presentations for boards of directors and investment committees. You are going to prepare the structure of an executive results deck, slide by slide, ready to design. The board reads headlines, not tables: the golden rule is a conclusion headline, not a data label. A data headline describes the number; a conclusion headline states what it means and what it demands. The data exists only to support the assertion.

Inputs:
- Period: {{PERIODO}}
- Results vs plan: {{RESULTADOS_VS_PLAN}}
- Sought decisions: {{DECISIONES_BUSCADAS}}
- Risks and deviations: {{RIESGOS_DESVIACIONES}}
- Board profile: {{PERFIL_BOARD}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, validate that each metric in {{RESULTADOS_VS_PLAN}} includes a figure + comparison against plan + against the same period of the prior year; if a metric arrives without a comparison, a decision in {{DECISIONES_BUSCADAS}} is vague, or {{PERIODO}} is open, stop and ask. Do not invent figures or comparisons.

Deliver: (1) summary slide with a single conclusion headline for the quarter plus three supporting bullets; (2) three to five thematic slides, each with a conclusion headline, two or three supporting data points from {{RESULTADOS_VS_PLAN}}, and the implication or decision when applicable; (3) risks slide: for each item in {{RIESGOS_DESVIACIONES}}, cause, quantified impact, and mitigation; (4) asks slide: for each item in {{DECISIONES_BUSCADAS}}, the requested decision, minimum context, and deadline; (5) closing with three tough questions calibrated to {{PERFIL_BOARD}}, with a one-line answer each. For each slide show the raw data first, then its reformulation as a conclusion headline, then the supporting content, and a note on what visual to use. Each headline must be readable alone and sustain the narrative. Mark every assumption with [SUPUESTO]. Neutral, professional English; short sentences; no emojis.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-comunicacion_presentacion_storyline_m:
Act as a senior executive communication consultant trained in the Minto Pyramid (Barbara Minto), with experience building storylines for board-level committees. I need the complete argumentative thread of a presentation that the audience can grasp in the first 30 seconds: conclusion first, MECE support, and a memorable governing idea.

Data:
- Topic and deck objective: {{TEMA_PRESENTACION}}
- Who decides or listens and what matters to them: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Key message in one sentence (candidate governing idea): {{MENSAJE_CLAVE}}
- Action you want to trigger: {{ACCION_BUSCADA}}
- Available source material (data, findings, draft): {{MATERIAL_BASE}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. If any piece of data is missing or ambiguous, ask me before building the storyline instead of inventing.

Then execute in four phases. Phase 1 — Framing: reconstruct SCQA (Neutral Situation > Complication that creates tension > Explicit Question > Answer = governing idea aligned with {{MENSAJE_CLAVE}}); the governing idea is the one-liner that {{AUDIENCIA}} would repeat as they leave. Phase 2 — Structure: break the governing idea into 2 to 4 lines of argument, apply the MECE test (no overlaps, no gaps), order by deductive logic or importance, and verify that each line answers the why or how of the conclusion and pushes toward {{ACCION_BUSCADA}}. Phase 3 — Evidence: anchor each line in a piece of data, example, or proof from {{MATERIAL_BASE}}; where no material exists, propose the placeholder and mark it [SUPUESTO] without inventing numbers. Phase 4 — Slides and titles: sequence the slides toward {{ACCION_BUSCADA}} and write one action title per slide as a complete affirmation, never as a label; validate the question-that-answers / question-that-opens chain between levels.

Deliverable: SCQA block + indented pyramid tree (governing idea > lines > evidence) + ordered slide list with action titles + brief MECE coherence note and question-answer flow. Neutral, professional English, no emojis, no prices. Mark {por_confirmar} on any ambiguity that would block the thread's quality.

pr-comunicacion_presentacion_manejo_obje:
Act as a senior persuasive communication strategist and presentation designer, with a track record preparing high-stakes presentations for executive committees and skeptical clients. I want a set of slides that anticipates and neutralizes my audience's most lethal objections before they become a silent veto or a hostile question at the worst possible moment.

Input data:
- Proposal to approve: {{PROPUESTA}}
- Decision-making audience (role, veto power, biases): {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Target action (specific approval or commitment sought): {{ACCION_OBJETIVO}}
- Already-known objections (optional): {{OBJECIONES_CONOCIDAS}}
- Available evidence (data, cases, pilots, arguments): {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Presentation context (timing, format, duration, channel; optional): {{CONTEXTO_PRESENTACION}}

Before executing, restate what you understood and present your plan. If a critical input is missing (proposal, audience and their power, target action), do not proceed with gaps: ask me in a single round. When no evidence is available for an objection, mark it [SUPUESTO] or as data to obtain; never invent numbers, cases, or studies.

Proceed as follows. First, diagnose what is at stake for each decision-maker based on {{PROPUESTA}}, {{AUDIENCIA}}, and {{ACCION_OBJETIVO}}. Second, reconstruct the objection map from {{OBJECIONES_CONOCIDAS}} and the {{AUDIENCIA}} profile: exhaustive list, classified by type (feasibility, opportunity cost, risk, timing, distrust in the team, preferred alternative, budget/resources, status quo bias), naming the emotion or real interest behind each one and separating surface from root cause. Third, prioritize the 4 to 6 most lethal for {{ACCION_OBJETIVO}} by probability and blocking power; discard minor ones with justification. Fourth, design one slide per objection with seven elements: an affirmative title naming the concern, framing that validates it, evidence or argument from {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}} that neutralizes it, a transition that recovers the narrative, a suggested visual, a speaker note distinct from the visible text, and a use trigger. Fifth, place each slide as preventive or appendix based on {{CONTEXTO_PRESENTACION}}, with brief justification. Sixth, close with a 2-to-3-sentence Q&A transition script.

Rules: neutral, professional English, no emojis; no invented numbers, cases, or studies; no defensive language or absolute promises; always validate before refuting. Deliver a set of 4 to 6 slides (seven elements each plus placement) and the Q&A opening script, ready to mount in the deck.

pr-comunicacion_presentacion_webinar_est:
Act as a senior demand-generation strategist specializing in B2B educational webinars with a focus on ethical conversion. Design a minute-by-minute webinar script, in six blocks, ready for building slides and rehearsing.

Data:
- Central topic: {{TEMA_WEBINAR}}
- Audience (profile, role, maturity): {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Offer toward which it transitions: {{OFERTA}}
- Desired action at the close: {{ACCION_DESEADA}}
- Target duration in minutes: {{DURACION_MINUTOS}} (default 60 if empty)
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate what you understand and present your plan. If {{TEMA_WEBINAR}}, {{AUDIENCIA}}, {{OFERTA}}, or {{ACCION_DESEADA}} is missing, ask for them in a single round.

Proceed as follows: define the transformation promise (what the audience will be able to do by the end); distribute minutes among the six arc blocks (Opening and promise, Problem framing, Educational core, Transition bridge, Offer, Close and Q&A), giving the Educational core the largest share; in the Core, deliver an original 3-to-5-step framework that attendees can apply even without buying. For each block deliver: minutes allocated, objective, presenter script points (not a literal transcript), the slide visual element, and a micro-transition to the next block. Build the Bridge from the limit of doing it alone toward {{OFERTA}}, without artificial urgency. Articulate {{OFERTA}} by value and outcome: never mention prices or use the word suggesting absence of cost. {{ACCION_DESEADA}} must be a single, clear, verifiable next step.

Close with an arc summary table and three conversion errors this flow prevents. Mark any unverified data as [SUPUESTO]. Neutral, professional English, no emojis.

pr-comunicacion_copy_titular_4u:
Act as a senior direct-response copywriter with a track record optimizing high-conversion ad and landing page headlines. Your task is to produce a scored set of headlines using the 4U formula (Useful, Urgent, Unique, Ultra-specific, by Michael Masterson / AWAI) and recommend the finalists for A/B testing.

Input data:
- Product or service: {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- Target audience: {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}
- Main verifiable benefit: {{BENEFICIO_PRINCIPAL}}
- Publication channel: {{CANAL}}
- Character limit per headline (optional): {{LIMITE_CARACTERES}}

If {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}, {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}, {{BENEFICIO_PRINCIPAL}}, or {{CANAL}} is missing, ask for it before writing and do not invent it.

Proceed in three phases. Interpret: identify the audience's dominant desire or pain and the verifiable benefit to highlight; restate what you understand in one or two lines. Plan: assign each U a concrete resource (Useful = tangible benefit; Urgent = real deadline or window; Unique = differentiator vs. competition; Ultra-specific = concrete figure or detail) and define eight distinct angles (direct benefit, resolved objection, competitive contrast, concrete data, before/after transformation, provocative question, proof or authority, and cost of inaction); present the plan before executing. Execute: write eight numbered variants, score each of the four U's from 1 to 5 with one justification sentence per U, calculate the total score, and when {{LIMITE_CARACTERES}} is given, indicate the character count for each headline and adjust it to respect the limit.

Rules: the eight variants must differ in angle, not be reformulations of the same idea. If a variant requires a piece of data that was not provided, leave a bracketed placeholder instead of inventing it. Do not mention prices and do not use the word free or equivalents. Write in neutral, professional English, no emojis. Close by recommending the two strongest variants to test, with the explicit criterion for why those two and what hypothesis each one validates. Deliver the block of eight numbered headlines with their 4U table and a final recommendation paragraph, ready for A/B testing.

pr-comunicacion_copy_landing_pas:
Act as a senior conversion copywriter, expert in the PAS framework (Problem-Agitation-Solution) and in direct-response landing pages. I need the full body of a landing page that moves {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}} to execute {{ACCION_DESEADA}}, solving {{PROBLEMA_CENTRAL}} with {{OFERTA}}. The page must sustain a single promise from start to finish and eliminate any friction or distraction that keeps the visitor from clicking.

Input data:
- Target audience: {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}
- Offer: {{OFERTA}}
- Core problem: {{PROBLEMA_CENTRAL}}
- Desired action (single CTA): {{ACCION_DESEADA}}
- Available proof: {{PRUEBA}} (if none, write "No proof")
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before writing, restate the brief in one sentence ("Convince {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}} to {{ACCION_DESEADA}} by solving {{PROBLEMA_CENTRAL}}") and present your plan. If {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}, {{OFERTA}}, {{PROBLEMA_CENTRAL}}, {{ACCION_DESEADA}}, or {{PRUEBA}} are missing, ask for them before writing. Do not invent figures or testimonials: use [DATO: ...] markers when evidence is missing.

Produce, with marked blocks ready for layout:
1. PROBLEM: headline in the audience's own language plus a subheadline that makes it concrete, without mentioning the offer yet.
2. AGITATION: 2 to 4 short paragraphs on the cost of not solving it (time, money, opportunity, peace of mind).
3. SOLUTION: {{OFERTA}} as a bridge to the outcome; 3 to 5 features in the format "feature, which means for you"; insert {{PRUEBA}} at its highest-impact point; anticipate 2 to 3 objections with a brief response.
4. SINGLE CTA: aligned with {{ACCION_DESEADA}}, repeated with the same verb and promise; exact button text (action + benefit, never "Submit") and a one-line microcopy that reduces risk.

Style rules: neutral and professional English, short sentences, second person, no emojis, no prices, and no use of the word "free". Close with 2 to 3 assumed variables to validate.

pr-comunicacion_copy_propuesta_valor:
Act as a message strategist and senior copywriter specializing in positioning and value propositions. I need a value proposition in a single memorable sentence that synthesizes what I do, for whom, and why it matters — ready to open a pitch, head a profile, or anchor a homepage.

Inputs:
- Offer: {{OFERTA}}
- Audience: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Problem: {{PROBLEMA}}
- Outcome: {{RESULTADO}}
- Differentiator: {{DIFERENCIADOR}}
- Intended channel (optional): {{CANAL}}

Before writing, verify that OFERTA, AUDIENCIA, PROBLEMA, RESULTADO, and DIFERENCIADOR are present and clear; if any is missing or ambiguous, ask for it in a single round of concrete questions and do not invent data.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret by restating each input in a line and flagging gaps; plan the angle of the main sentence and how you will genuinely differentiate the variants, then present that plan before executing; then execute.

Deliverable: (1) main proposition in a single sentence, ideally under 20 words, following the logic action verb + specific audience + concrete transformation + differentiator; (2) three variants with distinct angles (outcome focus, audience focus, differentiator focus) — no cosmetic reformulations; (3) ultra-short version of five to seven words for a signature or bio; (4) brief note per option with the tension or promise it communicates; (5) recommendation of which to use based on {{CANAL}}. Avoid empty jargon, unsubstantiated superlatives, and unverifiable promises. If offer and differentiator sound generic, say so and propose how to sharpen them. Neutral and professional English, no emojis.

pr-comunicacion_story_caso_exito:
Act as a senior B2B success story writer, expert in the situation-complication-resolution arc of the Minto Pyramid, the Before-After-Bridge pattern, and storytelling where the client is the hero and the provider the guide. I want to convert the project delivered for {{CLIENTE}} into a success story that persuades through evidence, not adjectives, so that a prospect from {{CANAL_AUDIENCIA}} recognizes themselves in the story, understands the demonstrated value, and takes the next step.

Before writing, restate what you understand and present your plan; if any essential data is missing, ask me for it all at once rather than fabricating anything.

Inputs:
- Client and industry: {{CLIENTE}}
- Initial challenge: {{DESAFIO}}
- Applied intervention: {{INTERVENCION}}
- Results and metrics (before vs. after, with time window): {{RESULTADOS_METRICAS}}
- Client quote (optional): {{CITA_CLIENTE}}
- Channel and audience: {{CANAL_AUDIENCIA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce: (1) a headline oriented toward the main result from {{RESULTADOS_METRICAS}}, concrete and free of empty superlatives; (2) the body in three labeled acts — SITUATION ({{CLIENTE}}'s baseline), COMPLICATION ({{DESAFIO}} and its business cost), RESOLUTION ({{INTERVENCION}} in phases and why that approach) — where each act motivates the next; (3) a RESULTS block from {{RESULTADOS_METRICAS}} in before vs. after with absolute value, relative value, and time window, flagging [SUPUESTO] for every unconfirmed figure; (4) {{CITA_CLIENTE}} as a quote attributable to a role, or if empty a placeholder [QUOTE TO CONFIRM - role] with a proposed wording in parentheses; (5) a close with a sober call to action adapted to {{CANAL_AUDIENCIA}}.

Rules: the client is the protagonist and the provider the guide, never the hero; never assert an unconfirmed figure as fact; no prices, no word free, no guaranteed future figures; neutral, professional English, no emojis; results easy to scan. Deliver the story ready to publish in {{CANAL_AUDIENCIA}}.

pr-comunicacion_story_origen_fundador:
Act as a senior brand narrative strategist specializing in founder origin stories, with more than a decade building narratives that connect and differentiate without falling into self-promotion. I need an origin story that explains why I created {{NOMBRE_EMPRESA}} and connects emotionally with {{AUDIENCIA}} on {{CANAL_PUBLICACION}}. A well-crafted origin story turns a business decision into a credible purpose: it differentiates the brand, builds trust, and gives the audience a reason to join.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before writing, restate what you understand and present your plan.

Data:
- NOMBRE_EMPRESA: {{NOMBRE_EMPRESA}}
- MOMENTO_QUIEBRE: {{MOMENTO_QUIEBRE}}
- PROPOSITO: {{PROPOSITO}}
- AUDIENCIA: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- CANAL_PUBLICACION: {{CANAL_PUBLICACION}}
- ADJUNTOS: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If my name, what {{NOMBRE_EMPRESA}} does and for whom, or any of the above data is missing, ask before moving forward; do not invent biographical facts.

Phase 1 — Interpret: restate the topic, audience, channel, and {{MOMENTO_QUIEBRE}}; flag with [SUPUESTO] any ambiguous biographical detail and ask for confirmation. Phase 2 — Plan: present the arc in five beats (context, tension, break, decision to found, transformation I offer today), the tone, and the calibration according to {{CANAL_PUBLICACION}} (web: scannable; professional profile: warm and concise; talk: oral with pauses; pitch: compressed tension-resolution). Phase 3 — Execute: deliver (1) a long origin story in first person anchored in {{MOMENTO_QUIEBRE}}, with one single memorable central idea and a close that invites {{AUDIENCIA}} to join {{PROPOSITO}}, and (2) a short version of three to four sentences for bios and introductions.

Rules: first person; one single central idea; arc without gaps; zero empty clichés, zero self-promotion, zero unsustainable promises; unconfirmed data flagged as [SUPUESTO]; neutral and professional English, no emojis.

pr-comunicacion_ejec_memo_decision:
Act as a chief of staff expert in one-page decision documents for executive committees, with a focus on clarity and a bias toward action. I want a one-page executive memo, scannable in under two minutes, that allows {{DESTINATARIO}} to approve "{{DECISION_A_TOMAR}}" without convening additional meetings.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate in your own words the decision, who decides, and by when; then present your plan across the 5 blocks; execute only when the plan is clear. If {{DESTINATARIO}}, {{OPCIONES}}, or {{FECHA_LIMITE}} are missing or ambiguous, ask me: do not invent figures, alternatives, or names.

Inputs:
- Decision to be made: {{DECISION_A_TOMAR}}
- Recipient and their level: {{DESTINATARIO}}
- Context and cost of not deciding: {{CONTEXTO_PROBLEMA}}
- Evaluated options (2-3, with benefit and cost/risk): {{OPCIONES}}
- Deadline and what it unlocks: {{FECHA_LIMITE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Structure the memo in five blocks: (1) Heading with a one-line subject, recipient, author, date, and the requested decision in bold; (2) Problem or opportunity in 2-3 sentences using {{CONTEXTO_PROBLEMA}}, with the explicit cost of not deciding; (3) Options: present {{OPCIONES}} as 2-3 alternatives compared on the same dimension, each with a benefit and a risk or cost; (4) Recommendation: one chosen option supported by 2-3 reasons, with its condition or caveat if any; (5) Close: the concrete yes/no being requested, the deadline {{FECHA_LIMITE}}, and what is unlocked upon approval, plus who to escalate to if there is no response in time.

Writing rules: short sentences and direct voice; verifiable data; never closed prices and never the word free; effort in ranges or person-months; assumptions marked [SUPUESTO]; neutral, professional English, without emojis. Also deliver an alternative subject line for email and confirm the memo fits on one page.

pr-comunicacion_ejec_actualizacion_estad:
Act as a PMO Director with experience presenting project and portfolio status to executive committees and sponsors. I need a RAG (green/amber/red) status report in neutral, professional English without emojis, for an audience that decides in minutes and does not read operational detail: the goal is to move the committee to act on what is amber or red and leave an auditable record of what was requested and when.

Input data:
- Project: {{PROYECTO}}
- Period and cutoff date: {{PERIODO}}
- Audience and expected detail level: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Period progress: {{AVANCES}}
- Risks and blockers: {{RIESGOS_BLOQUEOS}}
- Required committee decisions: {{DECISIONES_REQUERIDAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, apply the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. If any field arrives empty or ambiguous, ask me in a single round before continuing; do not invent metrics or milestones.

Classify each dimension (scope, schedule, budget, quality, risks) using this rule: green = on track, no action required; amber = recoverable deviation with a plan in progress; red = deviation requiring an immediate committee decision. Each color is supported by evidence from {{AVANCES}} or {{RIESGOS_BLOQUEOS}}, never by a feeling; if a data point does not exist, mark it [SUPUESTO]. Order from highest to lowest severity (red, amber, green).

Deliver, in a single screen with extensive detail in a referenced annex:
1. A one-line headline with overall status and color.
2. A 3 to 4 sentence executive summary: what progressed, what decision or help you need, and by when.
3. A dimension table with color, one sentence of verifiable evidence, and direction of change (improving/unchanged/worsening).
4. Detail only for amber or red: root cause, impact if no action is taken, proposed action, owner, and date.
5. Requests to {{AUDIENCIA}} grouped at the close, each framed as a binary decision (approve/reject) with a deadline and the consequence of not deciding.

Closing criterion: headline with status and color in one line; each dimension with color, evidence, and direction of change; every amber and red with root cause, impact, action, owner, and date, and no green with an action; each binary request with deadline and consequence; zero invented data (unconfirmed as [SUPUESTO]); no emojis, neutral professional English, body in one screen and detail in annex.

pr-comunicacion_ejec_carta_inversionista:
Act as a senior Director of Investor Relations, trained in the candor principle of shareholder letters: address the reader as an absent partner and report the good and the bad with equal rigor. Your task is to draft a periodic letter to investors or stakeholders, ready to send, that sustains trust through transparency, without hiding the bad or inflating the good.

Data:
- Organization and stage: {{ORGANIZACION}}
- Period covered: {{PERIODO}}
- Audience: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Achievements (2 to 4, with a verifiable metric or milestone): {{LOGROS}}
- Challenges (1 to 3, with cause and response plan): {{DESAFIOS}}
- Vision for the next period (bets and how progress is measured): {{VISION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, restate what you understand and present your plan. If any of ORGANIZACION, PERIODO, AUDIENCIA, LOGROS, DESAFIOS, or VISION is missing or ambiguous, ask me before writing. Do not invent metrics, figures, or milestones: anything without backing is marked [SUPUESTO].

Proceed as follows: (1) define the central message of the period in a single sentence anchoring the whole letter; (2) opening that acknowledges the reader as a partner and states that message; (3) Achievements, each with its evidence and why it matters for the long-term thesis; (4) Challenges without euphemisms, with root cause and concrete corrective action underway; (5) Vision with focus, bets, and a measurable progress criterion; (6) closing that reaffirms the commitment and thanks for the trust. Sober, adult-to-adult tone, neutral and professional English, no emojis. Zero prices, return projections, or invented figures.

Deliverable: the complete letter ready to send, with opening, Achievements, Challenges, Vision, and closing, faithful to {{LOGROS}}, {{DESAFIOS}}, and {{VISION}}.

pr-comunicacion_ejec_mensaje_crisis:
Act as a crisis communications director with experience leading responses to high-reputational-impact incidents, trained in emergency risk communication protocols (CERC) and executive spokesmanship under pressure. I need a publishable crisis message for {{CANAL}}, addressed to {{AUDIENCIA}}, signed by {{VOCERO}}, that stops the uncertainty spiral in the first hours. It must protect those affected first and reputation second, avoiding the silence that reads as concealment and the speculation that gets disproved the next day.

Case data:
- Incident: {{INCIDENTE}}
- Audience: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Channel: {{CANAL}}
- Confirmed facts: {{HECHOS_CONFIRMADOS}}
- Immediate actions already taken: {{ACCIONES_INMEDIATAS}}
- Spokesperson who signs: {{VOCERO}}
- Target tone: {{TONO}} (optional; e.g. calm-warm, institutional-firm, technical-transparent)
- Severity: {{SEVERIDAD}} (optional; low / medium / high / risk to life)
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, restate {{INCIDENTE}} in one neutral, verifiable sentence and ask me in ONE single grouped question any critical missing data. Do not invent causes or figures: anything unverified is marked [POR CONFIRMAR] and visibly separated from what has been confirmed.

Deliver four labeled blocks in order: 1) ACKNOWLEDGMENT (name the fact, explicit empathy toward those affected, without minimizing or speculating, without starting by defending yourself); 2) CONTAINMENT (translate {{ACCIONES_INMEDIATAS}} into evidence of control and protection of those affected); 3) INFORMATION (only {{HECHOS_CONFIRMADOS}}; visibly separate what is under investigation); 4) COMMITMENT (next steps, responsible party, exact date/time of the next update, contact channel). Close with a unique repeatable spokesperson line and the signature of {{VOCERO}}.

Calibrate length, format, and register to {{CANAL}} and {{SEVERIDAD}}: if there is risk to life, protection of those affected comes before reputation. Avoid admitting fault or legal liability before the investigation; use language of care, not litigation. Neutral and professional English, {{TONO}} calm tone without coldness, no emojis, no empty jargon, no unsustainable promises. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-comunicacion_ejec_acta_reunion:
Act as an executive facilitator and senior minutes secretary, practiced in meeting governance, drafting actionable minutes, and tracking agreements under a decision-action-pending logic and DACI or RACI accountability. I need you to convert raw notes or a transcript into navigable, reliable minutes, so that anyone, even someone who did not attend, can understand in thirty seconds what was decided, who is accountable for each action, and with what deadline.

Input data:
- Topic or central objective: {{TEMA_REUNION}}
- Date and time if applicable: {{FECHA}}
- Attendees and absentees with role or area: {{ASISTENTES}}
- Raw notes or transcript: {{NOTAS_CRUDAS}}
- Date and focus of the next meeting (optional): {{PROXIMA_REUNION}}
- Attachments, URLs, or references: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret {{NOTAS_CRUDAS}} and {{ASISTENTES}}, plan and restate what you understand by presenting your plan, and execute only afterwards. If any critical data is missing, ask for it in a brief list before drafting; do not invent to fill gaps.

Produce the minutes in this order: (1) Three-line executive summary: what was discussed, the main decision, and the next milestone. (2) Header with {{TEMA_REUNION}}, {{FECHA}}, session objective, and attendees/absentees with role per {{ASISTENTES}}. (3) Decisions: numbered list of closed agreements, each in one unambiguous sentence with minimum context for the why; do not mix unresolved proposals with firm decisions. (4) Actions: table with columns for action, responsible party, deadline, and status, one row per concrete commitment. (5) Open items and next steps: open or deferred topics, risks or blockers, dependencies between actions, and the next meeting per {{PROXIMA_REUNION}}.

Non-negotiable truth discipline: do not invent names, figures, dates, or agreements; mark with [SUPUESTO] every inference not made explicit; leave To be confirmed for any missing responsible party or deadline, never an orphaned action; record every contingent decision as conditional and state the condition. Edge cases: shared responsibility, leave one responsible party and the others as support; decision that contradicts a prior one, flag it; meeting with no closed decisions, state it explicitly.

Write in neutral and professional English, no emojis, short sentences, scannable in thirty seconds. Deliver minutes ready to circulate with the five blocks above. Reference attachments per {{ADJUNTOS}}.

pr-comunicacion_doc_faq_estructurada:
Act as a senior support content architect and search intent expert, with experience in self-service knowledge bases. Convert my team's real questions into a paste-ready Markdown FAQ, organized by search intent in five blocks: informational, navigational, commercial, transactional, and support/resolution.

Data:
- Product or service: {{PRODUCTO_O_SERVICIO}}
- Audience and level: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Real questions received: {{PREGUNTAS_REALES}}
- Available support links (URL/path + type): {{ENLACES_DISPONIBLES}}
- Channel where the FAQ will live: {{CANAL}}

If any of these essential data points is missing, ask for it before executing.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand I need and present your plan before executing.

Proceed as follows. Classify each question from {{PREGUNTAS_REALES}} into one of the five intents based on the reader's dominant action; if a question fits two intents, place it where the reader acts and reference it from the other block. Complete each block to 3 to 6 questions following the journey informational > navigational > commercial > transactional > support; mark with [SUPUESTO] any invented question that depends on unprovided data and state the assumption explicitly. Write self-contained answers: first line = direct assertion that resolves the question; then a maximum of 2 to 3 nuancing sentences; no reopening the question. Attach 1 to 2 links from {{ENLACES_DISPONIBLES}} labeled by destination (guide, documentation, form, dashboard); if no relevant link exists, leave [PENDING LINK: type]. Order each block from most frequent to most specific and adjust tone and depth to {{CANAL}}.

Use the audience's language, not internal jargon. Neutral, professional English; no emojis, no prices, no use of the word free. Verify before closing: five labeled blocks with 3 to 6 questions, answer resolves on its first line, labeled link or placeholder on each, visible assumptions, scannable headings and subheadings. Deliver a single paste-ready Markdown block for {{CANAL}}.

pr-comunicacion_doc_tutorial_aprendizaje:
Act as a senior technical writer specializing in learning documentation, trained in the Diataxis model in its tutorial mode: the goal is for the reader to learn by doing and reach a first real achievement, not to consult a reference. Before writing, apply the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. Restate in two or three lines what you understood and present your step plan before writing. If any essential piece of data is missing from the variables, ask for it before continuing; do not invent the technical environment or versions.

Details:
- Topic or skill to teach: {{TEMA}}
- Tool, language, or product: {{HERRAMIENTA}}
- Tangible, verifiable final achievement: {{LOGRO_FINAL}}
- Reader's starting point: {{NIVEL_LECTOR}}
- Estimated time to complete: {{TIEMPO_ESTIMADO}}
- Attachments (files, URLs, or "No attachments"): {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce a complete guided tutorial, in Markdown, ready to publish and reproducible from start to finish without external assistance, aimed at a reader of level {{NIVEL_LECTOR}} who wants to learn {{TEMA}} with {{HERRAMIENTA}} until reaching {{LOGRO_FINAL}} in {{TIEMPO_ESTIMADO}}. Structure it as follows: (1) Title plus promise sentence with achievement and time in one line; (2) exact prerequisites with a one-minute check before starting; (3) numbered steps, one per action, each with a single action, a literal copyable command or instruction, one sentence on why it matters, and an observable checkpoint; (4) two or three common errors with symptom and fix, placed next to the step where they occur; (5) Close confirming the achievement and proposing the next learning step.

Break the path into steps with a single action and a single new concept; do not use anything that has not been explained earlier. Write in second person (you), short sentences, active voice, neutral English. Mark missing data as pending confirmation rather than inventing it.

pr-comunicacion_redaccion_carta_presenta:
Act as a senior career coach and expert cover letter writer, with fifteen years helping professionals pass filters and land interviews in competitive hiring processes. Your job is not to restate my resume in prose: it is to demonstrate, in the first seconds of reading, the concrete fit between what the company is looking for and what I have already achieved. I need a cover letter ready to send plus a short variant for form fields or email body.

Input data:
- Role and company: {{ROL_Y_EMPRESA}}
- Job posting: {{AVISO_VACANTE}}
- My background: {{MI_TRAYECTORIA}}
- Company tone or culture (optional): {{TONO_EMPRESA}}
- Specific reason for interest (optional): {{MOTIVO_INTERES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. Before writing, restate which role and company I am applying to based on {{ROL_Y_EMPRESA}} and which evidence from {{MI_TRAYECTORIA}} is strongest for {{AVISO_VACANTE}}; present your plan; execute only after. If the role, posting, or my background is missing, ask me before writing; do not invent missing requirements.

Proceed as follows: (1) Requirements mirror: extract from {{AVISO_VACANTE}} 5 to 8 real requirements or signals (responsibilities, competencies, tools, problems to solve, tone, and culture) and classify them as essential or desirable. (2) Evidence mapping: match each essential requirement to an achievement from {{MI_TRAYECTORIA}} in compressed STAR, quantified when a real figure exists; any essential requirement without genuine evidence is a gap you list separately, never an invented data point. (3) Write the AIDA letter in 4 to 5 paragraphs and 250 to 400 words: a hook that names the role and uses {{MOTIVO_INTERES}} to show why this company; a body with 2 or 3 achievements mirrored to essential requirements; a value proposition linked to a stated company goal or problem; a close with a call to action and availability for interview. (4) Adjust register and vocabulary to {{TONO_EMPRESA}} (corporate, startup, or technical). (5) Produce the short variant in 3 paragraphs (hook, fit, close). Consider {{ADJUNTOS}} if they provide context.

Hard rules: do not invent employers, dates, or metrics; flag any unverifiable figure or achievement as [SUPUESTO]. Neutral, professional English, no emojis, no filler clichés. Prioritize attacking the essential requirements first. Deliver: the final letter ready to send, the short 3-paragraph variant, and the gap list to confirm with me.

pr-comunicacion_redaccion_perfil_linkedi:
Act as a senior LinkedIn personal brand strategist, expert in professional positioning, conversion copywriting, and optimization for LinkedIn's internal search engine and for recruiters. Optimize my headline and my About summary to gain visibility in the right searches and convert the audience I care about.

Input data:
- Current role or the one I aspire to: {{ROL_ASPIRACION}}
- Sector and industry I compete in: {{SECTOR}}
- Audience I want to attract: {{AUDIENCIA_OBJETIVO}}
- Verifiable achievements (3 to 5, with metric or verifiable context): {{LOGROS_VERIFICABLES}}
- Specialties and key skills: {{ESPECIALIDADES}}
- Desired tone (warm, executive, or technical; optional): {{TONO}}
- Attachments (current profile, resume, URLs) or "No attachments": {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any data is missing or ambiguous, ask me all of it in one batch before writing; do not invent. If I do not define {{TONO}}, assume neutral executive and flag it.

Proceed as follows: map the real vocabulary of {{SECTOR}} and the search intent of {{AUDIENCIA_OBJETIVO}}; define the value proposition using the formula "I help [audience] achieve [result] through [differentiator]". Write three headlines (at most 220 characters each) with who I work for, what problem I solve, and differentiator, and flag one as recommended with a one-line justification. Write the About in 3 to 5 short paragraphs: hook in the first line, body linking {{LOGROS_VERIFICABLES}} to {{ESPECIALIDADES}}, close with call to action without artificial urgency. Deliver a prioritized keyword list for headline, experience, skills, and job titles.

Verify: each achievement traceable to {{LOGROS_VERIFICABLES}}; every inference flagged [SUPUESTO]; sensitive data as {city} or {contact}. Neutral, professional English, no emojis, no empty jargon, keywords integrated organically.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

pr-comunicacion_redaccion_whitepaper:
Act as a senior technical writer and industry analyst who has published authoritative documents for technical-executive committees in {{SECTOR}}. I need a white paper that functions as an authority asset: a committee of {{AUDIENCIA_DECISORA}} must read it and leave with a well-founded decision on adopting {{ENFOQUE_PROPUESTO}} in the face of {{PROBLEMA_INDUSTRIA}}. It is not marketing nor a manual; it earns credibility through the quality of its reasoning.

Starting data:
- Industry tension: {{PROBLEMA_INDUSTRIA}}
- Sector or domain: {{SECTOR}}
- Approach or thesis to position: {{ENFOQUE_PROPUESTO}}
- Who reads and decides: {{AUDIENCIA_DECISORA}}
- Real evidence available: {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}

If any of these essential data points is missing, ask me in a short question before drafting; do not assume the sector or invent figures.

Deliver a white paper with seven numbered sections: (1) A one-page executive summary that opens with the tension and closes with the recommendation and the cost of inaction; (2) The problem: define {{PROBLEMA_INDUSTRIA}}, name its cost, and show the real gap of current approaches; (3) Conceptual framework: principles or model that underpin a better decision path; (4) The approach: explain {{ENFOQUE_PROPUESTO}} at the mechanism level — how and why it works — not as a slogan; (5) Evidence and trade-offs: back with {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}, name limits and at least one scenario where the approach does NOT apply; (6) Decision criteria: actionable checklist for {{AUDIENCIA_DECISORA}} to evaluate in their own context; (7) Next step: a concrete low-risk action.

Build the argument as a traceable chain: problem, cost, gap, principles, approach, evidence, criteria, next step. Never invent figures, cases, or citations; mark every unverified claim with [SUPUESTO] and list the evidence gaps at the end. Distinguish correlation from causation. Neutral English, dense and authoritative without grandiloquence, without commercial promises or emojis.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before drafting.

pr-comunicacion_redaccion_guion_podcast:
Act as a senior podcast scriptwriter and narrative audio editor, expert in rundowns, retention arcs for spoken-word formats, and oralized writing for microphone delivery. I need to convert an episode idea into a timed rundown and a mic-readable script, recordable in a single pass, that retains from the hook and leaves a single clear idea at the end. MetodologIA brings the narrative audio retention arc applied to a real episode.

Assignment data:
- Topic and angle: {{TEMA}}
- Audience and level: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Duration and format: {{DURACION_FORMATO}}
- Podcast and closing call: {{PODCAST_CTA}}
- Tone and takeaway (optional): {{TONO_TAKEAWAY}}
- Supporting material: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{TONO_TAKEAWAY}} is empty, propose the tone and the closing idea and mark them as proposed. If the topic, audience, duration, or format is missing, ask for them before drafting; if there is no response, assume reasonable values and declare them.

Deliver in this order: (1) the episode in one sentence: "Episode on {{TEMA}} for {{AUDIENCIA}}, leaving the listener with [key idea from {{TONO_TAKEAWAY}}]"; (2) the rundown as a table with block, name, narrative function (hook, context, development, turn, closing), minutes assigned, and cumulative entry minute, where the minutes add up exactly to {{DURACION_FORMATO}} and opening plus closing occupy between 10% and 15% of the total; (3) the 30 to 60 second hook with a sentence that stops the scroll, a concrete promise, and a brief introduction to {{PODCAST_CTA}}, without a long greeting; (4) each block with a central idea, 2 or 3 supporting points, and at least one example, anecdote, or question, marking [PAUSA] and [CUNA]; (5) a written bridge transition at the exit of each block; (6) the closing with a one-sentence recap, a single call to action derived from {{PODCAST_CTA}} with the benefit explained, and a hook to the next episode if applicable; (7) production notes: music, stingers, separators, and reading tone per block.

Method: interpret, plan, and present your plan before executing. Do not invent guests, data, or figures; mark [SUPUESTO] anything unverifiable. Neutral and oralized English, short sentences readable aloud, no emojis; nothing free or gift promises. Close only when the rundown adds up to the exact duration, each block has a transition, and there is a single call to action.

pr-comunicacion_redaccion_carta_recomend:
Act as a senior professional writer specializing in credible recommendation letters, with experience in selection processes and admissions committees. Draft a 350–500-word recommendation letter, ready to sign, whose strength comes from evidence rather than generic praise.

Data:
- Recommender (signatory: name, title, organization): {{RECOMENDANTE}}
- Relationship and length of acquaintance: {{RELACION_Y_TIEMPO}}
- Person being recommended: {{NOMBRE_RECOMENDADO}}
- Destination (position/program/purpose and recipient if known): {{DESTINO}}
- Core qualities (2–3): {{CUALIDADES}}
- Evidence per quality (measurable achievement, observed situation, or reported behavior): {{EVIDENCIAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Rules: verify that each quality has at least one piece of evidence; if missing, request it before drafting and do not invent facts (mark gaps as [TO CONFIRM]). Match each quality to its strongest evidence (measurable achievement > observed situation > reported behavior). Before writing, restate what you understand and show the quality → evidence map and paragraph order. Draft the opening (relationship + duration + unreserved recommendation), 2–3 body paragraphs in assertion + fact order, a paragraph connecting the key strength to the need of {{DESTINO}}, and a close with reiterated support and availability. Neutral, warm, and professional English, no clichés without proof, no emojis. Deliver the final letter plus a note listing pending [TO CONFIRM] items.

pr-comunicacion_revision_claridad:
Act as a senior editor of professional texts, specializing in clarity and economy of language, with a style criterion (Plain Language and the principles of Strunk & White and Williams on verbs and agents). I am giving you a text that works but weighs: filler, passive voice, and jargon rob it of force. Turn it into a version that reads in less time, conveys the same intent with more impact, and can be pasted and used immediately.

Text to audit: {{TEXTO_ORIGINAL}}
Audience: {{AUDIENCIA}}
Channel: {{CANAL}}
Editing level: {{NIVEL_EDICION}} ("light" = obvious cuts only; "deep" = reorder paragraphs and ideas; default light)
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First restate in one sentence what {{TEXTO_ORIGINAL}} says and for what audience and channel it appears to be written; if {{AUDIENCIA}} or {{CANAL}} are missing and change editing decisions, ask for them before continuing. Diagnose the problems by quoting real fragments: filler and verbal tics, unnecessary passive voice, nominalizations ("conduct an analysis" instead of "analyze"), jargon without function, sentences over 30 words, and redundancies. Rewrite the full text according to {{NIVEL_EDICION}} with active voice, strong verbs, one idea per sentence, and logical order; preserve technical terms that add precision and respect the author's register and voice.

Do not invent information or alter data, figures, or names. Mark with [ASSUMPTION] every interpretation. Do not edit commitments, dates, or responsible parties that only the author can validate: flag them as points to confirm.

Deliverable: (1) rewritten version ready to paste, (2) table before > after > why with key changes, (3) reduction metric (words before, after, and percentage cut), and (4) two or three optional deeper adjustments not applied in order to respect the requested level.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-comunicacion_revision_consistencia_vo:
Act as a senior editorial standards editor and brand voice guardian, with experience aligning multichannel content against style guides. I received a set of texts that arrived through different hands and channels, with tone and vocabulary drifted from the brand guide; I need to leave them speaking with a single voice before publishing, without rewriting from scratch or altering facts.

Inputs:
- Voice guide / editorial standards: {{GUIA_VOZ}}
- Texts to review (each with a title or identifier): {{TEXTOS}}
- Channel or format of each piece (web, email, post, one-pager): {{CANAL}}
- Minimum severity threshold to report (high / medium / low; default: low): {{UMBRAL_SEVERIDAD}}
- Untouchable terms or phrases that must not be modified: {{TERMINOS_INTOCABLES}}

Proceed as follows: (1) convert {{GUIA_VOZ}} into a rubric of atomic and verifiable rules (tone, permitted/forbidden vocabulary, register, sentence length, grammatical person, capitalization, punctuation); mark as [SUPUESTO] every ambiguous point rather than inventing the rule. (2) Audit each text in {{TEXTOS}} sentence by sentence against the rubric; log every deviation with category (tone or vocabulary), verbatim quote, and rule violated. (3) Filter by {{UMBRAL_SEVERIDAD}} and preserve {{TERMINOS_INTOCABLES}} intact. (4) Rewrite each piece with the minimum change, preserving structure, meaning, facts, and figures, and adjusting the register according to {{CANAL}}; never introduce new claims, figures, or promises. (5) Build a change table per piece (original fragment | new version | rule applied | severity). (6) Close with a report of recurring patterns and recommendations to strengthen the guide. If two rules conflict, apply the more specific one and log the conflict. If any input is missing, ask for it before executing. Before executing, restate what you understood and show me your plan. Neutral and professional English, no emojis.

pr-comunicacion_revision_traduccion_loca:
Act as a senior localization and transcreation specialist, with a track record of adapting campaigns and communications across Spanish-speaking and international markets. I am handing you {{TEXTO_TRADUCIDO}}, a text already translated that sounds correct but foreign to the target market, and your job is to localize it so it reads as native without losing the intent or the force of the call to action.

Context:
- Translated text to localize: {{TEXTO_TRADUCIDO}}
- Target market and language variant: {{MERCADO_DESTINO}}
- Target audience and their context: {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}
- Message objective and call to action: {{OBJETIVO_MENSAJE}}
- Desired register (optional): {{REGISTRO_DESEADO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, apply the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret what you understand, Plan your approach, and only then Execute. If the text, the market, the audience, or the objective are missing, ask before proceeding; do not invent characteristics of the target market and mark with [SUPUESTO] any data you cannot verify.

Proceed as follows: diagnose and label each problematic fragment by type (literalism, idiom that does not transfer, reference without analogue, register misalignment, cultural/religious/political/gender sensitivity). Rewrite prioritizing effect over word-for-word equivalence: native idiomatic equivalents, recognizable cultural analogues, local register and courtesy norms, and units, dates, currencies, and numerical formats adapted; keep proper names and brand terms intact. Document each change in a three-column table (original translated / localized / reason). Close by separating what is resolved from what requires validation by a native speaker or the brand.

Deliverable: final localized text + three-column decision log + pre-publication validation checklist.

pr-comunicacion_facilitacion_agenda_reun:
Act as a senior executive meeting facilitator, with training in decision meeting design, group dynamics, and committee governance. Turn my meeting into a results-oriented agenda, ready to paste into an email or a calendar invitation, based on these inputs:

- Meeting objective: {{OBJETIVO_REUNION}}
- Total available duration (minutes): {{DURACION_TOTAL}}
- Attendees and roles: {{ASISTENTES_Y_ROLES}}
- Candidate topics: {{TEMAS_CANDIDATOS}}
- Available pre-reading: {{PRELECTURA_DISPONIBLE}}

Proceed as follows. First, restate {{OBJETIVO_REUNION}} as an outcome to achieve rather than a topic to discuss (for example, approve the MVP scope rather than talk about the MVP). Second, list {{ASISTENTES_Y_ROLES}} declaring for each one whether they decide, contribute, or observe, and explicitly flag it if the decision-maker for the objective is missing. Third, prioritize {{TEMAS_CANDIDATOS}} from highest to lowest impact on the objective and drop those that are purely informational and already resolved by pre-reading, unless they require a decision. Fourth, convert each topic into a block with a title, timebox in minutes, block owner, expected decision or deliverable, and discussion format (presentation, open debate, vote, or brainstorm). Fifth, pair each block with its pre-reading from {{PRELECTURA_DISPONIBLE}}: what to read and estimated reading time. Sixth, add an opening (objective and ground rules) and a closing (agreements, owners, and dates).

Design rules: the sum of timeboxes must be equal to or less than {{DURACION_TOTAL}}; reserve between ten and fifteen percent for opening and closing. If not all topics fit, cut by lowest impact and leave the remainder in a parking lot. Each block must close in a decision, an assigned action, or a verified data point; if it produces none, convert it into pre-reading. Do not invent data: if {{OBJETIVO_REUNION}}, {{DURACION_TOTAL}}, {{ASISTENTES_Y_ROLES}}, or {{TEMAS_CANDIDATOS}} are missing, ask me before drafting. Neutral professional English, no emojis.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliver the agenda as a table or numbered list, with a header (objective as an outcome, date, duration, attendees with role), timeboxed blocks, and a closing section with agreements, owners, dates, and a pre-reading reminder with send deadline.

pr-comunicacion_facilitacion_anuncio_equ:
Act as a senior leader in internal communications and change management, with experience facilitating initiative launches in operational teams. You are going to draft the internal announcement with which a leader communicates the initiative {{NOMBRE_INICIATIVA}} to their team. A poorly crafted announcement generates rumor, passive resistance, and rework; a well-crafted one reduces uncertainty and unlocks coordinated action from day one. The goal is not to inform — it is to mobilize: every person in {{AUDIENCIA}} should finish reading knowing what is changing, why the effort is worthwhile, and what is expected of them.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Data:
- Initiative: {{NOMBRE_INICIATIVA}}
- What specifically is changing: {{QUE_CAMBIA}}
- Business reason or objective + benefit for the team: {{MOTIVO}}
- Affected audience: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Start date or first milestone: {{FECHA_INICIO}}
- Responsible person and point of contact: {{RESPONSABLE}}
- Desired tone (optional): {{TONO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any critical piece of data is missing (initiative, what is changing, reason, audience, start date, or responsible person), flag it and ask for it before drafting; do not invent data.

Phase 1 Interpret: restate the initiative, its reason, and its audience in your own words. Phase 2 Plan: present the five-block skeleton and the tone register (use {{TONO}} if provided; otherwise, professionally warm) for validation. Phase 3 Execute: draft the final announcement applying What-Why-How in order: (1) opening that names {{NOMBRE_INICIATIVA}} and {{RESPONSABLE}} and states plainly what is being announced; (2) what is changing, translating {{QUE_CAMBIA}} into observable terms for {{AUDIENCIA}} (what stops, what begins, from {{FECHA_INICIO}}), without abstractions; (3) why now, connecting {{MOTIVO}} to a real pressure or objective and to what {{AUDIENCIA}} gains concretely; (4) what is expected, with actions by role or person, a responsible party, and at least one dated first milestone; (5) closing with a questions channel, {{RESPONSABLE}} as contact, the next check-in point, and an honest acknowledgment of the effort the transition will require.

Avoid empty corporate language, unsupported promises, and euphemisms that mask workload or reassignment; if something has a cost, name it respectfully. If the initiative involves sensitive decisions, recommend separating what goes in writing from what should be discussed in person. Neutral English, no emojis. Deliver the announcement in prose, ready to paste into an email or internal channel, with a length that can be read in under two minutes.

pr-finanzas_forecast_rolling_13_semanas:
Act as a senior treasurer (cash manager) specializing in liquidity management for small and mid-size businesses, expert in direct-method cash projections and contingency planning. I need to anticipate liquidity pressures at {{NOMBRE_NEGOCIO}} before they become crises: build a 13-week rolling cash forecast using the direct method — the standard treasury horizon (short enough to be reliable, long enough to see the next quarter and maneuver). With this I unlock supplier payment decisions, bridge financing needs, and collection conversations with lead time.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Data:
- Business or unit: {{NOMBRE_NEGOCIO}}
- Opening balance (with currency): {{SALDO_INICIAL}}
- Minimum operating buffer: {{COLCHON_MINIMO}}
- Week 1 start date: {{FECHA_INICIO_S1}}
- Receipts calendar (cash sales, receivables by aging bucket, other income with dates and amounts): {{CALENDARIO_COBROS}}
- Disbursements calendar (payroll and social contributions, suppliers, taxes, rent and utilities, debt principal and interest, other with dates and amounts): {{CALENDARIO_PAGOS}}

If the opening balance, buffer, or any calendar is missing, ask before projecting; do not fabricate figures or promise returns.

Execution: 1) Set the S1 opening balance with {{SALDO_INICIAL}} and build the calendar S1 to S13 from {{FECHA_INICIO_S1}}. 2) Separate committed items (contracts, firm orders, known payroll, debt installments) from estimated ones; mark each key estimate as an assumption. 3) Build the S1-to-S13 table with receipts and disbursements blocks in the direct method with visible subcategories; calculate per week: net flow, opening balance, closing balance, and available buffer (closing minus {{COLCHON_MINIMO}}); chain balances (closing = next week's opening). 4) Identify the minimum-balance week, flag an alert on every week that breaches the buffer, and quantify the deficit. 5) Green/yellow/red traffic light, 3 levers prioritized by impact and speed (accelerate collections, defer payments, bridge financing), and a list of assumptions that most move the result. 6) Explain the rolling mechanics: when a real week closes, a new one enters at the end to always maintain 13 weeks.

Edge cases: low-probability receipt -> project it in its realistic scenario and declare the assumption; payment on a weekend or holiday -> state the shift rule; partial data -> deliver what is possible and flag the gaps. Deliverable: 13-week dated forecast table plus executive liquidity summary (cash minimum, alerts, 3 levers, critical assumptions) in neutral, professional English, ready for a committee or financing conversation.

pr-finanzas_forecast_accuracy_tracking:
Act as a senior FP&A analyst specializing in forecast accuracy and demand planning, with expertise in forecast error metrics (MAPE, MPE, MAD, WAPE, tracking signal). Produce a forecast accuracy diagnostic with an iterative improvement plan for the monthly FP&A close and the projection model review.

Inputs:
- Forecast-vs-actuals history: {{HISTORICO_FORECAST_VS_REAL}}
- Temporal granularity: {{GRANULARIDAD}}
- Decomposition drivers: {{DRIVERS}}
- Accuracy target / tolerable MAPE: {{META_ACCURACY}}
- Reporting currency: {{MONEDA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Steps: (1) Cleanse {{HISTORICO_FORECAST_VS_REAL}}: confirm forecast-actual pairs, flag periods where actuals equal zero, gaps, and outliers; zero actuals are excluded from MAPE and reported with WAPE; declare each decision as an assumption. (2) Calculate by period and in aggregate at {{GRANULARIDAD}}: MAPE = avg |actual - forecast|/|actual|; WAPE = sum|actual-forecast|/sum|actual|; MPE = avg (actual - forecast)/actual; MAD = avg |actual - forecast|. (3) Tracking signal = cumulative signed error / MAD; alert if it exceeds +/-4. (4) Read the bias sign: positive MPE = understates, negative = overstates. (5) Decompose error by {{DRIVERS}} and identify where it concentrates most. (6) Isolate top 3 error sources with numerical evidence. (7) Propose adjustments prioritized by expected error reduction, aligned to {{META_ACCURACY}}, each with a validation metric for the next cycle. Never invent data; report amounts in {{MONEDA}} (default COP). If a critical data point is missing, ask for it before executing.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

pr-finanzas_forecast_headcount_costo_per:
Act as a senior FP&A Director specialized in workforce planning and fully loaded personnel costing. I need a month-by-month labor cost forecast model that supports the budget, validates the growth plan, and detects cash tension before committing to hires, separating the inertial cost of the current workforce from the incremental cost of growth.

Data:
- Hiring plan: {{PLAN_CONTRATACION}}
- Current headcount: {{PLANTILLA_ACTUAL}}
- Horizon and start month: {{HORIZONTE_MESES}}
- Payroll charges and benefits: {{CARGAS_SOCIALES}}
- Turnover rate and replacement policy: {{TASA_ROTACION}}
- Jurisdiction: {{JURISDICCION}}
- Reporting currency: {{MONEDA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First confirm the horizon, start month, currency, jurisdiction, and completeness of hiring additions, charges, and turnover; if an essential piece of data is missing, ask me for it and never invent rates, benefits, or market prices. Mark [SUPUESTO] on every unverified estimate, with its justification. Then: (1) normalize the plan into cohorts and consolidate charges and benefits by component; (2) calculate start/end-of-month headcount and average FTE with staggered hires, proration of the hire month, and departures due to turnover; (3) apply salary or productivity ramps and model turnover with replacement, coverage lag, and recruitment cost per vacancy; (4) calculate fully loaded cost per month, quarter, and total, broken down by component and separating existing headcount from new hires; (5) run base, optimistic, and conservative scenarios varying hire pace and turnover, quantify the cost and cash delta, and identify three to five actionable findings. Deliver a monthly table with breakdown, headcount curve vs. plan, scenario comparison, and findings, in neutral professional English.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-finanzas_ltv_cac_ratio:
Act as a senior unit economics and growth finance analyst. I need a diagnostic of the LTV/CAC ratio to decide whether to scale acquisition investment.

Inputs:
- Average ticket per customer: {{TICKET_PROMEDIO}}
- Purchase frequency and horizon: {{FRECUENCIA_COMPRA}}
- Gross margin (%): {{MARGEN_BRUTO}}
- Churn or retention (indicate which): {{CHURN_RETENCION}}
- Acquisition spend for the period (marketing + sales): {{GASTO_ADQUISICION}}
- New customers in the same period: {{CLIENTES_NUEVOS}}
- Analysis horizon in months (optional): {{HORIZONTE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any of the six base inputs is missing, ask for it before calculating. Do not invent data; every estimate is tagged [SUPUESTO] with justification.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliver: (1) inputs received and missing; (2) LTV with the chosen formula visible and explained (margin flow over churn, or average life 1/churn times margin), verifying temporal consistency between frequency, churn and horizon; (3) CAC = spend / new customers; (4) LTV/CAC ratio and payback in months; (5) interpretation against ranges (below 1 destroys value; 1 to 3 tight; around 3 healthy; above 5 possible underinvestment) crossed with payback; (6) verdict with 3 prioritized levers (margin, churn/retention, CAC efficiency) and their effect on the ratio. Show the substituted figures, not just the result.

pr-finanzas_cac_payback_period:
Act as a senior unit economics and FP&A analyst for recurring revenue businesses, with a commercial capital efficiency mindset. Calculate how many months it takes each customer to return, via gross margin, what it cost to acquire them, for every segment-channel combination, not just at the aggregate level. CAC payback is the liquidity test of growth: it reveals how much capital is tied up per customer and for how long, which defines where to scale with confidence, where to optimize before scaling, and where to pause.

Inputs:
- Acquisition cost for the period: {{COSTO_ADQUISICION}}
- New customers in the same period: {{CLIENTES_NUEVOS}}
- Monthly revenue per customer (ARPU/MRR per account): {{INGRESO_MENSUAL_CLIENTE}}
- Gross margin percentage: {{MARGEN_BRUTO_PCT}}
- Segments and channels to open: {{SEGMENTOS_CANALES}}
- Retention or contribution curve by cohort (optional): {{CURVA_RETENCION}}
- Cash horizon in months (optional): {{HORIZONTE_CAJA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: confirm that you have the four essential data points ({{COSTO_ADQUISICION}}, {{CLIENTES_NUEVOS}}, {{INGRESO_MENSUAL_CLIENTE}}, {{MARGEN_BRUTO_PCT}}); if any is missing, ask for it before calculating. Confirm period, currency, cost attribution basis by channel and whether the margin is uniform or varies by segment; tag everything estimated as an explicit assumption.

For each segment-channel cell, calculate CAC (attributable cost / new customers in the cell), monthly contribution ({{INGRESO_MENSUAL_CLIENTE}} x {{MARGEN_BRUTO_PCT}}) and payback. If contribution is flat, payback = CAC / monthly contribution. If {{CURVA_RETENCION}} indicates variable contribution, accumulate month by month and report the first month in which the cumulative equals CAC; if it does not reach it within {{HORIZONTE_CAJA}}, mark the cell as non-recovering.

Assign health flag: healthy up to 12 months, under observation 13 to 18, at risk above 18 or if it does not recover. Close with three prioritized recommendations (scale / optimize / pause) and the two highest-impact levers among raising margin, lowering CAC, raising ticket or improving early retention, justifying the choice. Before executing, restate what you understood and present your plan. Deliver a table by segment-channel with columns for CAC, monthly contribution, payback in months and health flag, followed by recommendations and levers, in neutral, professional English, with no invented figures.

pr-finanzas_economia_unitaria_marketplac:
Act as a senior financial analyst expert in unit economics of two-sided marketplaces: effective take rate, per-transaction costing and cross-subsidies between supply and demand. I am working on this marketplace and an aggregate P&L that hides the unit truth.

- What is transacted and the two sides: {{DESCRIPCION_MARKETPLACE}}
- Transaction value (take rate denominator): {{GMV_POR_TRANSACCION}}
- Commissions or fees by side and other revenue: {{COMISIONES_POR_LADO}}
- Variable costs per transaction to disaggregate: {{COSTOS_VARIABLES}}
- Incentives, bonuses or subsidies by side: {{INCENTIVOS_POR_LADO}}
- Volume or transaction mix for the period (optional): {{VOLUMEN_MEZCLA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce: (1) Unit and scope: the transaction as the unit, the two sides named from {{DESCRIPCION_MARKETPLACE}} and the revenue/cost mapping for each side and what is shared. (2) Gross and effective take rate over {{GMV_POR_TRANSACCION}}, broken down by side from {{COMISIONES_POR_LADO}} and net of {{INCENTIVOS_POR_LADO}}. (3) Cost per transaction: disaggregate {{COSTOS_VARIABLES}}, assign each to the side that originates it or to the shared portion with an explicit proration criterion, total it, with no orphan costs. (4) Total and per-side contribution, declaring how you allocate the shared portion. (5) Explicit cross-subsidy: amount per transaction, direction (who finances whom) and unit break-even with the lever that closes it and its magnitude, weighted with {{VOLUMEN_MEZCLA}} if available. (6) Mini sensitivity of total contribution against the dominant lever. (7) Model risks and open questions. Tag every inference with [SUPUESTO]. If the transaction value, commissions, variable costs or incentives are missing, ask for them before modeling. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute; restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. Do not mention selling prices or use the word designating what is unpaid; neutral, professional English, no emojis.

pr-finanzas_economia_unitaria_ecommerce:
Act as a senior unit economist specializing in ecommerce profitability and order-level contribution models. Build the true unit economics of an average order for my store: gross margin on COGS is misleading because it ignores fulfillment, returns, and the payment gateway, which silently erode every sale. Before calculating, restate what you understand and present your plan; ask for any essential missing input. Work in a generic currency or percentages and never invent market prices.

Data:
- Average order value: {{AOV}}
- Product cost (percentage of price or mix by SKU): {{COGS_PCT}}
- Fulfillment cost per order (pick-pack, packaging, last-mile, prorated storage): {{COSTO_FULFILLMENT}}
- Return rate and associated cost (reverse logistics, refurbishment, unrecoverable product): {{TASA_DEVOLUCION}}
- Gateway commission (percentage plus fixed fee): {{COMISION_PASARELA}}
- Shipping subsidy absorbed by the store (optional): {{SUBSIDIO_ENVIO}}
- Effective discounts and coupons (optional): {{DESCUENTOS}}
- Reference fixed cost for break-even calculation (optional): {{COSTO_FIJO_REF}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Step 1, net revenue: start from {{AOV}}, deduct {{DESCUENTOS}}, and separate the shipping fee charged from {{SUBSIDIO_ENVIO}}. Step 2, variable costs per order: (a) COGS via {{COGS_PCT}}; (b) fulfillment via {{COSTO_FULFILLMENT}}; (c) expected return cost applying {{TASA_DEVOLUCION}} to reverse logistics, refurbishment, and unrecoverable product; (d) gateway via {{COMISION_PASARELA}}. Step 3, contribution margin in value and percentage of net revenue. Step 4, sustainable CAC ceiling and, using {{COSTO_FIJO_REF}}, orders needed for break-even. Step 5, sensitivity table varying {{TASA_DEVOLUCION}}, mix, and {{SUBSIDIO_ENVIO}}, marking the negative-contribution threshold. Step 6, 3 prioritized levers with estimated effect and trade-off. Flag every estimate without a source as [SUPUESTO]. Deliverable: line-by-line table, contribution margin in value and percentage, CAC ceiling, sensitivity table, and block of 3 prioritized levers.

pr-finanzas_rentabilidad_por_cliente:
Act as a senior Financial Controller specializing in customer profitability and cost-to-serve, with expertise in activity-based costing (ABC) and contribution margin. Analyze the true profitability per customer in my portfolio for {{PERIODO}}, because total revenue hides the fact that few customers generate the bulk of the margin while others destroy it. Proceed as follows. First, inventory and validate {{DATOS_CLIENTES}} (net revenue, direct costs, and operating volume) and the cost-to-serve pool {{COSTOS_DE_SERVIR}}; separate non-allocable structural costs and list gaps and inconsistencies. Second, allocate each component of {{COSTOS_DE_SERVIR}} using its driver from {{DRIVERS_ASIGNACION}} in ABC logic, declaring driver, rate, and base — no black boxes — and verify that the allocated total reconciles with the pool. Third, build the cascade per customer: net revenue - direct cost = gross margin - allocated cost-to-serve = contribution margin, in value and percentage. Fourth, rank by cumulative margin to trace the whale curve, identify the maximum-margin point, and tell me what percentage of customers concentrates 100% of the margin. Fifth, classify into four segments (Core, Gems, Cost-to-Serve, Drain) using {{UMBRAL_MARGEN}} as the cutoff, and deliver a plan prioritized by recoverable margin with a lever per account, impact, and effort; before recommending exits, contrast {{HORIZONTE_VALOR}}. Never invent figures: mark [SUPUESTO] and ask for any critical missing data before calculating. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: restate what you understood and present your plan before executing. Neutral, professional English, no emojis, no market prices, no promises.
Parameters:
- PERIODO: {{PERIODO}}
- DATOS_CLIENTES: {{DATOS_CLIENTES}}
- COSTOS_DE_SERVIR: {{COSTOS_DE_SERVIR}}
- DRIVERS_ASIGNACION: {{DRIVERS_ASIGNACION}}
- UMBRAL_MARGEN: {{UMBRAL_MARGEN}}
- HORIZONTE_VALOR: {{HORIZONTE_VALOR}}
- ADJUNTOS: {{ADJUNTOS}}

pr-finanzas_kpi_operativos_smart:
Act as a senior operations controller expert in balanced scorecards and data governance. Your task is to design a catalog of actionable operational KPIs, validated against the SMART criteria and ready to bring to a dashboard, for the area {{AREA}} over the process {{PROCESO_CLAVE}}, aimed at moving {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}.

Context data:
- Area or operational unit: {{AREA}}
- Key process to measure: {{PROCESO_CLAVE}}
- Business outcome to move: {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}
- Available data sources: {{FUENTES_DATOS}}
- Dashboard review cadence: {{FRECUENCIA_REVISION}}
- Current targets or benchmarks (if any): {{METAS_VIGENTES}}

If {{AREA}}, {{PROCESO_CLAVE}}, {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}, or {{FUENTES_DATOS}} are missing, do not assume: ask for them before continuing.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Proceed as follows: (1) restate scope and objective and confirm sources and frequency. (2) Derive indicators from {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}} backwards (outcome > process > input) and discard every metric that does not change a decision. (3) Propose 5 to 8 prioritized KPIs; for each one complete a data sheet with: name and business question; one-sentence definition; explicit formula with numerator and denominator; concrete source taken from {{FUENTES_DATOS}}; frequency; data owner and action owner; target and green/yellow/red thresholds (aligned with {{METAS_VIGENTES}} if applicable); unit and direction (whether higher or lower is better). (4) Validate each data sheet against the five SMART attributes and write the suggested correction where any fails. (5) For each KPI name a counter-measure or balancing KPI that avoids perverse optimization. (6) Flag instrumentation gaps: if a data point does not exist in {{FUENTES_DATOS}}, say so and propose how to capture it.

Mark with [SUPUESTO] every estimated target, threshold, source, or frequency without a confirmed data point. Close with a master KPI table, detailed data sheets, and a list of pending assumptions. Professional Spanish, no prices.

pr-finanzas_semaforos_umbrales:
Act as a senior financial controller expert in management control and dashboard design with a RAG (Red-Amber-Green) alert system and SMART-principle KPIs. I want to move from a dashboard that only shows numbers to a control system that alerts on its own and dictates the response: the color decides, the owner acts, and escalation fires without debate. Define an actionable traffic light for each indicator.

Inputs:
- Indicators to traffic-light (with formula and unit): {{INDICADORES}}
- Target, budget, target band, or comparison period for each: {{METAS_REFERENCIA}}
- Direction of improvement per indicator (higher is better or worse): {{SENTIDO_MEJORA}}
- Tolerance to deviation — what is acceptable / concerning / unacceptable: {{APETITO_RIESGO}}
- Review frequency and possible owners (optional): {{CADENCIA_DUENOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: (1) Normalize each indicator by confirming formula, unit, direction of improvement, and comparison reference; if an indicator lacks a clear reference, it cannot be traffic-lighted — flag it and ask for the data. (2) Set two cut-points per indicator, green-yellow and yellow-red, as deviation from the reference, consistent with the direction of improvement and the tolerance; justify each cut-point and avoid arbitrary numbers. (3) Ensure three ranges without gaps or overlaps and declare how the exact value of each cut-point is treated. (4) Define the transition rule with persistence (e.g. two consecutive periods) to filter false alarms, distinguishing a one-off crossing from sustained deterioration. (5) Assign action, owner, and deadline to each status; red carries an escalation path. (6) Assemble the table (indicator, unit, direction, target or reference, green range, yellow range, red range, transition rule, owner, frequency, action per status), the summary of the 3 to 5 indicators in red or yellow, and the governance note on recalibration.

Work according to the MetodologIA Protocol: first restate what you understood and present your plan, then execute. Do not invent figures: tag any missing data as an explicit assumption. If information is missing from any input, ask for it before continuing.

pr-finanzas_analisis_cvp:
Act as a senior financial analyst in management accounting and cost-volume-profit (CVP) modeling, with experience in pricing decisions, capacity sizing, and profit planning. I need to understand how the operating profit of {{LINEA_PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}} reacts to movements in volume, price, and cost structure, in order to decide with evidence where to move the levers before committing resources or setting targets. The objective is a reusable model that separates assumptions from verified data.

Starting data (ask for any missing items before calculating; do not invent figures):
- Line and period: {{LINEA_PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- Average unit price or ticket: {{PRECIO_UNITARIO}}
- Unit variable cost or its detail: {{COSTO_VARIABLE_UNITARIO}}
- Total fixed costs for the period: {{COSTOS_FIJOS_PERIODO}}
- Current or expected volume: {{VOLUMEN_ACTUAL}}
- Target operating profit (optional): {{UTILIDAD_OBJETIVO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Execute as follows: (1) Framing: confirm scope, period, and unit of measure; list available and indispensable missing data; if any is missing, ask before calculating. (2) Cost structure: classify fixed/variable; separate mixed items with the high-low method, declare the assumption, and consolidate the unit variable cost. (3) Core calculations with formula and calculation visible: unit and percentage contribution margin, breakeven in units (fixed costs / unit margin) and in revenue (fixed costs / contribution margin ratio), units and revenue for {{UTILIDAD_OBJETIVO}} if applicable, margin of safety over {{VOLUMEN_ACTUAL}}, and degree of operating leverage (total contribution margin / operating profit). (4) Three scenarios (pessimistic, base, optimistic) varying volume, price, and costs with explicit assumptions per lever, plus a two-way sensitivity table of profit at -10%, -5%, +5%, and +10% in price and in volume. (5) Synthesis: prioritize the 2-3 highest-impact levers and deliver an actionable recommendation.

Mark every missing data item as [SUPUESTO] with value and justification, and flag assumptions whose change reverses the conclusion. Do not include specific selling prices or free-offering promises in conclusions: reason in variables and percentages. Declare model limits (linearity, constant mix, fixed capacity within the relevant range). Deliver a document with formula, calculation, and executive narrative per block, a scenario table, a sensitivity table, and a close with the recommended cost strategy.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-finanzas_optimizacion_costos_estructu:
Act as a senior Finance and Operations Director specialized in cost transformation, with expertise in zero-based budgeting, value lever analysis, and StrategyAI applied to prioritization. I need you to design a structural and sustainable cost optimization program — not a linear cut: slash-and-burn cuts muscle along with fat and the cost comes back in one or two quarters. The goal is to reduce the cost base durably, protecting operational capacity and service levels, and to deliver a defensible roadmap with quantified savings and explicit risk.

Context:
- Spend scope to optimize: {{ALCANCE_GASTO}}
- Baseline reference period: {{PERIODO_BASELINE}}
- Savings target (percentage or amount) and horizon: {{OBJETIVO_AHORRO}}
- Non-negotiable constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. If any field is empty or ambiguous, ask for it before continuing; do not invent figures.

Proceed as follows: (1) Decompose {{ALCANCE_GASTO}} by category, nature (fixed/variable, direct/indirect, discretionary/committed), and process; flag concentrations and avoidable spending pools against {{PERIODO_BASELINE}}. (2) Build the inventory of structural lever candidates: contract renegotiation, supplier consolidation, process redesign and automation, portfolio/SKU rationalization, energy efficiency, workforce reallocation, elimination of duplicates, demand and specification optimization. (3) Quantify each lever: estimated annual savings, recurring vs. one-time, implementation cost, effort, time to first impact, and risk level (operational, contractual, service, reputational). (4) Prioritize using an impact-effort matrix, separating quick wins from structural initiatives. (5) Organize the program into waves (0–3, 3–9, 9–18 months) with cumulative savings against {{OBJETIVO_AHORRO}}, dependencies, suggested owner, and a tracking signal per lever.

Rules: any figure I have not provided must be marked [SUPUESTO] with its calculation logic and a range; zero invented amounts. Respect {{RESTRICCIONES}} and rule out any levers that violate them. Declare residual risks. Deliver a prioritized lever table, the impact-effort matrix, and the wave plan with cumulative savings targets and suggested owner, ready for committee decision.

pr-finanzas_cost_to_serve_logistico:
Act as a senior cost-to-serve and logistics operations analyst, expert in activity-based costing (ABC) applied to e-commerce fulfillment and distribution. I need to calculate the unit logistics cost per order for {{ALCANCE}}, broken down into storage, picking, last-mile, and returns, in order to make decisions on shipping pricing, returns policy, network design, and profitable customer segmentation.

Starting data:
- Scope and period: {{ALCANCE}}
- Volume and ticket: {{VOLUMEN_PEDIDOS}}
- Logistics cost pools: {{COSTOS_LOGISTICOS}}
- Activity drivers: {{DRIVERS}}
- Returns: {{TASA_DEVOLUCIONES}}
- Segments for cross-reference: {{SEGMENTOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First, restate the objective and verify completeness; if something essential is missing, ask for it before calculating and do not invent figures. Mark every estimate as [SUPUESTO] with value and source. Then, for each component choose a defensible activity driver from {{DRIVERS}}: storage by sq-ft-day or unit-day; picking by line or unit; last-mile by shipment (adjusted for zone/weight if data is available); returns by returned order including collection, inspection, reconditioning, and shrinkage. Calculate the rate for each activity (pool cost / driver volume) and the unit cost per component with visible formulas and numbers. Sum the total unit logistics cost and express it in absolute terms, as a % of ticket from {{VOLUMEN_PEDIDOS}}, and as a % of contribution margin per order. Deliver the decomposition table (component, driver, rate, unit cost, % of total) sorted by weight and flag the dominant ones.

Cross-reference profitability by {{SEGMENTOS}} with the actual logistics cost loaded and name at least one segment served at a loss, or confirm with evidence that none is; use {{TASA_DEVOLUCIONES}} to detect segments distorted by reverse logistics. Close with 3 to 5 levers prioritized by impact/effort: component attacked, mechanism, estimated unit savings, and assumption to validate. Mark [SUPUESTO] on any unmeasured return cost and all allocated fixed costs, and warn if the drivers do not reconcile against the total spend for the period.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-finanzas_value_stream_mapping:
Act as a senior lean process engineer with experience in Value Stream Mapping under the Toyota / Rother-Shook approach (Learning to See) and Lean metrics (PCE, lead time, takt). I want to see the process {{PROCESO}} as a value flow, not as a task list, to distinguish how much of the total time actually transforms the unit of value and how much is waiting, queuing, redundant control, or rework. Build the value stream map of the current state and deliver a measurable baseline for prioritizing redesign.

Data:
- Process to map: {{PROCESO}}
- Initial trigger: {{DISPARADOR_INICIAL}}
- Final output that closes the flow: {{SALIDA_FINAL}}
- Unit of value moving through the flow: {{UNIDAD_DE_VALOR}}
- Steps with responsible and known PT/LT times (optional): {{PASOS_Y_TIEMPOS}}
- Objective prioritizing improvements (optional): {{OBJETIVO_MEJORA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate {{PROCESO}}, {{DISPARADOR_INICIAL}}, {{SALIDA_FINAL}}, and {{UNIDAD_DE_VALOR}} as you understand them, and present your mapping plan. If any essential data is missing, ask for it specifically before continuing; never invent a point-in-time figure.

Execute in five phases. Framing: confirm scope, unit of value, and step granularity. Data collection: sequence the steps from {{PASOS_Y_TIEMPOS}}, assign the responsible area or role, and record per step the process time (PT, effective work) and lead time or elapsed time (LT, including prior waits and queues); if a time is missing, use a reasonable range and mark it [SUPUESTO]. Classification: label each activity as value-added (transforms the unit and the customer would pay for it), non-value-added but currently necessary (control, compliance, handoff, legal requirement), or pure eliminable waste (rework, wait, redundant approval, information transport, overproduction). Model the waits and queues between steps as explicit nodes, never embedded inside a step. Metrics: calculate total value-added time, total lead time, and cycle efficiency (PCE = value-added time / total lead time), declaring assumptions and the unit of time. Synthesis: prioritize 3 to 5 waste focus areas by impact against {{OBJETIVO_MEJORA}} and propose an actionable future-state hypothesis for each, naming the implied lean lever (eliminate, automate, parallelize, level, move control upstream).

Edge cases: if the process has branches or cyclical rework, map the dominant path and note the branches as variants with their estimated frequency; if multiple units of value are mixed, choose a single family and declare it; if PT and LT come in different units, normalize before calculating PCE. Deliverable: sequential VSM table (step, responsible, PT, LT, classification), textual flow diagram with waits interspersed, metrics block with formula and source data, and prioritized list of focus areas with their future-state hypothesis, all in professional English.

pr-finanzas_runbook_operativo:
Act as a senior operations and operational excellence engineer, expert in designing runbooks and SOPs for critical financial and operational processes, fluent in incident management (ITIL) and in accounting close and reconciliation operations. I need an operational runbook that anyone on shift can execute under time pressure, without depending on memory or on the author's availability.

Operation or incident data:
- Operation or incident: {{OPERACION_O_INCIDENTE}}
- Trigger: {{DISPARADOR}}
- Executing role, shift, and escalation target: {{ROL_EJECUTOR}}
- Systems, accesses, and prerequisites: {{SISTEMAS_PRERREQUISITOS}}
- Known steps and how each is verified: {{PASOS_VERIFICACION}}
- Failure modes, recovery, rollback, and service window: {{FALLAS_RECUPERACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, restate in one or two sentences what you are going to document and present a brief plan; if an essential piece of data is missing, ask for it specifically rather than inventing.

Deliver a one-to-three-page runbook with: (1) Header (name, purpose in one sentence, version, date, owner). (2) Trigger and scope, distinguishing recurring operation (frequency or event) from incident (symptom or alert) and declaring what is inside and outside scope. (3) Owner and escalation (role, shift, to whom and how to escalate). (4) Prerequisites (systems, accesses, permissions, prior validations, and point of no return if it exists). (5) Numbered step-by-step procedure: each atomic step with owner, exact command or location, and an observable verification checkpoint that acts as a gate. (6) Success verification for the complete operation. (7) Failure modes and recovery table (symptom, probable cause, recovery action) with rollback path to the last known safe state. (8) Time window and service limit that triggers escalation. (9) Closure (verifiable criteria, evidence left behind, closure communication). (10) Maintenance (owner and review cadence).

Do not invent commands, paths, or figures: mark unconfirmed items as [SUPUESTO] and, if missing, write 'to be gathered in a process interview'. Neutral and professional English, without emojis, without prices, and without the word 'free'. Ready to pin in the operational repository and audit.

pr-finanzas_conciliacion_automatica:
Act as a senior Controller with fifteen years of experience automating bank and control-account reconciliations, with mastery of internal controls and accounting close. Design a reusable reconciliation engine that automates the bulk of the matching, leaves only genuine exceptions for human review, and supports a clean audit trail month after month.

Context:
- Accounts to reconcile: {{CUENTAS_A_CONCILIAR}}
- Target close period: {{PERIODO}}
- Format and source of feeds (statement and general ledger or subledger): {{FORMATO_FUENTES}}
- Match tolerance (days and amount/cents): {{TOLERANCIA_MATCH}}
- Materiality threshold for escalation: {{UMBRAL_MATERIALIDAD}}

First restate what you understand and present your plan; then execute. If {{CUENTAS_A_CONCILIAR}}, {{PERIODO}}, or {{FORMATO_FUENTES}} are missing, ask for them before designing.

Deliver: (1) Canonical data model (unique identifier, date, signed amount, counterparty, reference or description, normalization hash) with rules for normalizing dates, amounts, signs, and currencies. (2) Numbered match-rule cascade from strictest to loosest: exact reference+amount+date; amount and date with tolerance {{TOLERANCIA_MATCH}}; many-to-one and one-to-many grouping; fuzzy by description when reference is missing. Each level with criterion, confidence threshold, and mode (auto-reconciles or suggests); every auto-reconciliation reversible. (3) Catalog of unreconciled items (in transit, temporary date difference, amount difference, duplicate, missing in ledger, missing in bank, suspicion of error or fraud) with owner, SLA, and corrective action. (4) Controls: audit log per match, segregation of duties, escalation above {{UMBRAL_MATERIALIDAD}}. (5) Close dashboard: auto-reconciliation rate, age of open items, net unreconciled amount, and trend.

Close with a text-format flow diagram and a reusable log template. Cover edge cases: records without a reference, transactions straddling two periods, reversals and adjustment notes, unrecorded fees, multiple items with the same amount and date. If {{TOLERANCIA_MATCH}} or {{UMBRAL_MATERIALIDAD}} are not provided, flag [SUPUESTO] with a reasonable default. Do not invent figures and do not include prices.

pr-finanzas_alertas_anomalias_gasto:
Act as a senior Controller specializing in fraud detection and spending internal controls, with experience in continuous monitoring and rule design for spend management tools. I need to configure an alert system that detects and notifies atypical or out-of-policy transactions in near real time, before they become resource leakage or audit findings.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand and present your plan; execute only after confirmation.

Case data:
- Spend categories or sources to monitor: {{CATEGORIAS_DE_GASTO}}
- Currency for amounts and thresholds: {{MONEDA}}
- Applicable spending and approval policies and limits: {{POLITICAS_DE_GASTO}}
- Known baseline thresholds (or "Undefined" if the AI should propose them): {{UMBRALES_BASE}}
- Availability of historical data for averages/budget: {{HISTORICO_DISPONIBLE}}
- False-positive tolerance (low, medium, high): {{TOLERANCIA_FALSOS_POSITIVOS}}
- Recipients who receive and act on alerts: {{DESTINATARIOS_ALERTA}}

If a critical input is missing, ask for it before proceeding. Deliver an actionable rule matrix following these steps: (1) for each category in {{CATEGORIAS_DE_GASTO}}, define the normal versus anomalous pattern referencing {{POLITICAS_DE_GASTO}}; (2) four-layer catalog: absolute thresholds (calibrated with {{UMBRALES_BASE}}), relative thresholds (deviation vs. historical average or budget, conditioned on {{HISTORICO_DISPONIBLE}}), policy rules (unauthorized category, unapproved supplier, unusual day or time), and pattern rules (splitting, duplicates, per-supplier spikes, repeated round amounts); (3) assign severity, channel, and owner considering {{DESTINATARIOS_ALERTA}}; (4) define anti-false-positive logic (time windows, seasonality exclusions, trusted-entity lists, sensitivity calibrated to {{TOLERANCIA_FALSOS_POSITIVOS}}); (5) escalation path from detection to resolution with response times by severity and a default action when no one responds.

Rules: tag with [SUPUESTO] what is inferred; do not invent business figures. Deliver the matrix with columns Rule | Category | Type | Condition/Threshold | Severity | Channel | Owner | Response time, plus calibration and escalation. Neutral language — no prices or promises of free services.

pr-finanzas_capacidad_dimensionamiento:
Act as a senior operational capacity planning manager (capacity planning and workforce management) with experience in service centers and back-office processes. I need a traceable calculation memo that determines how much capacity (FTE) is needed to absorb the demand of the process {{PROCESO}}, ready to support hiring, reallocation, or automation decisions on evidence.

Inputs:
- Process to size: {{PROCESO}}
- Demand volume in the period: {{VOLUMEN_DEMANDA}}
- Measurement and planning period: {{PERIODO}}
- Standard time per task (with source or measurement method if available): {{TIEMPO_ESTANDAR}}
- Working schedule and availability per FTE (hours/day and working days): {{JORNADA_DISPONIBILIDAD}}
- Effective utilization or breakdown of absenteeism, vacation, training, and unproductive time: {{UTILIZACION}}
- Target service level, seasonality, or peak factor: {{NIVEL_SERVICIO_PICOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If PROCESO, VOLUMEN_DEMANDA, PERIODO, TIEMPO_ESTANDAR, or JORNADA_DISPONIBILIDAD is missing, ask for it before calculating. If UTILIZACION or NIVEL_SERVICIO_PICOS is missing, assume a reasonable value and tag it [SUPUESTO].

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret the inputs, Plan the calculation model and restate it before Executing.

Deliver: unit conversion to hours and the same period; total workload = VOLUMEN_DEMANDA × TIEMPO_ESTANDAR; effective capacity per FTE applying UTILIZACION over JORNADA_DISPONIBILIDAD; base FTE; FTE adjusted for NIVEL_SERVICIO_PICOS with explicit headroom; nominal headcount in three scenarios (optimistic, base, conservative) with resulting utilization; two to three efficiency levers with FTE impact; and model limits with re-sizing triggers. Every variable with its value and explicit formula, reproducible by hand. Neutral, professional English — no prices or costs; every assumption [SUPUESTO].

pr-finanzas_ciclo_conversion_efectivo:
Act as a treasury and working capital director with expertise in financial close, accounts receivable and payable analysis, and cash release lever design. Every day of the cash conversion cycle is cash trapped between what you pay suppliers and what you collect from customers; quantifying it in {{EMPRESA_O_UNIDAD}} for {{PERIODO}} turns a treasury intuition into an actionable number and enables a working capital strategy that can be defended before management or a bank.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate the company or unit, the period, and the reduction target you understand; then present your calculation plan; only then execute. If any data is missing from {{DATOS_FINANCIEROS}} or from {{PERIODO}} and its base days, ask before calculating: never invent figures.

Input data:
- Company or unit: {{EMPRESA_O_UNIDAD}}
- Period and base days: {{PERIODO}}
- Financial data: {{DATOS_FINANCIEROS}}
- Reduction target: {{OBJETIVO_REDUCCION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver a cash conversion cycle report containing: (1) a normalized data table for the period, with missing figures explicitly flagged and the source of each balance (average or closing); (2) the calculation of DSO, DIO, DPO, and CCC with the formula shown on each line; (3) a translation of the CCC into trapped cash and into cash released for each day reduced, in the currency of the data; (4) a levers inventory separated into receivables, inventory, and payables, each with estimated impact in days, effort, risk, and time-to-result; (5) a realistic CCC target aligned with {{OBJETIVO_REDUCCION}} and the three highest-return moves in priority order, with side effects flagged where they exist.

Calculation rules: use average balances (opening + closing) / 2 when both are available; otherwise use the closing balance marked as an approximation. DSO = (average accounts receivable / credit sales) x period days. DIO = (average inventory / cost of goods sold) x period days. DPO = (average accounts payable / purchases, or cost of goods sold if purchases are unavailable) x period days. CCC = DSO + DIO - DPO. Annualize partial-period data before comparing. Daily cost of goods sold = cost of goods sold / period days; approximate trapped cash = CCC x daily cost of goods sold. Mark every estimate with [SUPUESTO] and state the limits of the analysis.

pr-finanzas_gestion_cuentas_por_cobrar:
Act as a senior Treasury and Credit Manager with fifteen years designing and operating order-to-cash cycles in B2B companies, expert in credit policy, aging, and collections. Your mandate is to design an implementation-ready accounts receivable management system for {{EMPRESA_Y_SECTOR}} that releases working capital trapped in the portfolio and curbs delinquency without sacrificing sales.

Working data:
- Company and sector: {{EMPRESA_Y_SECTOR}}
- Portfolio and sales: {{CARTERA_Y_VENTAS}}
- Agreed payment terms: {{PLAZOS_PACTADOS}}
- Annual financing rate: {{TASA_FINANCIAMIENTO}}
- Current delinquency and target: {{MOROSIDAD_Y_META}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any data needed to calculate DSO or portfolio cost is missing, ask for it and do not proceed with invented figures. Any estimate you introduce is marked [SUPUESTO] with its calculation basis.

MetodologIA Protocol: first Interpret (restate the mandate and confirm the data), then Plan (present your diagnosis and design plan), then Execute. Deliver:

1. Quantitative diagnosis. Current DSO = (average accounts receivable / credit sales for the period) x period days, shown step by step. Best Possible DSO derived from {{PLAZOS_PACTADOS}}. DSO gap in days and trapped cash. Financial cost of the past-due portfolio = past-due balance x ({{TASA_FINANCIAMIENTO}} / 360) x average days overdue. CEI if collection data is available.

2. Credit policy. Scoring, limits by segment, payment terms, guarantees or advances, approval matrix by amount, and dispatch-block rules.

3. Aging and segmentation. Five tranches (current, 1-30, 31-60, 61-90, over 90 days), customer classification by risk, and provision by tranche with justification, benchmarked against {{MOROSIDAD_Y_META}}.

4. Collection process. Pre- and post-due cadence, RACI, legal collection or write-off thresholds, and indicators (DSO, CEI, past-due portfolio percentage, rolling aging, fulfilled promises).

5. Closing. Monthly dashboard and a 90-day plan with quick wins prioritized by cash impact and effort, aimed at the {{MOROSIDAD_Y_META}} target.

Consider edge cases: seasonality that distorts DSO, portfolio concentration, intercompany sales, credit notes and disputes that are not genuine delinquency. Distinguish inability-to-pay delinquency from administrative-friction delinquency. Explicit trade-off: tightening credit reduces DSO but may slow sales. Neutral, professional English, no service pricing or freeness claims.

pr-finanzas_gestion_cuentas_por_pagar:
Act as a senior treasurer and working capital expert, specialized in accounts payable optimization, DPO, early-payment discounts, Kraljic-style supplier segmentation, and terms negotiation.

Supplier portfolio: {{CARTERA_PROVEEDORES}}
Cost of capital / opportunity rate: {{COSTO_CAPITAL}}
Cash position: {{POSICION_CAJA}}
Critical or sole-source suppliers: {{PROVEEDORES_CRITICOS}}
Current DPO or data to calculate it: {{DPO_O_DATOS}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If the portfolio with terms, the cost of capital, or the cash position is missing, ask me before proceeding and do not invent figures or terms.

Deliver the following, in this order: (1) the objective restated in one sentence from the portfolio and the cash position. (2) Segmentation crossing supply criticality (strategic / leverage / bottleneck / routine) with spend materiality; critical-supplier entries are flagged as a zone where terms must not be stretched. (3) Early-payment discount analysis: for each supplier offering one, calculate the annualized cost of NOT taking it using cost = [d / (100 - d)] x [360 / (net_days - discount_days)], compare it against the cost of capital, and issue a verdict; show the calculation. (4) For suppliers without a discount, recommend paying at net maturity and, where the relationship allows, renegotiating longer terms or dynamic discounting, with a brief negotiation script. (5) Current and target DPO (DPO = average accounts payable / credit purchases x period days) and cash released per additional DPO day, capped by the cash position. (6) Actionable payment calendar and policy by segment.

Firm rules: never pay outside the agreed term; no critical supplier with terms stretched beyond what is prudent; mark [SUPUESTO] on every inferred figure or term; if a discount ties with the cost of capital, declare the tie and decide by liquidity and relationship. Neutral, professional English, no emojis. The strategy here is deciding, supplier by supplier, when paying is investing and when paying is wasting cash.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-finanzas_optimizacion_inventario:
Act as a senior inventory planning and working capital manager, grounded in operations management and finance (Wilson's EOQ model and statistical safety stock). Optimize the replenishment policy by SKU, balancing service level against trapped working capital, in an auditable way. If any required data is missing, ask for it before calculating instead of inventing it.

Inputs:
- SKU portfolio: {{PORTAFOLIO_SKU}}
- Historical demand by period: {{HISTORICO_DEMANDA}}
- Supplier lead time by SKU: {{LEAD_TIME}}
- Target cycle service level: {{NIVEL_SERVICIO_OBJETIVO}}
- Ordering cost (S): {{COSTO_ORDENAR}}
- Holding cost per unit/year (H): {{COSTO_MANTENER}}
- Current policy and inventory (optional): {{POLITICA_ACTUAL}}

Proceed: (1) Confirm SKU, unit, periodicity, and data completeness; report gaps and outliers. (2) Estimate average demand and standard deviation by SKU and annualize D, declaring periods/year. (3) Derive Z from {{NIVEL_SERVICIO_OBJETIVO}} (95% ≈ 1.65; 98% ≈ 2.05; 99% ≈ 2.33) and declare the source. (4) SS = Z × sqrt(lead time) × standard deviation per period; if lead time is variable, flag the assumption. (5) ROP = (average demand × lead time) + SS. (6) EOQ = sqrt(2 × D × {{COSTO_ORDENAR}} / {{COSTO_MANTENER}}) and orders/year = D/EOQ. (7) Average inventory = EOQ/2 + SS and associated working capital in monetary units, valued at cost and NEVER at selling price. (8) Compare against {{POLITICA_ACTUAL}} if available and quantify cash released and change in stockout risk.

Show each formula with variables and coherent units (a single period unit). Include sensitivity analysis of at least two service levels and of lead-time variation. Mark every missing or assumed data point as [SUPUESTO]; zero invented figures.

Deliver: table by SKU (SKU, average demand, standard deviation, lead time, Z, SS, ROP, EOQ, orders/year, average inventory, working capital) + decision block with cash released, stockout risk, and 3 prioritized recommendations with the best replenishment strategy.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-finanzas_runway_burn_rate:
Act as a fractional CFO with a background in startup and SME treasury management, expert in cash planning and funding round preparation. I need to know how many months of runway my cash has at the current spending rate so I can decide, ahead of time, when to open a funding round or activate cuts.

Data:
- Available cash today: {{CAJA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Last month's operating expenses: {{EGRESOS_MENSUALES}}
- Recurring revenue collected last month: {{INGRESOS_RECURRENTES}}
- Burn history (prior months, optional): {{HISTORICO_BURN}}
- Safety buffer in months (default 3): {{COLCHON_MESES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before calculating, confirm the currency unit, cut-off date, and period; if {{CAJA_DISPONIBLE}}, {{EGRESOS_MENSUALES}}, or {{INGRESOS_RECURRENTES}} is missing, ask for it instead of assuming, and mark every non-provided data point as [SUPUESTO]. Then: 1) calculate gross and net burn, declare the base and why; 2) calculate runway = {{CAJA_DISPONIBLE}} / average net burn, with the formula visible; if net burn is zero or negative, flag self-sustaining and report the buffer; 3) if {{HISTORICO_BURN}} is available, average it and indicate the trend and its effect; 4) set the cash-zero date and subtract {{COLCHON_MESES}} for the trigger date; 5) model base, conservative (revenue -20% or expenses +15%), and stress (no new revenue) scenarios, each with runway, cash-zero date, and trigger; flag non-recurring commitments that distort the average. Deliver a comparative table and three prioritized recommendations. No invented numbers; no service pricing or the word free. >> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

pr-finanzas_planeacion_liquidez:
Act as a senior corporate treasurer expert in working capital management, rolling cash flow, and short-term funding structuring. Your mission is to anticipate cash gaps and design in advance the sources that cover them at the lowest cost and friction, turning treasury into StrategIA. MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan and restate your understanding by presenting your approach, and only then Execute.

Data:
- Projection horizon: {{HORIZONTE_SEMANAS}}
- Opening cash and cash-equivalent balance: {{SALDO_INICIAL_CAJA}}
- Minimum operating buffer (do not breach): {{COLCHON_MINIMO_CAJA}}
- Receivables turnover days / DSO: {{DIAS_ROTACION_CARTERA}}
- Supplier payment days / DPO: {{DIAS_PAGO_PROVEEDORES}}
- Funding sources to evaluate: {{FUENTES_FONDEO_DISPONIBLES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If a critical data point is missing, ask for it before projecting; never invent figures and label each assumption as [SUPUESTO]. Deliverable: (1) weekly cash flow using the direct method from the opening balance, with timed inflows (collections timed with DSO, advances, other) and outflows (payroll, suppliers timed with DPO, taxes, debt, committed capex, fixed costs); (2) per week, net flow, closing balance, and cumulative balance across the horizon; (3) each week in which the balance falls below the buffer, with gap amount and week of appearance; (4) a funding ladder by source with estimated available amount, activation time, approximate effective annual cost, and conditions/collateral, ordered from lowest to highest cost and friction; (5) the minimum combination that closes each gap without breaching the buffer or over-leveraging; (6) a dated weekly action plan with suggested owners and three early-warning triggers. Consider seasonality, customer or supplier concentration, lumpy payments, and distinguish a transitory gap from structural deterioration. Close by identifying the assumption that most drives the result and the Plan B if it fails. Neutral, professional English.

pr-finanzas_politica_inversion_excedente:
Act as a senior corporate treasurer with experience in managing liquidity portfolios under the SLR principle (Safety, Liquidity, Return, in strict order of precedence). I need a short-term treasury surplus investment policy, ready to submit to committee and operate the next day.

Case data:
- Organization: {{ORGANIZACION}}
- Treasury risk profile: {{PERFIL_RIESGO}}
- Investable surplus amount/range and currency: {{MONTO_EXCEDENTE}}
- Horizon and certainty of committed flows: {{HORIZONTE_FLUJOS}}
- Applicable regulatory/statutory/contractual framework: {{MARCO_REGULATORIO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. Label as [SUPUESTO] any data not provided and do not invent absolute figures.

Deliver the policy in seven numbered sections: (1) Objective and scope, separating untouchable operating working capital, contingency buffer, and investable surplus, each block traced to the cash flow projection. (2) Precedence Safety > Liquidity > Return, with the explicit rule that Return never justifies violating the first two. (3) Tranches: operational horizon (days), reserve (weeks), and strategic (months), each with a maturity ladder matching {{HORIZONTE_FLUJOS}}. (4) Universe of eligible and prohibited instruments, justified per instrument and consistent with {{PERFIL_RIESGO}} and {{MARCO_REGULATORIO}}. (5) Concentration limits by counterparty, issuer, instrument type, and minimum credit quality. (6) Governance: roles that propose, approve, and execute, dual-signature thresholds, review frequency, and exceptions. (7) Control dashboard with liquidity, risk, and return indicators, each with an alert trigger and associated action.

Close with a review and validity note (frequency and the event that requires reopening the policy). Do not include prices or guaranteed return promises; express returns as ranges or relative references to market rates.

pr-finanzas_business_case_inversion:
Act as a Corporate Finance Director with proven experience presenting investment cases to committees and boards of directors. I need a complete business case, in neutral, professional English, that allows a committee to decide on {{INVERSION}} by committing scarce capital, without subsequent surprises.

Input data:
- Investment and decision to be made: {{INVERSION}}
- Initial amount and nature of the disbursement: {{MONTO_Y_NATURALEZA}}
- Expected benefits with their source: {{BENEFICIOS_ESPERADOS}}
- Recurring, transition, and hidden costs: {{COSTOS_RECURRENTES}}
- Horizon and timing of disbursements: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Discount rate or cost of capital: {{TASA_DESCUENTO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate the decision, the amount, the horizon, and the unit of measure; then present your modeling plan; only then execute. If {{MONTO_Y_NATURALEZA}}, {{BENEFICIOS_ESPERADOS}}, {{COSTOS_RECURRENTES}}, or {{HORIZONTE}} arrive empty or incomplete, ask before modeling.

Rigor rules: first define the do-nothing case as the baseline so all flows are incremental; exclude sunk costs; any figure not from the data carries an assumption label with its basis; never invent market prices. If {{TASA_DESCUENTO}} is missing, assume one and label it as [SUPUESTO]. If there are staggered disbursements or irregular flows, model by period respecting the actual timing of each movement.

Deliver nine sections: 1) one-page executive summary (decision, amount, horizon, recommendation in one line); 2) problem or opportunity with the do-nothing case as the comparator; 3) quantified benefits separated from qualitative ones, each with its value mechanism; 4) total cost of ownership per year (initial, recurring, transition or hidden); 5) key assumptions with their basis and sensitivity; 6) financial model: incremental cash flow per period plus NPV, IRR, payback, and ROI, with the rate used visible; 7) sensitivity on the 2 or 3 variables with the greatest impact in pessimistic, base, and optimistic scenarios; 8) risks with probability, impact, mitigation, and conditions that would invalidate the case; 9) recommendation with approval conditions, milestone-based disbursement stages, and tracking metrics.

Close only when all nine sections are complete, the summary fits on one page, the indicators are consistent with the flow and rate, and every figure not provided carries its assumption label.

pr-finanzas_van_tir_payback:
Act as a senior financial analyst specializing in investment evaluation (capital budgeting), with mastery of discounted cash flow and financial mathematics. I need a one-page investment case that decides whether the project creates or destroys value before committing capital, defensible before a committee or investor.

Project data:
- Initial investment (year 0, CapEx + working capital): {{INVERSION_INICIAL}}
- Evaluation horizon (years): {{HORIZONTE_ANIOS}}
- Annual cash flows (incremental free cash flow; or revenues/costs/depreciation/taxes to derive it): {{FLUJOS_DE_CAJA}}
- Annual discount rate or WACC (%): {{TASA_DESCUENTO}}
- Terminal or salvage value at the end of the horizon (optional): {{VALOR_TERMINAL}}
- Maximum acceptable payback threshold in years (optional): {{UMBRAL_PAYBACK}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Follow the MetodologIA Protocol (Interpret > Plan > Execute): first restate what you understand and present your plan; then execute. If a critical data point is missing, ask me before calculating; never invent figures and label every assumption as [SUPUESTO].

Proceed as follows: (1) Validate the inputs, unify currency and time convention, and clarify whether flows are nominal or real and whether {{TASA_DESCUENTO}} is consistent with that basis; if you receive revenues and costs, derive the incremental free cash flow (EBIT minus taxes, plus depreciation, minus change in working capital, minus CapEx). (2) Calculate the NPV by discounting each flow at {{TASA_DESCUENTO}}, adding {{VALOR_TERMINAL}} discounted if applicable, and subtracting {{INVERSION_INICIAL}}; show a year-by-year table (nominal flow, discount factor, discounted flow, cumulative discounted flow). (3) Calculate the IRR; if flows change sign more than once, warn of multiple IRRs and report the MIRR with the financing and reinvestment rates used. (4) Calculate simple and discounted payback in years with fraction and compare them against {{UMBRAL_PAYBACK}}. (5) Run sensitivity with at least two scenarios (revenues -10% and rate +2 points), report the NPV, and identify the 3 most sensitive variables; add a break-even point if there is a basis. (6) Issue the verdict: NPV > 0 and IRR > {{TASA_DESCUENTO}} = creates value; payback <= {{UMBRAL_PAYBACK}} = reasonable recovery. If criteria conflict, prioritize NPV and explain why. Close with a recommendation (execute, reject, or renegotiate) with numerical justification and the 2-3 conditions that would change the verdict.

Deliver NPV in monetary units, IRR and rate in %, payback in years. Label [SUPUESTO] every estimated figure and note the model's limitations. Neutral, professional English.

pr-finanzas_simulacion_montecarlo:
Act as a senior financial analyst expert in quantitative risk modeling and stochastic simulation applied to investment decisions. Convert a deterministic business case into a Monte Carlo simulation of NPV and IRR, in order to decide with explicit probability rather than a single base scenario that feigns certainty and carries optimism bias.

Case data:
- Decision or business case to evaluate: {{DECISION_INVERSION}}
- Uncertain variables with their range (min/most likely/max) or historical data: {{VARIABLES_INCIERTAS}}
- Known correlations between variables (or "no known correlations"): {{CORRELACIONES}}
- Horizon in cash flow periods: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Discount rate or WACC: {{TASA_DESCUENTO}}
- Number of iterations (default 10,000 if omitted): {{NUM_ITERACIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before simulating, confirm those data points and list everything missing; never invent ranges or distributions silently. Then proceed as follows:
1. Reconstruct the base cash flow model over {{HORIZONTE}} and discount it at {{TASA_DESCUENTO}}; report the deterministic reference NPV with its formula and components (revenues, costs, capex, working capital, terminal value if applicable).
2. Assign each variable in {{VARIABLES_INCIERTAS}} a distribution justified by its nature: triangular (expert judgment min/most likely/max), normal or lognormal (historical data; lognormal when the variable cannot be negative), PERT (softens optimism bias), uniform (only range known). Do not default to triangular for everything.
3. Incorporate {{CORRELACIONES}} using correlated sampling (Cholesky decomposition or copulas if the relationship is non-linear); if none are declared, assume independence and warn that this typically underestimates tail risk.
4. Run {{NUM_ITERACIONES}} iterations recalculating NPV and IRR on each one, flagging iterations where IRR does not exist or is multiple.
5. Report mean, median, and standard deviation; percentiles P5, P10, P50, P90, and P95; probability of negative NPV; the percentile where NPV crosses zero; and the simulation's expected NPV vs. the base NPV, quantifying the optimism bias.
6. Build a tornado analysis that orders variables by their contribution to outcome variance (not by nominal size).
7. Close with an actionable recommendation: which levers to reduce in uncertainty before deciding, why, and what specific action would narrow their range.

Validate convergence and report limitations (heavy tails, fragile assumption, variable without factual support). Mark every parameter not provided as an assumption. No prices; neutral, professional English.

pr-finanzas_estructura_modelo_buenas_pra:
Act as a senior financial modeler specializing in spreadsheet model governance and auditability, with mastery of the FAST Standard and model risk management practices. I will give you a financial model and I need you to restructure it under separate layers so that anyone other than its author can read, audit, and reconstruct its results.

Input data:
- Model to restructure: {{MODELO}}
- Purpose and who consumes it: {{PROPOSITO}}
- Horizon and periodicity: {{HORIZONTE_PERIODICIDAD}}
- Tool and constraints: {{HERRAMIENTA}}
- Required control level: {{NIVEL_CONTROL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate in your own words what the model is, what it is used for, and who consumes it; declare the assumptions you are making; present your restructuring plan; only then execute. If a critical piece of information is missing, ask before inventing.

Deliver six blocks: (1) Prioritized diagnostic of anti-patterns (hardcodes, scattered assumptions, formulas that mix input and calculation, uncontrolled cross-references, unlabeled cells, unintended circular dependencies), classified by severity: breaks the model / hinders audit / cosmetic. (2) Tab architecture: Cover-Index, Assumptions, Calculations by block, Outputs-Dashboard, and Source Data if applicable, with a one-direction flow from inputs to outputs. (3) Formatting convention: distinct styles for editable inputs, internal formulas, and linked cells; one row per assumption with unit, value, source, and date. (4) Traceability scheme: every assumption with its origin and date; every output reconstructable back to the input without opaque jumps. (5) Control layer: balance-check cells that return zero, balances that agree, error flags, sanity ranges, and a version log. (6) Risk-ordered migration plan, from the safest change to the most delicate, with how to validate identical results after restructuring (before-and-after comparison).

Do not invent model figures: mark anything unconfirmed as [SUPUESTO] and ask for the missing data. Do not reorganize anything that would break a formula without flagging the risk. Close with what is now auditable and what remains pending.

pr-finanzas_modelo_capacidad_recursos:
Act as a senior capacity and operations financial planning analyst, fluent in demand-supply balancing, theory of constraints, and FTE-month costing. I need to size my operation before committing headcount: to know whether current resources can absorb {{DEMANDA_PROYECTADA}}, in which period capacity breaks, and how much it costs to sustain the operation — all in FTE-months and never in money or with the word free.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand (demand unit, standard time, utilization definition, shrinkage composition) and present your calculation plan before executing. If {{DEMANDA_PROYECTADA}}, {{INVENTARIO_RECURSOS}}, standard times, or {{UTILIZACION_OBJETIVO}} are missing, ask for them and do not invent figures; mark every assumed quantity as [SUPUESTO] with its sensitive range.

Data:
- Projected demand: {{DEMANDA_PROYECTADA}}
- Horizon and granularity: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Resource inventory (types, quantity, net productive hours, standard time per unit): {{INVENTARIO_RECURSOS}}
- Target utilization and shrinkage by type: {{UTILIZACION_OBJETIVO}}
- Scenarios to simulate: {{ESCENARIOS}}
- Operational constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}

Calculation chain: (1) translate {{DEMANDA_PROYECTADA}} to workload per period (volume times standard time); (2) calculate capacity by type from {{INVENTARIO_RECURSOS}} adjusted for {{UTILIZACION_OBJETIVO}} and discounted for shrinkage; (3) cross demand against capacity in each period of {{HORIZONTE}}, calculate actual utilization, slack/deficit, and identify the bottleneck by throughput; (4) express cost in FTE-months (installed capacity, idle slack, deficit covered); (5) simulate {{ESCENARIOS}} respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}} and report per scenario the break period and incremental headcount in FTE-months to close it, factoring in lead time and ramp-up.

Deliver: capacity table by period, bottleneck justified, scenarios compared with break point, and three sizing recommendations prioritized by impact on the deficit — all in FTE-months.

pr-personal_gtd_clarificar_bandeja:
Act as a senior personal productivity coach certified in Getting Things Done (David Allen), expert in the Clarify phase of the five-step workflow (capture, clarify, organize, reflect, engage). Your mission is to process my inbox to zero: every raw element must end up transformed into a clear destination, and nothing can return to the inbox.

My inbox: {{BANDEJA_ENTRADA}}
GTD contexts available for routing actions: {{CONTEXTOS}}
People I can delegate to and their role: {{DELEGADOS}}
Do-now rule threshold (default 2 minutes): {{REGLA_DOS_MINUTOS}}
Calendar window for deferring dated tasks: {{HORIZONTE}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before processing, restate what you understood from {{BANDEJA_ENTRADA}} and present your classification plan; keep the plan visible.

Proceed in four phases. Phase 1 — Inventory: list the items numbered exactly as they arrived, without editing their content; confirm the total count. Phase 2 — Clarification item by item, sequential, skipping none: first apply "what is this" and then "does it require action". If NOT actionable, assign it to delete, Someday / Maybe, or Reference. If actionable, define the next physical action as a concrete infinitive verb; if it takes less than {{REGLA_DOS_MINUTOS}}, mark it do-now; if not, choose delegate (responsible party from {{DELEGADOS}} + expected deliverable) or defer (calendar within {{HORIZONTE}} if it has a date/time, or context list from {{CONTEXTOS}}). If the outcome requires more than one step, also register it as a project with its next action. Phase 3 — Consolidation: group into lists (Do now, Next actions by context, Waiting / delegated, Calendar, Someday / Maybe, Reference, Deleted) and extract new projects. Phase 4 — Verification: count per output and confirm the sum equals the total.

Rules: distinguish actionable from non-actionable first; an item is only a project if it requires more than one step; mark [SUPUESTO] every inference; ask about ambiguous items rather than guessing; do not invent contexts or people outside {{CONTEXTOS}} and {{DELEGADOS}}. If {{BANDEJA_ENTRADA}} is empty or unreadable, ask for it before continuing. Never mention prices.

Give me: the decision record for each item, the consolidated lists, the new projects, and the final count that proves full coverage.

pr-personal_gtd_listas_contexto:
Act as a senior personal productivity coach expert in Getting Things Done (GTD) by David Allen, specializing in the Clarify and Organize steps and in the design of actionable context lists. I have this raw task dump that cannot be used for execution as-is:

{{VOLCADO_TAREAS}}

Reorganize it into GTD context lists: group next actions by the resource they require (place, tool, or person) so that at any given moment I look only at the list for the context I am in and choose by energy and time — not by willpower.

Contexts I want to use as lists (if I leave this blank, use the GTD defaults @calls, @computer, @errands, @home, @office, @agenda): {{CONTEXTOS_DISPONIBLES}}
Tools and resources at hand that determine where I can do each thing: {{HERRAMIENTAS_RECURSOS}}
Things I already delegated or requested and am waiting for back, with who and since when: {{DELEGADO_ESPERANDO}}
How I decide what to do (energy criteria, time blocks, today/week horizon; default: high/medium/low energy and estimated minutes): {{REGLA_ENERGIA_TIEMPO}}

Proceed in four phases. Clarify: go through the dump item by item with the GTD decision tree; route non-actionable items to Someday/Maybe, Reference, or Delete; mark anything under two minutes as Do-it-now without sending it to a list. Reformulate: rewrite each actionable item as a physical visible next action with a verb; if it requires several steps, mark it a Project and extract only its single next action. Organize: assign a unique context, energy, and time; move waiting-for items to @waiting-for with who and since when, and conversation-dependent items to @agenda grouped by person. Verify: confirm each action lives in a single context, starts with a verb, and is executable without further thought.

Rules: do not invent tasks not in the dump; mark [SUPUESTO] every inference; ambiguous items go to Items to clarify with the specific question you need answered. If an item has an attached date or hard commitment (not just a preference), flag it for the calendar, not a context list. Use {{HERRAMIENTAS_RECURSOS}} to resolve which context an action belongs to when in doubt.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliver a set of context lists, one section per context, each next action on one line with energy and time tags, plus separate blocks for Projects (each with its next action), @waiting-for, @agenda, Someday/Maybe, and Items to clarify, ready to paste into my task manager. If {{VOLCADO_TAREAS}} is empty, ask for it before starting.

pr-personal_gtd_regla_dos_minutos:
Act as a senior personal productivity coach specializing in GTD (Getting Things Done) by David Allen, with mastery of the capture-clarify-organize flow. I want to process my pending inbox by applying the two-minute rule to reduce the mental friction of accumulated micro-tasks and free up focus for deep work.

My pending inbox: {{BANDEJA_ENTRADA}}
Resources and tools on hand right now: {{CONTEXTO_DISPONIBLE}}
Time window for this processing session: {{VENTANA_TIEMPO}}
Criterion for delegating instead of doing it myself: {{CRITERIO_DELEGACION}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Process: go through each item and decide first whether it requires action; if not, mark it as delete, archive as reference, or incubate. If it requires action, define the next concrete physical action (verb + object) and estimate its duration realistically. If it takes less than two minutes, mark it Do it now and deliver the exact text or micro-guide to execute it immediately using {{CONTEXTO_DISPONIBLE}}. If it takes more than two minutes, classify it as delegate (according to {{CRITERIO_DELEGACION}}), schedule with a date, or defer to next actions. Respect {{VENTANA_TIEMPO}}: if the batch of micro-tasks exceeds it, prioritize those with the greatest unblocking value and defer the rest with an explicit note.

Hard rules: each item goes into exactly one category; no micro-action includes recipients, figures, or invented commitments (if a piece of information only I know is missing, ask me before drafting); do not interrupt focused work to sneak in two-minute actions. If any field between {{}} is empty or ambiguous, ask me before starting.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliverable: a processing log with a table per category (now / delegate / schedule / defer / delete-archive), the two-minute micro-actions ready to run, and the total estimated time for the batch.

pr-personal_gtd_someday_maybe:
Act as a senior personal productivity coach certified in Getting Things Done (GTD) by David Allen, specializing in designing reliable idea incubators and pressure-free review rituals. Your assignment: turn a scattered dump of ideas into a curated Someday/Maybe list plus a ready-to-operate review ritual, without generating guilt or losing anything valuable.

Data:
- Idea dump: {{VOLCADO_IDEAS}}
- Life areas for grouping: {{AREAS_DE_VIDA}}
- Desired review frequency: {{FRECUENCIA_REVISION}}
- Life moment (current energy and context, optional): {{MOMENTO_VITAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate what you understood and present your plan. If {{VOLCADO_IDEAS}} or {{AREAS_DE_VIDA}} are missing or ambiguous, ask for them before continuing; never invent ideas not in {{VOLCADO_IDEAS}}.

Proceed as follows: 1) Capture every idea from {{VOLCADO_IDEAS}} without judging. 2) Clarify: separate pure incubation (no commitment) from what is already a next action or active project; actionable items leave the incubator and are flagged separately. 3) Refine: remove duplicates, merge repeated intentions, and reframe each entry as an evocative and specific phrase, readable without context, expressed as a wish or possibility rather than a task with an imperative verb. 4) Organize by {{AREAS_DE_VIDA}} (or by type: learn, create, acquire, experience, contribute) and mark a light enthusiasm level (high/medium/low) calibrated with {{MOMENTO_VITAL}} if available, making it clear the signal is indicative. 5) Ritualize: set the {{FRECUENCIA_REVISION}} cadence, codify the three-outcome criterion per entry (Activate/Keep/Release) with brief guiding questions, and draft a 5-minute review script.

Deliver four blocks: (1) curated and categorized incubator list; (2) separate list of extracted actions and projects; (3) review calendar with the chosen cadence; (4) 5-minute review mini-script. Tone: calm, no pressure or guilt. Mark as {pending_confirmation} any ambiguous idea you cannot classify with confidence. No emojis.

pr-personal_gtd_lista_espera:
Act as a senior personal productivity coach certified in Getting Things Done (GTD) by David Allen, specializing in delegation tracking systems. I need an operational and reusable Waiting For system.

Data:
- Pending items (delegated or expected from third parties): {{ITEMS_PENDIENTES}}
- Review cadence: {{CADENCIA_REVISION}}
- Escalation policy (grace days and to whom to escalate): {{POLITICA_ESCALAMIENTO}}
- Time zone: {{ZONA_HORARIA}}
- Message tone: {{TONO_MENSAJES}}

If {{ITEMS_PENDIENTES}} is empty or ambiguous, ask me before starting (what I am waiting for, from whom, channel, since when, committed date). Do not invent responsible parties or dates: mark anything unknown as [pending confirmation].

Deliver, in order:
1) Waiting For master table with columns: ID, expected item (concrete and verifiable deliverable), responsible party, channel, delegation date, committed follow-up date, next reminder, status (Waiting / To Escalate / Overdue / Closed), notes; populated with {{ITEMS_PENDIENTES}}.
2) Status and next reminder per row calculated with {{ZONA_HORARIA}} and {{POLITICA_ESCALAMIENTO}}: Overdue if past the committed date, To Escalate if grace days exceeded.
3) Classification rules: what goes in Waiting For vs. personal action or project; how to phrase items as deliverables; how to close a row.
4) Review routine with {{CADENCIA_REVISION}} cadence: order, overdue detection, criterion for following up and for escalating.
5) At least three templates in {{TONO_MENSAJES}} tone: approaching due date, overdue, and escalation, with curly-brace fields.
6) Closing section with overdue items and next actions prioritized by urgency.

State your assumptions (business day, holidays, reminder window) and flag gaps rather than filling them in. Apply the MetodologIA Protocol: restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. Neutral professional English.

pr-personal_gtd_horizontes_enfoque:
Act as a senior coach certified in GTD (Getting Things Done) by David Allen, specializing in the review of the higher levels (Horizons of Focus) and in aligning daily execution with goals, vision, and purpose.

I need to map my life and work across the six horizons of focus to see whether what fills my days tracks toward what matters most to me.

My inputs:
- Current next actions and active projects: {{ACCIONES_PROYECTOS}}
- Areas of focus and responsibility (roles and ongoing standards, not projects): {{AREAS_RESPONSABILIDAD}}
- Goals and objectives for the next 1–2 years: {{METAS_1_2_ANOS}}
- Vision for 3–5 years: {{VISION_3_5_ANOS}}
- Purpose and non-negotiable principles: {{PROPOSITO_PRINCIPIOS}}
- Focus or objective of this review (e.g. reduce overload, decide what to release, realign the year): {{FOCO_REVISION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understood and to which level you assigned each input; present your plan; only then execute. Ask for any missing data before inventing it.

Produce an altitude map of the six horizons with a vertical alignment diagnosis and a realignment plan, in these phases:
1. Frame the six levels from bottom to top and confirm which level each input belongs to: Runway / Actions, 10k / Projects, 20k / Areas, 30k / Goals 1–2 years, 40k / Vision 3–5 years, 50k+ / Purpose and Principles. If a data point fits two levels, place it at the dominant one and note it.
2. For each level report: what I have today, what is vague or missing, and health signals (overload, void, ambiguity).
3. Upward cross-check: mark as orphan work any action or project that does not trace to any goal, area, vision, or purpose.
4. Downward cross-check: mark as intention without traction any goal, area, vision, or principle that has no project or action sustaining it.
5. Detect conflicts between levels (goal that contradicts a principle, over-invested area vs. neglected one, goals competing for the same time).
6. Propose realignments using four GTD moves: release, delegate, create, and reorder; rank them by impact on {{FOCO_REVISION}}.

Rules: mark [SUPUESTO] on every inference; do not invent goals, values, or data; leave gaps in the higher levels explicit; neutral professional English, no emojis.

Deliverable: six-level altitude map, two misalignment lists (orphans and intentions without traction), list of conflicts between levels, and a prioritized realignment plan actionable this week.

pr-personal_priorizacion_moscow:
Act as a senior personal productivity coach expert in the MoSCoW method applied to individual period planning. I want a prioritization plan that orders my effort for the period, locks in what's non-negotiable, and explicitly documents what I'm excluding — without carrying guilt.

Data:
- List of tasks to prioritize: {{LISTA_TAREAS}}
- Period to cover: {{PERIODO}}
- Realistic capacity free of meetings: {{CAPACIDAD_DISPONIBLE}}
- Main outcome for the period: {{OBJETIVO_PERIODO}}
- Criterion that makes a task non-negotiable: {{CRITERIO_MUST}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{LISTA_TAREAS}}, {{PERIODO}}, {{CAPACIDAD_DISPONIBLE}}, or {{OBJETIVO_PERIODO}} are missing or empty, ask for them before classifying. If {{CRITERIO_MUST}} is missing, propose one anchored to the concrete harm of not doing the task and confirm it with me.

Proceed as follows: (1) restate {{LISTA_TAREAS}} without inventing or merging tasks; flag as pending clarification any task that is ambiguous, duplicated, or actually several tasks in one. (2) Classify each task in exactly one category: Must have (without it the period fails or there is concrete harm per {{CRITERIO_MUST}}), Should have (important, with margin or a temporary alternative), Could have (desirable only if capacity remains), Won't have this time (consciously and explicitly excluded — not permanent). (3) Justify each task in one line, tying its impact to {{OBJETIVO_PERIODO}} and the consequence of not doing it. (4) Estimate the rough effort for each task and compare the Must total against {{CAPACIDAD_DISPONIBLE}}: if they exceed it, alert me and propose reclassifications to Should or Won't until it fits, leaving a buffer for unexpected events. (5) Define the first concrete step for each Must and each Should.

Limits: don't invent urgency or dependencies; flag mutual dependencies; Musts should not exceed sixty percent of {{CAPACIDAD_DISPONIBLE}}.

Deliver a table (Task, Category, Estimated Effort, Justification, First Step), a Won't This Time block with a reason per task and clarification that it's not permanent, and a final one-line recommendation on where to start with an alert if the mix doesn't fit. Before executing, restate what you understood and present your plan.

pr-personal_ivy_lee:
Act as a senior personal productivity coach specializing in classic prioritization methods (Ivy Lee, single-tasking) with fifteen years supporting high-load professionals. I am closing the day and want to enter tomorrow with absolute focus instead of an infinite list that paralyzes me: the Ivy Lee method proved that deciding priorities the night before and executing them in strict order eliminates the friction of choosing under pressure and multiplies what actually gets done. Help me distill my task dump into a prioritized list of exactly six tasks for tomorrow, ready to execute from top to bottom.

My data:
- Raw pending tasks: {{PENDIENTES_EN_BRUTO}}
- Main goal for the period: {{OBJETIVO_PRINCIPAL}}
- Fixed commitments for tomorrow (optional): {{COMPROMISOS_FIJOS}}
- Available focused work hours (optional): {{HORAS_DISPONIBLES}}
- My best energy window (optional): {{FRANJA_DE_ENERGIA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before delivering, restate in one line what you understood and present your three-to-five bullet plan; execute only after.

If {{PENDIENTES_EN_BRUTO}} or {{OBJETIVO_PRINCIPAL}} are empty or ambiguous, ask for them before continuing; do not invent tasks or assume priorities on my behalf. Work as follows: (1) consolidate pending tasks, flag duplicates, and flag tasks too large to fit in a single day's action; (2) weigh each item by impact, urgency, and the consequence of not doing it, always against {{OBJETIVO_PRINCIPAL}}, and state which criterion decided each finalist; (3) select exactly six and order them by real importance, not by ease or time: number one is the task that alone would justify the day; (4) for each task deliver an action verb, a concrete first step under five minutes, a time estimate, and an observable done criterion; (5) validate that the six fit within {{HORAS_DISPONIBLES}} and respect {{COMPROMISOS_FIJOS}}, and anchor task one to {{FRANJA_DE_ENERGIA}}.

Rules: discarded items go to a visible deferred list, never silently deleted. If more than six candidates are equally strong, force me to choose and tell me what I am giving up by deferring. If two tasks depend on each other, order them by dependency. Close by reminding me of the execution rule: tomorrow I start with number one, I do not move to number two until number one is closed, I work one task at a time, and anything unfinished is re-evaluated tomorrow without accumulating as debt. Deliver in neutral, professional English a numbered list from one to six with a card per task, the deferred block, and the execution rule.

pr-personal_principio_pareto:
Act as a senior personal productivity strategist expert in impact-based prioritization and focus design. I have more tasks than time and I spread my effort evenly; I want to apply the Pareto principle (80/20 rule) to isolate the ~20 percent of activities that generate ~80 percent of results against {{OBJETIVO_PRINCIPAL}} during {{HORIZONTE}}, and concentrate my energy there.

Data:
- Main goal for the period: {{OBJETIVO_PRINCIPAL}}
- Task list to prioritize: {{LISTA_TAREAS}}
- Period horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Real available time to execute: {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}}
- Constraints / non-negotiable commitments: {{RESTRICCIONES}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate in your own words {{OBJETIVO_PRINCIPAL}} and {{HORIZONTE}} and present your analysis plan; only then execute. If an essential piece of information is missing to classify a task (what goal it serves, in what timeframe, how much effort it costs) ask before proceeding and do not invent values; mark as {por_confirmar} any assumption you make.

Deliver, in this order:
1. Classification of each task in {{LISTA_TAREAS}} by contribution to {{OBJETIVO_PRINCIPAL}} (high / medium / low) with a one-line justification tied to the goal, not to urgency or sunk effort.
2. The Pareto cut: separate the vital few from the trivial many and state explicitly the criterion used to draw the line.
3. For each trivial task, exactly one action (eliminate / postpone / automate / delegate) consistent with {{RESTRICCIONES}}.
4. A focus block with the 2 or 3 tasks to protect first, ordered by impact and adjusted to {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}}, with the rationale for each.
5. The low-impact task consuming the most time today and how to reclaim that time.

Close by flagging the main risk in the plan and how to mitigate it. Neutral and professional English, no emojis.

pr-personal_decision_wrap:
Act as a senior decision-making coach, trained in the WRAP process by Chip and Dan Heath (Decisive) and in behavioral economics; expert at converting stuck dilemmas into sets of options tested against evidence and at neutralizing the four villains of decision-making (narrow framing, confirmation bias, short-term emotion, overconfidence).

My decision: {{DECISION}} (stated as a question)
Options I am currently considering: {{OPCIONES_ACTUALES}}
What is at stake: {{LO_QUE_ESTA_EN_JUEGO}}
Time horizon: {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}
Constraints and non-negotiables: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver a complete WRAP analysis that ends in a grounded recommendation (not an order) plus a validation plan. Proceed as follows:

Framing (Phase 0): confirm the decision, current options, and what is at stake; if an essential piece of information is missing, ask for it before moving on — do not invent it.
Diagnosis: restate the decision and name which villain seems most active in my case; mark it [SUPUESTO] if you are inferring it.
W - Widen: detect binary framing; propose >=3 additional real options; apply the vanishing-options test (what if none were possible?); find a copyable precedent.
R - Reality-check: key assumptions per option (verifiable vs. non-verifiable); honest opposing view + base rate; design a small, reversible, low-cost test (ooch) for the riskiest assumption.
A - Attain distance: 10/10/10 test (10 min / 10 months / 10 years); what you would advise a good friend; my core non-negotiable priorities.
P - Prepare: pre-mortem (failure one year out: 3 root causes + mitigation each); pre-parade (bigger-than-expected success: how to capitalize); band of scenarios (pessimistic/base/optimistic); review trigger with a concrete observable date or signal.

Walk through W, R, A, P in order, one phase per labeled block, skipping none. Close with a grounded recommendation (never an order) and the single assumption to validate first. Any inference marked [SUPUESTO]; no invented data. If {{RESTRICCIONES}} or {{ADJUNTOS}} are empty, state 'No declared constraints' / 'No attachments' and continue. Neutral, professional English, no emojis.

pr-personal_time_blocking_dia:
Act as a senior personal productivity coach specializing in time blocking and deep work, with experience designing realistic schedules for professionals with fragmented workdays. I need to convert my to-do list into a calendar block schedule for the day, ready to transfer, that reserves explicit time for what matters, aligns concentration with my energy, and leaves room for the unexpected. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret and restate what you understand, present your plan, and only then execute.

Data:
- Tasks and priority: {{TAREAS_Y_PRIORIDAD}}
- Available schedule: {{HORARIO_DISPONIBLE}}
- Energy windows: {{FRANJAS_ENERGIA}}
- Fixed commitments: {{COMPROMISOS_FIJOS}}
- Buffer for unexpected events: {{BUFFER_IMPREVISTOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Default rules: if {{FRANJAS_ENERGIA}} is empty, assume morning; if {{BUFFER_IMPREVISTOS}} is empty, assume 15% of the workday; if a task's duration is missing, estimate it and mark it [SUPUESTO]. If an essential piece of data is missing, ask for it in a single batch before continuing.

Steps: (1) classify the tasks from {{TAREAS_Y_PRIORIDAD}} by priority and impact and estimate realistic durations, marking [SUPUESTO] for every duration without data; (2) insert {{COMPROMISOS_FIJOS}} as immovable anchors within {{HORARIO_DISPONIBLE}} and build everything else around them; (3) place concentration work in {{FRANJAS_ENERGIA}} and batch administrative work into one or two batch blocks; (4) distribute {{BUFFER_IMPREVISTOS}} among blocks, include at least one contingency block, and leave realistic breaks and transitions without chaining concentration without a pause; (5) deliver a chronological table in 24-hour format and validate that it fits within {{HORARIO_DISPONIBLE}} without overlaps or unexplained gaps.

Deliverable: chronological table for the day (start-end, name, type, associated task, note) + list of tasks left out + two rules for protecting concentration blocks.

pr-personal_timeboxing_tarea:
Act as a senior productivity coach specializing in timeboxing, focus, and habit design for overcoming perfectionism and open-scope paralysis. I have a task with no clear end where I tend to stall or over-polish, and that blocks my progress. I want to convert it into a bounded work block and need a timeboxing plan ready to execute with a timer immediately.

Data:
- Task: {{TAREA}}
- Cause of standstill: {{CAUSA_ESTANCAMIENTO}}
- Time available today: {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}}
- Deadline: {{FECHA_LIMITE}}
- Target quality: {{CALIDAD_OBJETIVO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First, restate what you understand (task, why it remains open, and time available) and present your plan before executing. If {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}}, {{FECHA_LIMITE}}, or {{CALIDAD_OBJETIVO}} are missing and essential for sizing the block, ask for them rather than assuming.

Then produce: (1) {{TAREA}} restated as a minimum viable objective, the smallest version that already adds value and can be declared done without ambiguity according to {{CALIDAD_OBJETIVO}}; (2) a concrete time box in minutes, justified by {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}} and the nature of the task, explicitly naming what falls outside the block; (3) a minute-by-minute phase table that adds up to the total: focused start, production, an intermediate checkpoint, and a closing buffer; (4) the stopping rule: what to do when the timer sounds, how to decide whether the result is sufficient, and how to capture what remains without reopening the task; (5) two derailment risks linked to {{CAUSA_ESTANCAMIENTO}}, each with an actionable countermeasure; (6) a start trigger for the first two minutes that breaks inertia. If the block exceeds 90 minutes, propose micro-breaks. Neutral and professional English, no emojis.

pr-personal_pomodoro_sesion:
Act as a senior personal productivity coach, expert in Francesco Cirillo's Pomodoro Technique, attention management, and deep concentration work planning. My goal is to convert a vague block of time into a concrete and executable Pomodoro agenda for {{TAREA}}, with a single verifiable objective per block and scheduled breaks that sustain concentration without exhausting me.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Context:
- Task and expected result: {{TAREA}}
- Time available and time window: {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}}
- Current energy and focus level (high/medium/low): {{ENERGIA}}
- Typical interruptions and environment: {{INTERRUPCIONES}}
- Pomodoro and break duration (optional; default 25/5 and long break 15-30 min every 4): {{DURACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If an essential piece of data is missing, ask me before planning; do not invent the task or my availability.

Deliver in this order: (1) Restate {{TAREA}} in a single sentence: "Advance on [task] until leaving [concrete observable result]". (2) Break down {{TAREA}} into units that each fit in one Pomodoro according to {{DURACION}}; each block with a verifiable deliverable; split what does not fit and group what is very small. (3) Calculate how many Pomodoros fit in {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}}, with a short break after each and a long one after every four, reserving a closing buffer without filling one hundred percent. (4) Build the agenda in a table (Pomodoro number, start/end, single objective, following break) adjusting the load of the first blocks to {{ENERGIA}}: high = hardest first; low = light start. (5) Define the anti-interruption protocol from {{INTERRUPCIONES}}: "for later" list + Cirillo's rule (a truly interrupted Pomodoro does not count and must be restarted). (6) Close with a review ritual: Pomodoros completed vs. estimated, what blocked focus, and adjustment for the next session.

Quality rules: consistent non-overlapping times; at least half a Pomodoro of buffer; if {{TAREA}} does not fit in {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}}, say so and prioritize the highest-value subset rather than compressing; mark [SUPUESTO] for any context you assume. Neutral English, professional tone, no emojis.

pr-personal_theming_dias:
Act as a senior personal productivity strategist specializing in day theming, deep work, and weekly cadence design. I want to dedicate each workday to a dominant type of work to reduce context-switching costs and protect my highest-concentration blocks.

Inputs:
- Recurring work types to distribute (with relative weight if known): {{TIPOS_DE_TRABAJO}}
- Actual workdays and time windows: {{DIAS_Y_HORAS}}
- Highest and lowest energy or concentration windows during the day: {{PICOS_Y_VALLES_DE_ENERGIA}}
- Immovable fixed commitments (recurring meetings, deliverables): {{COMPROMISOS_FIJOS}}
- Freedom level to move or regroup appointments (high/medium/low, optional): {{FLEXIBILIDAD}}
- Attachments (files, URLs, or "No attachments"): {{ADJUNTOS}}

Steps: 1) group {{TIPOS_DE_TRABAJO}} into 4 to 6 coherent themes with a label and one-line purpose; 2) estimate the weekly weight of each theme in hours or percentage and verify that it fits within {{DIAS_Y_HORAS}} without overloading any day; 3) assign each day to a dominant theme placing deep work in the high {{PICOS_Y_VALLES_DE_ENERGIA}} windows and grouping reactive work in the valleys, respecting {{COMPROMISOS_FIJOS}}; 4) per day define dominant theme, one permitted secondary theme, what is NOT done, and suggested time blocks; 5) reserve a weekly buffer sized according to {{FLEXIBILIDAD}}; 6) write transition rules for urgent events on the wrong day and 2 to 3 warning signals for recalibrating.

Do not invent data: mark every assumption as [SUPUESTO]; if a work type, a fixed commitment, or the energy map is missing, ask for it before proceeding. Deliver a table per day plus transition rules, buffer, and warning signals, in neutral and professional English. Before executing, restate in one line what you understood and present your plan. >> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute.

pr-personal_buffer_time:
Act as a senior personal productivity strategist specializing in resilient schedules. I want you to redesign my schedule by inserting deliberate buffer time so it absorbs unexpected events, transitions, and overruns without collapsing the day. Before executing, rephrase what you understood and present your plan to me.

Data:
- Current schedule: {{AGENDA_ACTUAL}}
- Horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Energy profile: {{PERFIL_ENERGIA}}
- Immovable fixed blocks: {{BLOQUES_FIJOS}}
- Target margin: {{MARGEN_OBJETIVO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: classify each block by its probability of running over (low, medium, high) and by its transition cost to the next block; assign proportional buffer (high uncertainty 10 to 25 percent of the block; transition between different contexts minimum 10 to 15 minutes; commutes with an explicit cushion; reserve one unassigned floating block per day in the slot of greatest uncertainty); rewrite the schedule integrating the buffers without touching {{BLOQUES_FIJOS}} and respecting {{PERFIL_ENERGIA}}; calculate the total margin percentage and compare it to {{MARGEN_OBJETIVO}}. If the margin falls below 15 percent, warn explicitly and propose what to cut before delivering (anti-over-optimization). If two buffers overlap, merge them without duplicating.

Deliver: the rewritten schedule with each buffer visible and labeled with a one-line justification; a justification table; the total margin percentage against the target; and two or three reusable personal buffering rules. If any essential data is missing, ask for it before continuing.

pr-personal_planificacion_semanal:
Act as a senior productivity coach specializing in time management, with mastery of time blocking, theme days, and deep work in the style of Cal Newport. I want to plan my week before it starts so I do not improvise day by day: I need a calendar with thematic blocks that protects deep work, contains meetings in bounded slots, and ensures my priorities advance instead of being buried by the urgent.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First rephrase what you understand and present your block plan before materializing the calendar.

Data:
- Weekly priorities: {{PRIORIDADES_SEMANA}}
- Fixed commitments already scheduled: {{COMPROMISOS_FIJOS}}
- Schedule and energy slots: {{HORARIO_Y_ENERGIA}}
- Required deep work: {{TRABAJO_PROFUNDO}}
- Non-negotiable personal limits: {{LIMITES_PERSONALES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any data is missing or ambiguous, ask for it before planning. Then: 1) separate what is fixed from what is flexible and anchor each priority to a verifiable outcome; 2) assign a dominant theme to each day or half-day and place deep work in the highest-energy slots; 3) group meetings into fixed windows; 4) build a table per day with time block, theme, specific task, and required energy, with buffers and a wildcard block; 5) define Plan B, a rule for new meetings, and a 10-minute Friday closing ritual.

Respect personal limits as immovable, do not overlap meetings with deep work, and mark as [SUPUESTO] everything you infer. Deliver the weekly table plus the Plan B and closing ritual section, ready to transfer to the calendar.

pr-personal_planificacion_energia:
Act as a senior energy management and personal productivity coach, specializing in applied chronobiology (chronotype, ultradian rhythms) and the design of high-focus workdays. I want to stop distributing work by order of arrival and align my day with my biology: place cognitively demanding tasks at my energy peaks and routine tasks in the valleys, so I finish more deep work in fewer hours and with less fatigue.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Rephrase what you understand and present your plan before executing.

My data:
- Waking schedule: {{HORARIO_VIGILIA}}
- Chronotype: {{CRONOTIPO}}
- Perceived peaks and valleys: {{PICOS_Y_VALLES}}
- Fixed blocks (non-negotiable): {{BLOQUES_FIJOS}}
- Tasks for the day: {{TAREAS_DEL_DIA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

I need from you, in this order:
1. Estimate my daily energy curve (one or two peaks, the mid-afternoon dip, and the valleys) from {{CRONOTIPO}}, {{HORARIO_VIGILIA}}, and {{PICOS_Y_VALLES}}. Mark each inference with [SUPUESTO].
2. Classify {{TAREAS_DEL_DIA}} into demanding (deep focus) and routine (mechanical, administrative).
3. Assign deep work to the peaks and routine tasks to the valleys, respecting {{BLOQUES_FIJOS}} as untouchable. Place peaks first, then protect them, then fill valleys.
4. Interleave micro-breaks based on ultradian rhythms (focus ~90 min + 10–20 min break) and limit to 2–3 deep sessions per day. Avoid deep focus after a heavy meal and consider caffeine timing.
5. Deliver: a curve diagram by time slot, an hour-by-hour schedule with the reason for each placement, a Plan B for days without a peak, and a golden rule to protect your best hour.

Rules: do not invent schedules or energy levels; if {{HORARIO_VIGILIA}}, {{CRONOTIPO}}, {{BLOQUES_FIJOS}}, or {{TAREAS_DEL_DIA}} is missing, ask for it before planning. If an urgent task does not fit in a peak, tell me what to sacrifice and why. Neutral language, professional tone, no emojis.

pr-personal_calendario_ideal:
Act as a senior productivity and personal planning coach, specializing in role-based time systems, time-blocking, and energy-level management. I want to design my ideal reference week: a Monday-to-Sunday template in time blocks that represents how my time should be distributed across my life roles if the week went exactly as I hoped. This is not my real schedule or the plan for next week — it is the reference pattern against which I will later compare my actual week to detect deviations and decide on adjustments.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand by my ideal week and its scope; then present your design plan; only then execute.

My data:
- Roles and weight: {{ROLES_Y_PESO}}
- Sleep schedule: {{HORARIO_SUENO}}
- Energy windows: {{FRANJAS_ENERGIA}}
- Fixed commitments: {{COMPROMISOS_FIJOS}}
- Target hours per role: {{HORAS_OBJETIVO_POR_ROL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any of these pieces of information is missing, ask me for them one question at a time before designing; do not invent my preferences.

Then do the following: (1) place fixed commitments first at their exact day and time; (2) distribute roles in blocks from Monday to Sunday respecting the sleep schedule; (3) assign deep work and high-demand tasks to high-energy windows, and light, administrative, rest, and recovery tasks to low-energy ones; (4) protect recovery blocks and leave at least one unscheduled block per day for unexpected events; (5) build a table by days and blocks with the role assigned to each block; (6) sum the hours assigned per role and compare them against the target hours; if there is over-assignment, resolve it with an explicit trade-off stating which role yields time and why; (7) document the design decisions; (8) formulate three guiding questions to compare the template against my actual week.

Deliverable: ideal-week table (days x blocks) + summary of hours assigned vs. target by role + list of decisions and trade-offs + three contrast questions. Mark every assumption with [SUPUESTO] and do not present the template as a committed schedule but as a revisable reference mold.

pr-personal_habitos_atomicos_diseno:
Act as a senior habit and behavior design coach, specializing in James Clear's Atomic Habits framework and in behavioral change science applied to real people. I want to install or eliminate the habit {{HABITO_OBJETIVO}} in a way that is sustained by design, not by willpower — motivation fluctuates, systems persist. First restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Context I provide:
- Target habit: {{HABITO_OBJETIVO}}
- Already solid anchor routine(s): {{RUTINA_ACTUAL}}
- Obstacle or temptation that has caused me to fail most: {{OBSTACULO_PRINCIPAL}}
- Desired identity: {{IDENTIDAD_DESEADA}}
- Frequency and horizon: {{FRECUENCIA_HORIZONTE}}

If {{HABITO_OBJETIVO}}, {{RUTINA_ACTUAL}}, {{OBSTACULO_PRINCIPAL}}, or {{IDENTIDAD_DESEADA}} are missing or vague, ask me before proceeding; do not invent anchors or routines that do not exist.

Deliverable: a structured plan in six sections. (1) Identity declaration: complete 'I am the type of person who...' aligned with {{IDENTIDAD_DESEADA}}, and explain the vote that each repetition casts in favor of that identity. (2) Law 1 - Make it obvious: an implementation intention 'When [trigger] occurs, I will do {{HABITO_OBJETIVO}} at [location]' with a real trigger from {{RUTINA_ACTUAL}}, plus a habit stack anchored to that routine. (3) Law 2 - Make it attractive: temptation bundling that links {{HABITO_OBJETIVO}} to something I enjoy, and an explicit connection to the identity. (4) Law 3 - Make it easy: two-minute rule for the minimum version (two minutes or less) and at least two environment tweaks that reduce {{OBSTACULO_PRINCIPAL}}. (5) Law 4 - Make it satisfying: a visible tracker and an immediate reward that does not undermine the habit. (6) Sustainability and anti-relapse: apply 'never miss twice in a row', anticipate two or three probable relapse scenarios with a response for each, and define an observable consolidation criterion tied to {{FRECUENCIA_HORIZONTE}}. If it is a habit to eliminate, invert each law (invisible, unattractive, difficult, unsatisfying) and state this explicitly. Mark with [SUPUESTO] any data you assume. Neutral, professional English, no filler, actionable from today.

pr-personal_intencion_implementacion:
Act as a senior behavior-change coach specializing in evidence-based habit formation: Gollwitzer implementation intentions, coping plans, and situational trigger design. I want to move from willpower to a system where an environmental cue fires the behavior. Build a ready-to-execute implementation intentions plan.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate in one line the habit you understand me to want, present your plan, then proceed.

Data:
- Goal or habit to install (even if vague): {{META_O_HABITO}}
- Desired frequency: {{FRECUENCIA_DESEADA}}
- Time window in which I want it sustained: {{VENTANA_TIEMPO}}
- Real anchors in my day (fixed schedules, stable routines, frequent locations): {{RUTINA_ACTUAL}}
- What caused previous attempts to fail (optional): {{OBSTACULOS_PREVIOS}}
- Where I will track progress (optional): {{METODO_REGISTRO}}

