- Protocol for ambiguity, missing data, tool failure, or out-of-scope request: {{PROTOCOLO_ERROR}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}
If an essential datum is missing or {{HERRAMIENTAS}} is ambiguous, ask me before drafting and do not invent capabilities. Deliver in this order: (1) the mission in a single sentence: 'I am [role] and I exist to [measurable outcome] serving {{USUARIO}}, without [red line]'; (2) the full system prompt in second person addressed to the agent, with six mandatory sections (MISSION, SCOPE with explicit in-scope and out-of-scope, TOOLS with trigger and non-usage condition for each item in {{HERRAMIENTAS}} plus what to do if there is no tool, TONE AND FORMAT derived from {{TONO}}, LIMITS AND SAFETY from {{LIMITES}}, ERROR PROTOCOL from {{PROTOCOLO_ERROR}}); (3) a one-line golden rule for conflicts between instructions, at the close of the system prompt; (4) separately, three test messages (normal, ambiguous, out-of-scope) with the expected response. Instructions must be imperative and verifiable; no vague adjectives. Neutral professional English, ready to paste.

pr-ia-automatizacion_rag-arquitectura-ba:
Act as a senior RAG systems architect with proven experience in semantic search, vector databases, and LLMs in production. I need an implementable RAG architecture, not a conceptual summary: a weak decision in chunking, embeddings, or reranking is paid for later in imprecise responses, hallucinations, or unacceptable latency.

Context:
- Use case and domain: {{CASO_DE_USO}}
- Document volume and type: {{VOLUMEN_Y_TIPO_DOCUMENTOS}}
- Language (include if multilingual): {{IDIOMA}}
- Infrastructure, privacy, and deployment constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES_INFRA}}
- Target end-to-end latency (optional): {{LATENCIA_OBJETIVO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, rephrase the use case, corpus, language, and constraints; if {{CASO_DE_USO}}, {{VOLUMEN_Y_TIPO_DOCUMENTOS}}, {{IDIOMA}}, or {{RESTRICCIONES_INFRA}} are empty or ambiguous, ask me before designing — do not invent the domain. Then present the plan for the seven stages and the decision criteria. Only then develop the design.

Cover these seven stages in order, justify each decision with an explicit criterion (expected precision, recall, latency, relative operational cost, or maintainability), never with preference, and name the discarded alternative and why:
1. Ingestion: sources, formats, frequency, deltas or incremental re-indexing, deduplication, metadata contract (source, date, author, permissions, version, language).
2. Chunking: strategy with concrete size and overlap; granularity vs. context trade-off; handling of tables, code, and headings.
3. Embeddings: model and dimensionality justified by language and domain; index versioning and re-indexing on model change.
4. Indexing and storage: vector database, index type (HNSW, IVF, or other) with parameters, similarity metric, filtering by metadata and permissions.
5. Retrieval: top-k, dense + lexical hybrid search (BM25) with its fusion method (e.g. RRF) if applicable, query rewriting or expansion.
6. Reranking: cross-encoder reranker if appropriate, model, top-n input, and context trimming to the LLM window.
7. Generation with citations: prompt that enforces citing the exact source per claim, citation format, and explicit abstention rule without sufficient evidence.

Close with: text flow diagram of the seven stages, metrics (recall@k, precision@k, MRR/nDCG, faithfulness, answer relevance, p95 latency), recommended evaluation golden set, minimum acceptance tests, and a risk matrix with mitigation (hallucination, permission leaks, stale data, corpus drift).

Mark every unconfirmed estimate as [SUPUESTO]. No prices or monetary figures; reason about cost in relative terms. Neutral professional English, no emojis.

pr-ia-automatizacion_rag-estrategia-chun:
Act as a senior RAG engineer specializing in retrieval pipelines over heterogeneous corpora, with expertise in balancing retrieval precision, context coherence, and indexing cost. I am going to define the chunking strategy for a corpus before indexing it. Chunking is the highest-leverage decision in the pipeline: poorly calibrated segmentation fragments context, dilutes embeddings, and degrades retrieval more than any subsequent reranker. The goal is a differentiated strategy by document type and query pattern, ready to evaluate before indexing everything.

Data:
- Corpus document types: {{TIPOS_DOCUMENTO}}
- Expected query pattern: {{PATRON_CONSULTA}}
- Embeddings model and maximum window in tokens: {{MODELO_EMBEDDINGS}}
